      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>WOMAN'S W • LD.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S W • LD.</p>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>A University of Baby Craft. An admirable outlet for the activities of young society girls, whose usual mode of life has been so changed by the war, has been found by some of the leaders of society, This is a university for the study of baby crofts ftrid the atudenta àê trained to combat the scourge of infant mor- tality. When the training is completed, the stu- dents are expected to visit the homes of the poor, and to teach young and ignorant mothers how to care for their babies, so that they may grow up strong and healthy and maze good citi- zens. If tact and discretion be employed by the students, the work should prove a national benefit, for the future of this country will de- pend in a great measure on the physical fitness of the children now in their babyhood.</p>
        <p>Science and Disease. Science is respoll .jie for the carnage of mod- ern warfare, also . the comparatively low rate of mortality among the wounded. Science de- -41 feats the ravages of diseases, which in other ages were more destructive of human life than en- gines of war. It is curious to note the return not only to obsolete weapons, such as the hand grenade, but to old-fashioned methods of medical practice. Thus every French soldier is now supplied with a sealed bottle of iodine, and with its contents he paints his wounds before infec- tion sets in. This was the method adopted beiore • Lord Lister introduced anti-septics. Medical science is successfully waging war with tetanus -and gangrene, the two most prevalent and deadly diseases of this war. It frequently happens that after a battle a doctor runs short of anti-tetanus serum, then he has rapidly to choose to which of, perhaps, a hundred patients his fifty doses shall be distributed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>HOME NOTES. Profitable Recipes and Useful</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES. Profitable Recipes and Useful</p>
        <p>Suggestions.</p>
        <p>The right hand, which is moA sensitive to the touch than the left, is less sensitive than the latter to the effect of heat and cold. A nourishing, healthy diet may. be provided from Vegetarian foods at less cost than when flesh is used. Beans, peas, lentils and cheese contain, weight for weight, more nutriment than do beef, mutton or poultry.</p>
        <p>Silver. To prevent silver from turning dark when not in use, after cleaning, wrap each piece separ- ately in tissue paper and keep in a tight chest in some dry room free from all kinds of gases. Do not allow matches anywhere near the silver, as the sulphur darkens it.</p>
        <p>Frosted Walnuts. Make an icing of four tablespoonfuls of sugar and, the white of eggs, beaten to a stiff froth. Remoye the shells from the walnuts, separate them in halves and dip in the icing, after which place on a clean sheet of paper, stand in the oven and bake a light brown colour. The paper will not burn unless the ovi.n--is too-hot for the walnuts.</p>
        <p>Fried Bacon and Apples. Place the bacon on the rack in a dripping pan. The pan should have enough water in it to cover the bottom. Place in a hot oven, cook until the bacon is brown and crisp. Slice cored, un- pared apples. Heat the bacon drippings in a pan, lay in the apples, sprinkle with sugar, and cook in the oven until brown. Serve the bacon on a platter surrounded with apples.</p>
        <p>Old-Fashioned Recipes for Colds. • The following is a good recipe, a tablespoonful to be taken every four hours: One pound each of figs and loaf sugar. One tablespoonful each of flaxseed and boneset (thoroughwort). Boil the figs, flaxseed, and boneset in one quart of water until the figs are white. After straining add the sugar, boiling to a thick syrup. When a cold is felt in the throat or chest, melt together one tablespoonful each of turpentine, camphor gum, and olive oil. After rubbing the chest with this, a hot flannel cloth is placed over it. Rub eucalyptus oil on the throat, chest, and back just before retiring. Cover the same parts with hot flannels. Keep well protected and warm.</p>
        <p>Apple Charlotte. Take three cups of nicely stewed tart apples which have been beaten smooth, or rubbed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>orevew,o=d-Kv-,oo.9owhxyzeag CHILDREN'S CORNER. Lonvoy,Aducted by UNCLE NEwDjc</head>
        <p>orevew,o=d-Kv-,oo.9owhxyzeag CHILDREN'S CORNER. Lonvoy,Aducted by UNCLE NEwDjc</p>
        <p>A HEARTY INVITATION to join this Corner is extended to all children who read it regu- larly. Application for membership, stating name, address and age, should be made to: "UNCLE NED, Examiner Office, Bewsey- street, Warrington." Every girl or boy, whose name is in the Big Book, is entitled to enter the competitions held from time to time, to the winners of which useful prizes are awarded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR CHILDREN,— Heigh° ! the weary Winter is passing, and soon we shall be husking in the warm, cheerful rays of the Summer sun. I suppose that during the coming weeks we shall all be watching the flowers, as they break forth in variegated colours all over the garden; or the trees, as the beautitul green leaves gradually spread over their bare boughs.—At least, so we should ware we all lucky enough to live out in the country, and it is with a pang of regret we in most cases remember that such is not the</p>
        <p>case. No! it is, alas, not for us who dwell in the toWns to watch the wonderful way in which Nature nourishes her children, and to learn her lessons thereby. Yet we all have many oppor- tunities of helping to make more beautiful the town in which We live, and with this object all irtly nephews and nieces who have not yet done so should join the Beautiful Warrington Society, as I have done. The members try: Not to destroy, any trees or flowers, Not to throw paper about their schools or the streets, Not to write on walls or hoardings, Not to damage other people's property, Not to throw orange-peel or fruit skins about the streets. They also do their best to prevent anybody else doing these things; and, lastly, endeavour to grow plants and flowers, and make their homes and Schools beautiful., Now I am sure that you would all like to become me,mbers of this splendid society, and your teacher will tell you how to do so if you ask. Then you can go to a plant or bulb shop, and .start to grow ybur own plant, at home—thus getting a good idea of.. plant-life. And you can spend your Saturday afternoons by going rambres in the country with your playmates. ff you have not already tried these, you little know the pleasure which they afford. I always</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Fashions for 1915.</head>
        <p>Fashions for 1915.</p>
        <p>through a colander and sweetened to taste. The apples should have been cooked with only enough water to make them the consistency of thick marmalade. Remove the hard crusts from slices of bread, spread them quite thickly with prepared apple, and pack in layers in a pudding dish. Cover with a simple custard made of a quart of milk, three tablespoonfuls of sugar and two eggs. Let it stand half an hour, then bake. Other fruit marmalade may be used in place of the apple preparation if preferred.</p>
        <p>. . Everton Toffee. Pat 11b. of powdered sugar and one teacupful of cold water into a saucepan. When the sugar is dissolved, add ;tn. of butter beaten to a cream. Keep stirring the mixture over the fire until it hardens, when a little is dropped upon a buttered dish. Just before the toffee is done add a half-teaspoonful of lemon flavouring, and pour into a shallow buttered pan to cool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>Of the 1,001 young women who fainted last year, 998 fell into the arms of men, two fell on the floor, and one into a water butt. Generally a woman's reason for doing a thing is she hasn't any, and it turns out much better than with a man who has.</p>
        <p>His Revenge. .She (as the clock neared midnight): Are you fond of music? He (a rejected suitor) : Very much so. It com- pletely carries me away! She (rushes over to the piano and plays several popular airs): But I see you haven't gone. He (with a yawn) : No. She: Yet you said music always carried you away? He: To be sure—music!</p>
        <p>A Pathetic Chicken-liver Episode. She entered a high-priced butcher's shop on Upper Broadway the other day, relates a New York newspaper. Her automobile waited out- side. "Have you any chicken livers, William?" she asked of her favourite anatomist. "Yes, madam; 80 cents. a pound." "Give me half a pound," she said, "and cut them in halves. I'll take them," she said, "but be careful to cut each exactly in half." William fixed them with great care. "You see," she said, as she swept out, "I have two dogs, and if the liver is not exactly halved they fight about it." William fell in a faint.</p>
        <p>think that a day in the country is worth a week in the town.</p>
        <p>Before closing I must say that I shall be de- lighted to hear from you that you have fol, lowed my advice. Let me know what progress you make as members of the Beautiful War- rington Society ! _</p>
        <p>Your loving UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>THOUGHT FOR THE WEEK. . . Just the art of being kind Is all the sad world needs. ELLA WHEELER WILCOX..</p>
        <p>THE MODERN YOUTH. Boss: Haven't you swept the office out yet, Willie?"</p>
        <p>Willie: "No, sir." Boss: "Then what have you been doing?' Willie: "Sweeping out the dirt!"</p>
        <p>LIGHT _NOURISHMENT.</p>
        <p>A certain father who is fond of putting his boys through natural history examinations is often surprised by their smart replies. Re- cently he asked them to tell him "what animal is satisfied with the least nourishment." "The moth !" one of them shouted confidently: "It eats nothing but holes."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>BOYS, DON'T SMOKE!</head>
        <p>BOYS, DON'T SMOKE!</p>
        <p>In the Mims, Institute, in Philadelphia, the records for the past fifteen years have shown each year that tobacco users are more than twice -as likely as non-smokers to have tuber- lar Consumption—which shows very plainly that the lungs are damaged by the use of nicotine. •</p>
        <p>Growing boys who do not smoke increase their lung capacity in a year's time 75 per cent. more than smokers do, so that those who smoke use up their safety margin 75 per cent. faster than the non-smokers_ do, which explains why they get tuberculosis early. I Might go on and show how the safety margin of every organ of the body is consumed by the use of tobacco.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 23, 1915. IN THE COURTS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>BRAVE POLICEMAN.</head>
        <p>A BRAVE POLICEMAN.</p>
        <p>an instance of determined courage in the ~ of danger few stories could surpass that of Sergeant Cragg,... a Rochdale policeman, iii kugust, 1885 About one o'clock in the morning a terrible fire broke out at a shop in Drake- street, and before any 'help could arrive the whole front of the building was wrapped in flames. At an upper window two young ladies</p>
        <p>cold weather, but sitting long hours at needle- work and knitting in a thin blouse, as so many women have been doing this winter, is often a chilly occupation. Hence the evolution of a new garment known as the house coat. Simple in design and make, it comes well over the hips, the fulness at the waist being drawn in by a loose band. ,It may be made of divers materials —cloth, velveteen, crepe de chine, or knitted in silk, in which case it is furnished with sleeves. Usually it is sleeveless, the neck, armholes, and hem being edged with a narrow trimming. Made ill Mile eteerf:ll colour, the coat proviiits a real and useful house garment. The tea coat, designed to be worn with a trained skirt, is a more elaborate affair. In coloured materials it is built of printed chiffon, soft silk and satin, lace or any semi-transparent fabric, the neck O being open and the collar-..j A sTaniLnp one is preferred—wiF, Suitable materials and trim- for thesa garments may be .bought at considerebie 'reductions during the sales, and It is hil economical outlay, seeing that a smart Mat may be relied upon to vary two or khree gowns. For those in mourning a coat of black ninon relieved with plisse frills of black or white chiffon, is very suitable.</p>
        <p>Cluny and Torchon Lace. Cluny is a French lace made on a pillow, and now very rare and expensive. Torchon lace re- sembles it, and is often quite a clever imitation. Like Maltese lace, Cluny and Torchon frequently have geometrical designs introduced into their patterns. All these owe theit origin to the old lacis, made centuries ago in the convents, for the adornment of altar cloths, coverlets, and priests' vestments. Lacis had a groundwork of square net, on which patterns were worked. Coloured silks and gold threads were sometimes used. A modern name for lacis is Cluny -guipure. Both Cluny and Torchon are most cleverly imitated by machinery, but the produc- tions of the latter neither wear nor wash as well as the pillow-made lace, although they are almost as beautiful in appearance. The Ger- mans have, of late years, very cleverly imitated Maltese lace, which they have often sold for the work of Maltese lace-workers, and at the same prices. Detection is almost impossible, until the lace is cleaned or washell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <p>. 'e,•*</p>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH. Useful Information in Popular Language.</p>
        <p>Effects of Foul Air.</p>
        <p>Serious effects are produced by the breathing of vitiated air, and especially when the air is overheated as well as foul and more or less com- pletely saturated with moisture. Indeed, there is reason to believe that this saturation with moisture is one of the chief evils a-sociated with the breathing of impure air. The breathing of impure air has the direct effect of reducing vitality and lowering the natural resistive forces of the body. Respiration itself is not only slowed, but becomes more shallow so that less and less air is taken into the lungs. The cir- culation of the blood too is hindered and weak- ened and the health becomes undermined, and the victim becomes more and more susceptible to disease. Nutrition too is distinctly inter- fered with, and there is a tendency to fainting sensations, general malaise, drowsiness, head- aches, and ancenlia. Those who for one reason or another are obliged to breathe bad air for any length of time become more susceptible to catarrh, colds, influenza, bronchitis, pneu- monia, consumption, and ottter infectious dis- orders.</p>
        <p>How to Get Rid of Blackheads. What is termed a blackhead is simply the mouth of a little duct in the skin becoming filled with hardened fat. Whenever this occurs it is important that the entire skin be gone over in such a way as to completely empty each duct every day, so that there will be no accumulation of this hardened 'fat. If one of those little rolls of fat or comedones, after being squeezed out of the skin, is put under the microscope and ex- amined, it will be found to be swarming with parasites—A peculiar kind of creature, known as the dermodex folliculorum. There is a whole family of them scrabbling about, and they come out on the skin at night when everything is quiet, and creep into other places, and so extend the colony. There are multitudes of them, and as they move about they gather dirt and germs, I which they carry with them into the .skin. If there happen to be some infectious gerlbs there that produce suppuration, then you'," get a pimple. So it is necessary that theiiii follicles should be thoroughly emptied, and the skin I kept thoroughly clean. It is sometimes neces- sary to disinfect it. Washing the skin with a little soap• is of very great value. Applying a fine oil to the skin is a good means of softening up the fat and the follicles so that they can be emptied. The oil should be applied quite hot. Bathing the face with very hot water is a good plan, because that softens the fat, so it can be easily squeezed out.</p>
        <p>were seen in terror for their lives. Blankets were spread out for them,- and into these they jumped, receiving unfortunately serious in- juries, in their fall. But it was well they leaped, for when the escape appeared the flames had- become so fierce and the smoke so dense that the machine could not be used. Still two young boys were left in the building. One ' could be seen actually clinging tO the Window- , sill of a room •on the top floor. If anything was to be done to save them it must be attempted at once. Police-Constable Cragg made the effort. 'Three times he endeavoured to enter ,the house, and at length he succeeded and found the staircase. He climbed to the third floor, where he heard moaning; he strug- gled through the smoke towards the sound, and stumbled over a retriever dog, which was lying helpless on the floor, while on the bed lay a boy, quite unconscious. Cragg seized the lad and carried him to the stairs, stumbling and falling with his burden, for the choking smoke was beginning to tell upon him. But at the foot of the stairs he met others who relieved him of his burden; and alter breathing fresh air, the intrepid policeman ascended the ,stairs once again and brought down„the other boy terribly burned from the window. Surely this was enough. But no. Grigg plunged again into the burning house. For what? To save the dog Again he climbed those perilous steps; he lifted the dnimal from the floor, he bore it to the top of the stairs, and there he was met by a fireman, and together they rescued the poor creature from a terrible death. Cragg was made, a sergeant in the Police Fove, and received numerous testimonials, both pecuniary and otherwise, among them being the isilver medal of "The Quiver.".</p>
        <p>AT EVENSONG. (BY JESSIE Simi.) Tired bird, sink now to rest Within thy sheltering nest. r- The day is gone; Night Coineth on. •</p>
        <p>Our rat, too, past recall Fades: and the shadows fall Joy is forgot; Light Shine th not.</p>
        <p>Yet doth Time's burdened bourne Hold us not hope-forlorn. Lo! God's dear days, - Bright In His. praise.</p>
      </div>
