      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>– / / s • .1". o+oooo • **•••••••• •••••., ''‘ . ‘ ••••••• 00++0.`,44440***4&gt;+.00440 *460 4,.. 4,4. 4 .:******-&gt; o•)******** +++4+4 I w 4:****oo++ *0+4.0+4.00* 000 00 • 0.0 *4 00 ..2‘ -"... -:. 0* • 0 • * t• • 4* 4. •:. • • • • . • 0 • •</head>
        <p>- / / s • .1". o+oooo • **•••••••• •••••., ''‘ . ‘ ••••••• 00++0.`,44440***4&gt;+.00440 *460 4,.. 4,4. 4 .:******-&gt; o•)******** +++4+4 I w 4:****oo++ *0+4.0+4.00* 000 00 • 0.0 *4 00 ..2‘ -"... -:. 0* • 0 • * t• • 4* 4. •:. • • • • . • 0 • •</p>
        <p>. -_..._.._ Two distinct periods, 1850 and 1880, have lately furnished designers with inspiration for new fashions, the result being that two silhouettes have been proposed. The skirts modelled on those of the first date are wide at the sides only, and are quite fiat in front and at the back, the lining being used to heighten the effect of fulness. The bodices to these gowns are decidedly scanty, almost tight-fitting, and some are pointed in front, where they are boned!! For evening wear the corsago is cut very low in front and at the back, and the necks are shaped to show the top of the shoulder. The character- ' istic feature of the 1880 gown is a straight line front Shoulder to hem, in fact, a very simple silhouette, although the skirts hang in long folds. The tight waists of that period are taboo, and there is no danger that a fashion so prejudicial to health will be revived. The cor- sage of these gowns is very long and somewhat masculine in effect. Which of these styles will gain an ascendancy remains-10 be seen, but it is quite possible that both will be favoured: Fashionable Millinery.</p>
        <p>A cheerful note in Millinery, which solite'fr'hat relieves the gloom of the all-black hat, is struck when rich-coloured velvet and silk flowers are used as a trimming. This has a brightening effect which is, perhaps, more needed, because there is no Riviera season, and the gay fashions it inspires, to cheer the gloom of this january. One of the latest toques is a decided pork pie, I almost exactly like those depicted by Leech, many years ago. Some are stiffly trimmed with flowers, others wig), flat or standing ruches of black corded ribbon. These shapes are trying to certain types of faces, but are decidedly smart when worn by those they suit. Veils seldom appear with pork pie and other small hats, but when they do they end at the nose. On larger headgear veils of bordered lace are worn loosery over the face, and thrown back over the hat at will. These are decidedly picturesque and novel features, and most welcome at the pres- ent time. House and Tea Coats. -</p>
        <p>• Summer and winter make but little difference in our mode of dress. People do not nowadays smother themselves in thick clothing in the</p>
        <p>I- .'ik</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. S. Wright (in the chair), Aldermen Sniethuret and Bolton, Messfq. T. Looker, G. Pilling and W. Furness.</p>
        <p>"No. 2 Carson's Volunteers."—Michael Kelly, 22, Miller-street, Leigh, was charged with hav- ing been drunk and disorderly in Knutsford- road.—P.C. Ballard said that prisoner stuni6l.o off a tram-car in a drunken condition. Witness ' prevented him from falling, but prisoner com- menced to use abusive language and said: "I am No. 2 in Carson's Volunteers."—A fine of 4s. 6d. with an alternative of seven days' im- pris9ninent wos imposed, in a Pit oi fernper.—James Harris, 19, Bes- wick-street, was charged with having -wilfully broken- three saucers and three plates at his home, and with having assaulted his sister, Ethel.—The sister said that because her mother refused to give "Jim" any money he HOW into a temper and started quarreling. He said he "would finish" the baby, witness and her mother. He threw the pots off the table and kicked her in the side, also striking her on the, head with the poker, although it was only a ' light blow. They were afraid .to stay in the house unless a constable was posted in the neighbourhood.—P.C. Taylor said that when charged prisoner said "They have not two saucers in the hzonse, and only one cup; I did not strike her with the poker; I took it from her."—Prisoner said that his mother and sister were always ", throwing in his face" the fact</p>
        <p>that he had t'o maintain a -child. He did not kick his sister and only knocked the table over in temper. He :would pay the damages and. go</p>
        <p>in lodgings.—SuPerinteildent : It is quite impossible for him to keep away. This is his fourteenth appearance since he was up at eleven years of age for stealing. When he.was liberated after serving, a term under, the Borstal system he walked .all the way back from Mon- mouthshire. He enlisted in the Army and de- serted. He then came back to his mother.— Harris was sent to prison. for two months—one month on each charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair), Messrs. T. Locker, J. Hepherd, and C. Jackson Holmes.</p>
        <p>A Fit Case for Borstal Treatment.—The mag- istrates had again before• them Annie May Vickers, a young woman, giving an address in Willi a ms-street, Salford, who had previously been 'charged with obtaining by means of false pretences board and lodging to the value of 12s. from Lena Bostock, of 4, Poachers-lane, be- tween Christmas Day, and January 3rd, and with stealing 3s. 3d. in cash and a number of articles belonging to Mrs. Bostock, and also with burglariously entering the same woman's house on January sth, and stealing therefrom 2s. 9d. in cash and articles, to the total value of £3 15s.—Supt. Williamson said it was desir- able that the woman should be dealt with under the Borstal system, and he asked that she should be remanded for a further seven days to the prison at Liverpool. All the stolen property had been reeovered.—The magistrates agreed to the Superintendent's suggestion.</p>
        <p>Disorderly Man's Narrow Escape.—Joseph</p>
        <p>Barton, wiredrawer, 4, Firth-place; was sum- moned for having been guilty of disorderly con- duct on ,Bank Quay -Station.—Mr. A. Browne (for the L. and N.-W. Railway Company) said that on December 28th defendant went to see ;some soldier friends " off " by the 11.34 up mail _train to London. The platform was crowded. and defendant was walking up and down and bumping into people. He had had some drink, and"when the train came in he fell against the carriage door, and was saved from falling on to the metals by Foreman Bailey. Defendant be- came very abusive, and refused to leave the platform. Foreman Morley rendered assistance, and defendant was taken down to the ticket collector's office, having tripped Foreman Bailey on the way. When interviewed during the next day defendant said, "I am sorry for what oc- curred. I had had a drop of drink and I am always the same, when in drink. I hope the company will overlook it, as I am willing to pay for any damage done."—A fine of 10s. and costs was imposed, with £1 is. for the advocate's fee in addition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. J. W. Hayward (in the chair; Alderman Smethurst. and Messrs. C. Jackso:</p>
        <p>Holmes and W. T. Williams. How Not To See The Son Off.—Michael Davitt, labourer, 16, Erwood-street, was charged with hiving been drunk and disorderly in Bridge-street.—lt was stated that prisoner was helplessly drunk and fell against a pony and trap, which was standing in the street.—Pris- oner (who made his fourth appearance) said: My son was going to London and I got a drop of drink.—A fine of ss. including costs was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>COUNTY PETTY SOSIONS.</head>
        <p>COUNTY PETTY SOSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr.--R. C. Parr (in the chair), Councillor</p>
        <p>IL : Roberts and Mr. W. Fatraough.</p>
        <p>Wild Birds' Weekly Day of Immunity.— Joseph Reenan, 35, Hood-lane, Sankey, was summoned for having shot a wild bird on Sunday, January 10th, contrary to the Wild Birds' Protection Order.—P.C. Harding said he saw defendant firc at and kill a seagull which was flying over the St. Helens and Sankey Canal. Reenan, who had been firing from his back . garden, picked the bird up, and, when challenged about the matter, said it was one of his pigeons that he had shot. He refused to let witness see the bird. He said: "It's all right; I will give up shooting.. They are all after me."—Defendant: I have the pigeon here in my pocket.—The Clerk: It does not matter if it was a pigeont—lt was pointed out that it was an offenee to shoot or kill any bird on a Sunday.—A fine of ss. was infposed. Hit in the Pocket by the War.—When a traveller, residing in a house ented at .1:30 year, was ,summoned to show cause why dis— tress should not be levied on his goods and chattels, in respect of 1:13 arrears for rent, he said that he could not pay as the war had affected him.—He had, he said, only earned .V 29- 10s. since September, as against £4O for the corresponding period of the previous year.— The Clerk: And you think you can live rent free?—Defendant: No. I have paid some of the rent. I have offered to pay six months' rent and pay so much off the arrears.—The Clerk: You can get a cheaper house.—Defendant: I am prepared to go into a ,smaller house if they will release me.—The Agent: We will release you.— The Clerk: How has the war affected you?— Defendant: My commission has gone less, and that is all I earn.—The: Agent said he knew someone in the same line of business as de- fendant whose earnings had been more since the war.—The magistrates said that if the money were not paid in a month the distress warrant would be granted.—Defendant: You might as well do it at once, because I cannot pay it—The Clerk: Let it take its ordinary course.—Defendant: This is a case for the County Court, under the Emergency Act, and not for a Police Court.—A distress warrant was issued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. J. Rymer Young (in the chair), Dr. Naden, and Messrs. A. J. Jolley and W. T. Williams.</p>
        <p>"Celebrating" Soldier Son's ;-.•omecoming.--- A defendant who was ordered to, pay 4s. 6d. costs for having used obscene language in his house in such a manner that it could be heard in the street, pleaded that he was only making merry because one of his soldier sons had come home on a week's furlough. He did not think he used bad language; he was only singing and step-dancing. He had two sons at the Front, and a third was going in another month or so.</p>
        <p>Trio of J uven ile Defendants.—Three boys were brought before the magistrates, sitting as a Juvenile Court. One was .fined 2s. 6d, for throwing a stone in Buttermarket-street.' 'A police officer said the lad threw the stone at him, but the defendant said it was not meant for the officer.—Another lad was ordered to pay 3s. 6d. for shouting, whistling and "making an un§eenily noise with his mouth" in Sankey- street On Sunday night; and a third was fined 25.• 6d. for using an air rifle, for which he had no licence, to pot at the knob on the top of a street lamp. The lad's mother said the rifle was a Christmas boy, and she did not know a licence: was required, thinking .it was only a toy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ADULT SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>ADULT SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>THE YEAR'S ACTIVITIES OF THE BEWSLY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The annual meetings of the Bewsey Adult School were held last week. On Saturday even- ing there was a tea, followed by a concert. The attendance numbered. over '2OO. The evening meeting was presided over by Mr. Wm. Web- ster, who spoke of the pleasure it gave him to occupy that position, as he was well aware of the good work of the school in the Bewsey neigh- bourhood. The • reports read by Mr. Harry Wright on behalf of the Men's School; and Mrs. Itt Piduatr for tht, W9lan'q *c4991: 011 the whole, WM eriebiikaging. RefelTll,Cel we Made to the splendid Bible lessons hrtaitged Ly the National Council of Adult Schools. These Bible studies have been educational and helpful. Among the lecturettes, a variety of topics had been discussed, such as "The life of a common house fly," "Housing," "National Service," "Eugenics," "Insect life in Smith America," and "]volution." Such discussion could not fail to be of interest and instructive. The Social Club connected with the. Behool provided mem- er 111Qa!i o€ 'enjoyabl.o intercourse, such as billiard tournaments, draught matches, , &amp;c. An interesting and, important development took place last summer in the lease of some land near the school premises for allotment gardens and the provision of-a bowling green: The latter was largely due to the generosity Of Mr. Ran- some. This departure had succeeded in creat- ing much interest; and giving useful knowledge to the members in the art of gardening. Men- tion was made of the help rendered to the Bel- gian Refugees at the Adult School .Guest House, Heys Farm. The funds of the School were in a health i condition. The Secretary spoke with regret of the little use made Of the excellent School library. Under the guidance of Mr. John Elwell, the Junior School continued its good work. Mr. Wright concluded his encour- aging report by urging the members to further effort in spreading abroad the atmosphere and fellowship of Adult School work. Mr. David P. Boyd. (of Manchester), one of the National Organising Secretaries, spoke of the needs of to-day, and indicated ways in which Adult Schools could help to meet those needs. An excellent musical programme was -given. Mr. Tom Tanner officiated at the piano, Miss Eva Tanner, Mr. Pearce and Mr. J. Hannan being the vocalists. The music was much ap- preciated, several encores being given. Mr.</p>
        <p>Nurse amused the' audience with some-humorous sketches. A cordial vote of thanks was passed to the artists and all those who assisted to make the meeting so successful. HELPFUL BOOKS.</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning Mr. Boyd gave a talk to a combined school of men and women on "Books that have helped me." The lecturette consisted _</p>
        <p>of a brief su-r,vey of the principal novels of Charles Kingsley, Charles Dickens, Flora Annie Steel, and Nathaniel Hawthorn. Refer- ence was made to the important social reforms</p>
        <p>effected by some of these works, especially Kingsley and Dickens. Readingf light novels such as these was recommended as a change, after a hard day's toil in forge or factory. Mr. Boyd conducted the Bible lesson on "The Open Road." A free and useful discussion brought out several interesting points. THE NEED OF REVERENCE.</p>
        <p>,A fireside conference, attended by members of other Schools, was held after the ordinary 'school on "Extension work." The fellowship meeting in the evening was held in Messrs. Greening's dining hall, and in spite of the bad weather was Well attended. Mr. Bansome- pre- sided and an inspiring address was given by Mr. Boyd. The speaker pleaded for more reverence in life, reverence for the greater things. The age had unique opportunities for service, and he spoke of the many ways by which Adult School men and women could render service to their generation. • Mr. Turton kindly assisted as vocalist. The week-end proved to be a source of strength and encouragement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>CIIAMBEIILAIN, MUNICIPAL AM)</head>
        <p>CIIAMBEIILAIN, MUNICIPAL AM)</p>
        <p>SOCIAL REFORMER. To those who know ,ittle of Joseph Chamber- lain beyond his political activity since entering Parliament, the story of his municipal and social life before that time comes as a revela- tion.</p>
        <p>This was slumn very clearly in the lecturette given by Mr. A. Albinson at the Stockton Heath Adult School. Born in 1836, when Reform was in the atmosphere, Chamberlain imbibed much of the spirit of the time. Brought up in a "free" religious school, he came under the in- fluence of George Dawson and B. W. Dale, who at that time made Birmingham famous as a centre of practical religious teaching. . He de- voted himself so closely to the business of his father and uncle—studying at the same time the interests of the workers and joining them in debate and self-improvement—that he was early in life able to take a good measure of freedom which he employed unsparingly in the, muni- cipal and -social reform of his adopted city. For this work he got the best of all training by entering intimately into the life of the-workers; teaching in the night school such subjects as English and French, history and literature, and thus gaining their confidence and affection. This was a strong support in his future work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>CIVI-C GOSPEL.</head>
        <p>THE CIVI-C GOSPEL.</p>
        <p>Entering municipal lire in 1869—when the city abounded in slums and its management was at a low ebb—he began to put into practice the civic gospel preached by his friend, Geo. Daw- son. He found Birmingham dirty, with gas at ss. per 1,000, and bad at that, water supply only three days fier week, and a death-rate appallingly high. By preaching the dignity of municipal service he inspired a n 'tube... of like-minded men to join him, with the result that in :four years he was elected mayor of the city. A series of great reforms were then brought about, for which Birmingham will ever be his debtor. The gas supply came under the municipality, the water company was bought out, and follow- ing these a great scheme of street improvement was undertaken. These soon began to bear fruit. The death-rate -fell from 26 to 20 per 1,000, and in some areas from 60 and 80 down to 25 per 1,000. Nor was this all. He was largely responsible for the opening of a new free library and art gallery, and in the cause of educational reform he Iva., one of the foremost workers.</p>
        <p>Ile became chairman of the National Educa- tional League, and carried on a vigorous pro- paganda for compulsory and free education. The splendid Birmingham University owes much—very much—to his initiative and sup- port. Temperance reform also had in him an ardent</p>
        <p>—although independent—champion, as well as th- cause of land reform. He held that all -nonopolies for public 7ervices should be under public; control. Leaving out of consideration any other of his</p>
        <p>extensive' subsequent activities, a Programme of accomplished work to his credit has been ckeadY mentioned for which Birmingham—and more than Birmingham—may well honour his memory. k</p>
        <p>_ An interesting talk followed, in which it was pointed out that it was fitly during his mayor- alty that the statue to Priestley was unveiled by Profesor Huxley in 1874.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>STOP THAT RHEUMATISM</head>
        <p>STOP THAT RHEUMATISM</p>
        <p>"Stop it now, at once! There is no need for you to suffer the pangs and agonies of Rheuma- tism any longer. Every day, every hour almost, that you continue to suffer is self-inflicted tor- ture." This emphatic statement is made by Dr. Geo. E. Flood, whose successful labours to find a suitable and effective remedy for Rheu- matism, and not merely a means of temporary relief, have made his name famous all the world over.</p>
        <p>For the benefit of our readers, we append the prescription which he recommends. It, is : Syrup of Ginger Liquid Ralik-01a Compound - 1 oz. Syrup of Rhubarb - 1 oz. Tincture of Gentian Dose :. A teaspoonful after meals and at bed- time. We have the assurance of the Doctor that</p>
        <p>this prescription (which can -fie dispensed</p>
        <p>any chemist) can be taken with perfect safety by the most delicate man or woman. The first dose usually brings relief, ind if persisted in for a very short time will regult in a perfect cure of the ailment.</p>
        <p>WIZ</p>
      </div>
