      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>SANKEY – TREET, WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>SANKEY - TREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 23, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 23, 1915.</p>
        <p>Th e i)irectb 1 . . e ihi e - ,d 1 c , 1,-' - ' :::;. .1',1:..-• .0, • + ' `'. ''* ,' . . ' , , ' ~..1 A lea u , . . ..,, .. a • B I. ea C0U617..5'--,N.,.. . 'f 0 .11 dr ... S. - C (1119 ..S. &amp; lIRONCIIITIS . :-...:--_-. ~, PROTECT YOUR THROAT 8, CHEST. . . .</p>
        <p>i. 44.4.4.4.4.44+++++++++444 .1. 4. q. 44 POULTRY PEN. 'l4 .14 4. 4. 4. + Hints and Interesting Facts and .1. 44 Chat for Amateurs. .1.</p>
        <p>THE PURCHASE OF NEW STOCK</p>
        <p>A good many farmers have not yet completed their breeding pens, but it is high time that this was done. As a matter of fact, it should have been done at least six weeks ago. It is probable that one or more new male birds Will be required to mate with the pullets, since it does not do to use related birds for breeding purposes. Great care should be exercised in the purchase of new stock, since one injudicious mating may spoil the work of years. There are, however, nowadays so many reliable breed- ers who specialise in prolificacy and good table qualities that the difficulty is nothing, like so great as it was a few years ago. When requiring any new stock birds write to a trustworthy breeder, telling him exactly what is wanted, and the price one is willing to pay. Preferably the breeder should be one who lives some distance away, so as to avoid even. the least danger of obtaining a related bird. Contrary to the opinion of many poultry-keepers, the cock exerts a considerable influence upon both the laying and table qualities of the offspring, and thus, if a male bird, from a first-class laying strain is secured he speedily improves the egg record of the flock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>FOWLS AND ORCHARDS.</head>
        <p>FOWLS AND ORCHARDS.</p>
        <p>Many are the neglected orchards which we pass by road or rail, and their improvement is a national need. Stubbing and re-planting is in many cases the only apparent remedy, but the value of old orchards as fowl runs is worth con- sidering. The orchard is generally used as a run for convenience' sake and for the good of the fowls, but the benefit to the trees is very great, and we are confident that if chicken are systematically penned w an old orchard, the. trees at the same time eceiving a. thorough and expert pruning, in a kw. years a large and valuable increase of fruit will have been secured. Fowls are vigorous cultivators, and their manure is valuable, but they must be confined to the space which needs renovation.— "Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>AGE OF THE EGG.</head>
        <p>AGE OF THE EGG.</p>
        <p>The interval between the time eggs are laid and the time they reach the consumer should not be more than three to five days, according to the season of the year. If older than this they must face competition with foreign sup- plies, and have lost a measure of their new-laid quality. With things as they are at present delay in marketing is traceable to the dealer or retailer as well ai the producer. Unless the poultry-keeper delivers his eggs to private cus- tomers he loses control of them after they have passed out of his hands, but he Can at least ensure that he is responsible for no part of the delay. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>BREEDING.</head>
        <p>BREEDING.</p>
        <p>To ensure a regular supply of eggs throughout the year it is desirable to keep two breeds, one a non-sitting breed, and one kept for general purposes. The general purpose breed should Provide the necessary proportion of tinted eggs. Many strains of poultry at the present time are inclined to lay rather small eggs. To remedy this care should be taken to .breed only from hens laying eggs of the requisite size, and to select eggs for hatching tluitlfre of the desired weight. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>NESTS AND CLEANLINESS</head>
        <p>NESTS AND CLEANLINESS</p>
        <p>Nests should be sufficient in number, roomy, dry and clean. They should be removable to facilitate cleaning and the nesting material should be renewed frequently, especially if it becomes wet or soiled. If, in spite of every pre- caution, dirt has collected on the egg, it should at once be removed by means of a dry cloth. Should that not be sufficient a slightly damped but not wet, cloth may be used. It is desirable to lime-wash the nests frequently, to keep them free from parasites.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>WINTER RHEUMATISM.</head>
        <p>WINTER RHEUMATISM.</p>
        <p>The long spell of cold, wet weather is now having evil- effect on a number of people, and the tortures of rheumatism are disabling many. Victims of this winter malady have _every reason to fear the first dull aches in the limbs and joints, followed by sharp pains through the flesh and muscles; these are symptoms of poisons in the blood which will shortly develop into rheumatism at its worst.</p>
        <p>Rheumatism is a blood disorder; it arises from poisoned blood, loaded with acids and impurities, setting up inflammation in the muscles and joints. Pure, good blood dispels these dregs from the system; that is why, in cases of rheumatism, it is important to strengthen the blood supply by taking a course of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills; so rheumatism's pains and stiffnesses fade away, and you acquire freedom and a feeling of health that is a trans- formation from your former pain.</p>
        <p>Dr. Williams"- Pink Pills for Pale People make new blood, purify the existing blood, and cleanse the system of all dregs which give rise to rheumatism. Medicine has no direct action on rheumatism, but these Pills renew and purify the blood. Begin 'a course to-day; obtain them from any dealer (but be on your guard against substitutes), or send to Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, 2s. 9d. for one box, or 13s. 9d. for six.</p>
        <p>FREE.--Sufferers should- know more about the blood and its work; send a postcard to Book Dept., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, for the Free Health Guide.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>AFTER THE WAR.</head>
        <p>AFTER THE WAR.</p>
        <p>Work in the Empire for Returned</p>
        <p>Soldiers.</p>
        <p>As the result of recent informal conferences, aninfluential committee is, we understand, about to be formed with the object of urging upon the Government the necessity of making provision against the unemployment wh'ith it is anticipated will follow the declaration of peace. A guarantee of wctrk within the Empire, at home or in the Dominions, to every man who has responded to the country's call on his dis- charge from the forces is not considered impos- sible of attainment by those who are taking up the matter. The Salvation Army as one of the largest emigration societies of the Empire is, we are informed, deeply interested in the overseas aspect of the movement, and Commissioner Lamb, 'who continues to direct the Army's emi- gration work, sailed with the Transylvania for C0...0da on Saturday for the purpose of con- s.cring with the authorities of that dominion *cording the possibilities of the scheme in view.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>SHORTAGE OF FARM LABOUR IN CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>A Cheshire correspondent states that the shortage of farm labour is becoming serious in Cheshire. Farmers cannot get men. They have been complaining for two .or three years of the difficulty experienced in securing good farm hands, and the war has greatly increased these difficulties, for large numbers have now joined the forces. There is no labour at all obtainable in many places, and no little inconvenience is being caused, especially on farms where large milking stocks are kept and supplies of milk have to be sent away daily. The outlook for the busy season which is coming on is by no means favourable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>LIVE STOCK ON SMALL HOLDINGS.</head>
        <p>LIVE STOCK ON SMALL HOLDINGS.</p>
        <p>A significant fact about a good many small holdings created by County Councils in agricul- tural districts is the scarcity of live stock, which means that all the produce grown is sold off, no manure is made on the holding, and the land must inevitably suffer. No hard and fast rules can be laid down indicating how smallholders should proceed, because everything depends on local conditions. I will say this, however, that if the smallholder is to pay his rent and get his living by ordinary farming methods he must fall in line with the farmer and have some stock on his ground. I happen to know a hill sheep farm that was cut up some time ago for small holdings, but the holders are farming without sheep, presumably from the want of the necessary capital to buy them. I fear that trashy land will feel the need of the hoofs to consolidate it, and miss the old method of, being manured. What I say bf sheep in one district is applicable to horned stock in another, and the smallholder, in many cases, who does not possess the necessary capital to buy stock would be better without the land.—" Agricul- tural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>RASPBERRY CANES.</p>
        <p>It is a great mistake to leave raspberry canes that have just been planted their full length, as instead of making fresh growth from the base they start only at the top and try to fruit. The result of this non-cutting down is that the canes that are left are of no use that year. and no young ones having been made, there is no improvement the following year. The best way when forming fresh plantations of raspberries is to select the stoutest canes and those at some distance from the parent stool, as these are always well rooted, and when planted to cut them down at from 6in. to 9in. from the ground, allowing them not less than 4ft. apart in the rows; and if it is a strong-growing variety, such as Superlative, then a distance of 6ft. may be given. After planting, the young stools should be mulched with good (not over-rotten) manure, as in order to get them established quickly it is important to shade the roots' and prevent them suffering from drought. This the mulching will do, while at the same time the goodness of the manure will be washed down to the roots and the growth hastened. Amateurs and others often do their raspberry plantations great dam- age by digging among them. Instead of dig- ging, the ground should be heavily mulched after removing the old canes, leaving this as a surfacing all the summer, merely running the hoe through the plantation to destroy the weeds. To support .the canes of raspberries nothing equals, galvanised wire, which, to prevent injury to the canes, should receive two coats of the best white lead paint. With the first outlay for the wire the expense is over, whereas stakes want frequent renewal, and they are often ver difficult to procure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>APPLE STRIPED BEAUFIN.</head>
        <p>APPLE STRIPED BEAUFIN.</p>
        <p>Where a long supply of apples is required this should always find a place. It is not suitable as a garden tree, as, being a vigorous grower, the curtailment of the branches necessary affects its hearing. It is best in standard form, unless when planting it in the garden allowance is made for it to grow freely. The fruit is of large size and handsome appearance, while its late keeping is all in its favour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>RHUBARB.</head>
        <p>RHUBARB.</p>
        <p>This needs a good larder if large, succulent stalks are looked for. The present is a good time to apply a heavynlitich of very rich, rotten manure, which should be allowed to remain until .the early spring, when, as soon as growth is on the move, what is left mar be forked in, or, if the crowns are to be covered with litter to encourage serif° early gatherings, the ground should be forked ever beforehand, so thatthe large, fleshy roots may be benefited by the manure while they are assisting the tender young stalks to burst forth from the crowns, and also preparing the plants to build up fat crowns, for another season—that is, provided, of course, the produce is not gathered too closely. .‘</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>APPLE ADAM'S PEARMAIN.</head>
        <p>APPLE ADAM'S PEARMAIN.</p>
        <p>. This is one of the best of the Pearmain sec- tion, and does well in many of our Sussex orchards. Many years ago we remember the fine samples of this apple that used to be grown near Goodwood. It comes into Use in December, and, if allowed to hang late before being gath- ered, and carefully stored, may be had in use up till February. It is distinctly Pearmain shaped, the colour pale yellow, flushed and streaked with crimson, the flavour being brisk, juicy and refreshing. It is, however, an apple for certain soils only, as, if the ground is cold and heavy, then it is liable to canker. Give it, however, a warm, loamy, well-drained soil, and standards in such will not disappoint. We have also seen fine fruits in several of our Norfolk gardens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>GOOD KING HENRY.</head>
        <p>GOOD KING HENRY.</p>
        <p>This, also known as Mercury, is largely grown in Lincolnshire, and is valued on account of its hardiness. When well established, it will last for many years, but the best results follow on dividing the roots, say, every three years, and re-planting in well-manured fresh quarters. It can be easily raised from seed, which should be sown in April in rows 15in. apart, allowing about half that distance between the. plants in the row when thinned. The following year each alternate plant may be lifted and used for mak- ing a fresh bed. The young shoots early in the spring make a good Vegetable if cut like aspara- gus. When grown for this purpose the plants may be covered with leaf soil or very old rotten manure in the winter. The shoots are in this way blanched, and when cut make a very good addition to our vegetables at s time when they are not too plentiful.—Wick in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <p>0</p>
        <p>SYMINGT</p>
        <p>Cold and foggy! What a day! Face it bravely! Her Spningion's Essence!! Hurry up ! ! Pour boiling water in your cup And "Little Mary's" cheery cry is "Hullo Happiness. I—Gloom good-bye THOS. SYMINGTON O. CO.,Edhsburgh</p>
        <p>~ .. . . . ..rt. .; CIE . T. 1...., .... .e is the way : • , .. •- '7,-- . -!•,' ...--------. - "-f"---;(1 and Lenden ." 44/ ,o—or'' --.....0r• %,........." .....—.....-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>WALTON SHOW.</head>
        <p>WALTON SHOW.</p>
        <p>Agricultural Society Report Another</p>
        <p>EFFECT OF THE WAR.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Agri- cultural Society was held in the Masonic Rooms, Bold-street, on Wednesday afternoon, Sir Gilbert Greenall presiding over a fair attendance of members, including Mr. Bain- bridge (secretary) and Mr. A. Broome (treasurer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>FINANCES.</head>
        <p>THE FINANCES.</p>
        <p>The statement of accounts, as submitted by the Treasurer, showed that on the year's work- ing there was a deficit balance of Alk 15s. 7d. The receipts amounted to £476 Bs. 10d., and this sum included £233 is. subscriptions, £B9 18s. 6d. entrance fees, £63 us. 6d. gate receipts, and £4B special prizes. The total expenditure amounted to £7lB 4s. 5d., £452 of which was for prizes, £93 18s. 3d. for show-yard, £95 9s. 10d. for printing, advertising, &amp;c., and £5l 17s. 6d. for judges' fees and salaries. At the end of 1913 there was a balance in hand of £56 155., and Sir Gilbert Greenall having given £2OO, the actual financial position of the society was that to-day they had £l4 19s. sd. in hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>GETTING "WORSE EVERY YEAR."</head>
        <p>GETTING "WORSE EVERY YEAR."</p>
        <p>Mr. Broome said the balance sheet was not a good one. In fact, they seemed to get worse eery year. Ile would suggest that the amount of subscription be raised. He did not like the idea of going to Sir Gilbert for .R2OO or so every year on top of the hundreds of pounds he gave in various other ways. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>WHY LAST YEAR'S SHOW WAS HELD. The Cormcil's annual report was read bx the Secretary. It stated : • " The 25th annual show was held in Walton Park on Thursday, the 6th of August, and the gardens, piggeries and pleasure grounds were as usual kindly thrown open to the visftors to the show by Sir Gilbert-Greenall. "There were 39 classes with a total entry of 272 exhibits, as against 38 classes with an entry of 254 exhibits last year, so that from that point of view the show gives a decided improve- ment and meant a corresponding increage in entry fees; on the other hand, owing to the out- break of war just previous to the holding of the show, the consequent disorganisation of railway traffic and the general unrest of the whole country, a reduction in the gate was a foregone conclusion. It was felt that the show being largely of a local 'character and most of the entries, excepting the riding and driving classes, able to come by road, great disappoint- ment would be felt if it was abandoned (though it was one of the last to be held), Sir Gilbert Greenall very kindly, consented to go on with the building of the show-yard and recognised that he would have to do more than he has done in the past to meet the deficit.</p>
        <p>Big Drop In Gate Money.</p>
        <p>"The gate and stands only realised £BO 13s. 6d., as against ~C 153 15s. last year. One result of this was that the usual grant of the caterers, Messrs. Agar and Son; had to be foregone, and we were fortunate in being able to have them on any terms as they were already then requi- sitioned by the War Office to their full capa- bility. The prizes awarded amounted to within a few pounds of the same as last year. Printing and advertising to .V. 4 more, consequent on several items, which are stock, having to be renewed this year, and also the new innovation of giving rosettes.</p>
        <p>"As an exhibition it was one of the most successful ever held. The Shires were a splen- did collection, and the show of dairy cattle one that any show executive would be proud of. Great strides have been made in this latter de- partment in the last feiir— years, and an ever- increasing interest is being taken in milk pro- duction.</p>
        <p>"One new feature of an interesting character was the class for child's pony, for which there were eleven entries, and the boy and girl riders gave a good show in the large ring. The show of tradesmen's turnouts was excellent, espe- cially the heavy classes. More and more diffi- culty is experienced year by year to get a show of riding hunters and horses in harness, and after the war it is feared that for some time these classes will be hard to fill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Matter For Serious Consideration.</head>
        <p>Matter For Serious Consideration.</p>
        <p>"One matter for serious consideration is the future of this show; the subscriptions do not at all adequately represent the importance of the show and the value of the krizes given, and but for.-Sir Gilbert Greenall's munificent generosity it would, some time ago, .have had to have been given up or degenerated into a very' small affair. Sir Gilbert gives up his park each year, fits up the whole of the stands, ring, fittings for cattle and horses, finds free stabling for those who are too far away to come on the morning of the show, and in every possible way furthers the interests of the show, besides giving several special prizes and mak- ing a heavy grant each year to make up the deficit between income and expenditure. It is true that \ Sir Gilbert's tenants largely benefit from this show, but other estates' tenants also reap good results from it, and it is lamentable to think that so little real support is given to it by those interested in farming. Seven years ago— in 1907—the subscriptions and special cash prizes amounted, to £475 25.; last year they had gone down to £,299 Os. 6d. This year there is a further decrease, the total sum being only £279 lie., and this is to meet a prize schedule of £452, and all the other expenses. This year we have lost, by death or retirement, 53 members, and only 19 new ,ones have joined. Sir Gilbert has been called upon this year for £2OO, in addition to all the other expenses, and, notwithstanding this, you have only a balance in hand of £l4 19s. sd. The situation is serious and IVe submit it to the members for their con- sideration. The Council cannot adequately express their feelings of gratitude to Sir Gilbert for all he has done for the show in the past, but they. think the time has come when the society should make such an effort as will enable them to relieve him of at least some of the heavy liabilities he has so ungrudgingly settled during the past 25 years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Donors of Special Prizes.</head>
        <p>Donors of Special Prizes.</p>
        <p>"Lord Gerard kindly consented to act as vice- president for the year, and favoured the society by the presentation of a silver,pup in the cattle section. Special prizes of cups or money were also given by the following gentlemen, to whom the Council render their grateful thanks: Mr. P. Stapleton-Bretherton, Mr. John Coxon, Mr. H. H. Armitage, the Cheshire Hunt, Mr. G. Norris Midwood, Mr. Kenworthy, Mr. James Gould; Messrs. Whitley, and Colonel Alan J. Sykes, M.P., and the latter geptleman still further favoured the society by sending the splendid Band of the 6th Battalion Cheshire Regiment, free of cost, to the society. The Hunter, Shire and Hackney medals were also offered as usual. "Our sincere thanks are also 'due to the stewards and the honorary veterinary surgeons for the admirable manner in which they carried out their duties."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>"A CALAMITY -IF THE SHOW WENT DOWN."</head>
        <p>"A CALAMITY -IF THE SHOW WENT DOWN."</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Ball moved the adoption of the re- ports and said it would be a calamity if the show went down in a place like Warrington, with so many leading agriculturists around. He hoped each member would double his gubscrip- tion. He was sorry to see that so-many War- rington tradesmen had withdrawn. He did not know the reason. They might say that they did not get any benefit from the show in the 1</p>
        <p>WALTON SHOW—continued.</p>
        <p>exhibits they took, but he might say that they got the benefit from the people who did exhibit. He hoped the subscribers would try and get new members to join the society. (Hear, hear.) Mr. T. M. West seconded and said they all ought to do more than they had done. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greenall : It is an unfortunate time, but we always do have unfortunate times— first the railway strike and then the war. Mr. Ball: There might be bright days ahead. Mr. West: Every cloud has a silver lining.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <p>Continued in next coltunn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>LASTING PEACE.</head>
        <p>A LASTING PEACE.</p>
        <p>Can Only Be Founded Upon Spirit</p>
        <p>of Love.</p>
        <p>" In every tramcar is the picture of a well- known figure saying, with imperious gesture: ' I want more men and still more until the enemy is crushed.' In imagination I can see another face, full of sorrow and lined with grief. His eyes are looking out with pity upon the world, and He is saying in pleading tones:</p>
        <p>But I said unto you: Love your enemies.' Is Christ, then, not wanting us to-day to love all mankind?"</p>
        <p>With these words Mr. J. J. Garnett concluded the third of the series of addresses upon "Chris- tianity and War," v‘hich are being given at the Friends' Meeting House, Buttermarket-street, during the war.</p>
        <p>One could hardly speak to anybody nowadays, said the speaker, without ,hearing some expres- sion of the hatred and fear •of the Germans, and the fearful things they would do if they could reach this country. Men's minds seemed to be gripped with fear, and it was hard to keep a calm mind, and not to be affected by that same spirit of fear and hate. This spirit was not only swaying our own country, but the whole civilised world—it seemed the one force that was uppermost. But they who were met in that room believed in something different from that ;,.they believed that Christ came into the world to teach man that, instead of fear and hate, the spirit of love was to rule.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>PROBLEMS OF SETTLEMENT.</head>
        <p>PROBLEMS OF SETTLEMENT.</p>
        <p>During all this time of strife, they heard of etalk going on—more or less behind the scenes —as to what the settlement was to be based upon when the time of peace came. All seemed agreed on one point : There must be no more war. On the one hand they beard the militar- ist bring forward his plea for a citizen army and compulsory military service, saying that this would ensure such a peace as had not been enjoyed before. He said, for instance, that if we had been prepared on August 4th, we should never have had that terrible conflict. All his arguments were based upon the idea that force was to be the remedy—that the same powers which had actuated men's minds in the past were to actuate them in the future. To his (the speaker's) mind that seemed an utterly false stand to take, and one which was bound to fail. Then on the other hand th6re were .those—very unpopular at present—who were tell- ing us in a quiet way, because they were await- ing the time when they should get a better hearing, that the settlement must be based upon an entirely different international policy, and that no secret alliances must be entered into— the\ people must have full knowledge of all alli- ances. In this way they hoped to remove causes of rancour and bitternesS amongst the nations. their proposals being based upon the eternal principles of love. Let them hope that wisdom would be given to them if they ever succeeded in bringing their principles into practise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>PROBLEMS OF EXPEDIENCY.</head>
        <p>PROBLEMS OF EXPEDIENCY.</p>
        <p>Now all these problems were problems or ex- pediency, depending upon certain contingencies. They could only be carried out if one assumed that the arms of the Allies were to be success- ful. Upon those principles entirely depended Christ's law of love and brotherhood. Then what was the, Christian's attitude towards these problems to be They would have to take a much bolder stand Than they had ever taken in the past. They would have to take a united stand, dropping all differences and forgetting they had ever had but one thought in their minds—that of loving service. Ile supposed that they were all convinced that in their lives they had had some knowledge of Christ's pres- ence. If they dwelt upon that thought for a moment they would see that Christendom had been guilty of a great omission, and that she would have to sdo that work which had been en- trusted to her.</p>
        <p>Let them think of the warring nations which had called upon men to-comelorward and fight. They had talked• of patriotism, honour, self- sacrifice and what not? These men had an- swered the call in millions; they were per- suaded that they were doing their duty, and wer, prepared to die for their ideas. Their own personality was sunk in a common obedi- ence to the spirit of fear and hate; the whole fabric of society was shaken by its dread.</p>
        <p>CHRIST'S LAW OF LOVE?</p>
        <p>Howcihen, was it going to be with them, who believed in Christ's law of love? Was His call to be world-wide? Was the obedience less re- quired? Just as the many-hued Allies united in a common obedience, so must Christians forget their varying creeds and beliefs and races in the common service, where must be no note of strife or of discord. The only thing that counted would be the fellowship of service. If the outcome of this war were such ,a uniting together of Christians it would not have been in vain. Let them throw away trivialities, and learn that the chief thing was love.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>"BLACK LIST" SUGGESTED.</head>
        <p>A "BLACK LIST" SUGGESTED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ball suggested that a "black list" of those who did not pay should be given to the Council representative for the district, so that he could persevere with them. Mr. A. Willett said that the work in ihe various districts should be taken up more actively. They had become somewhat apathetic and lazy, thinking that Sir Gilbert was behind. (Laughter, and hear, hear.) •• The reports were adopted, and it was decided that the questions raised therein should be dealt with fully by the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>PRESIDENT RE-ELECTED.</head>
        <p>THE PRESIDENT RE-ELECTED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ball proposed that Sir Gilbert be re- elected president, and said they all knew how generous he was, and he (the speaker) was only sorry they had to trespass on ills generosity. Mr. T. Whitlow seconded, remarking that Walton Show would be a poor thing without Sir Gilbert.</p>
        <p>In reply, the President said they would try the best they could to pull things through, for they must have a show of some description, if it were only a small one. If they could keep the breeder-classes together it did not matter much about the spectacular side of the show. (Hear, hear.) He would be• pleased to do all he could. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>NEW VICE-PRESIDENT.</head>
        <p>THE NEW VICE-PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Roger C. Parr was elected vice-president, on the proposition of Mr. T. Warburton, sec- onded by Mr. T. M. West. Mr. Parr said he was always away in August and had not attended a: single show. Sir Gilbert: If you help us during the year we will let you off for the show. (Laughter.) Mr. Parr: I will do .my best to promote the interests of the society, which is so well known. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>COUNCIL FOR 1915-16.</head>
        <p>THE COUNCIL FOR 1915-16.</p>
        <p>The following gentlemen were elected mem- bereof the Council for the districts mentioned: N.E., Messrs. F. A. Milling and T. M. West; N.W., G. G. Marsh (Speke) and Thomas Nor- cott (Burtonwood); S.E., H. E. Gibbon (tymm) and Joseph Knowles (Agden); S.W., C. Black- shaw (Hatton) and T. Warburton (Daresbury).</p>
        <p>A hearty' vote of thanks was accorded. Sir Gilbert ,Greenall, on the proposition of Mr. West, seconded by Mr. Willett:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>JUNIOR LIBERAL CLUB.</head>
        <p>JUNIOR LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>Proposals to Enhance Its Popularity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>NEW FEATURES INTRODUCED.</head>
        <p>NEW FEATURES INTRODUCED.</p>
        <p>With a view to, in a measure, re-organising and enhancing the attractiveness of the War- rington Junior Liberal Club—which has most comfortable quarters in Horsemarket-street, and from which much good educational and hard spade-work for the cause of Liberalism has radiated in the past—a well-attended meeting of members and friends was held at the Liberal Club, Bewsey-street, on Tuesday evening, when various proposals were submitted with the object of increasing the membership and other- wise putting the Club on an even sounder *and more satisfactory basis than in the past. The chair was taken by Mr. George Toulmin, and among those present, were Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Alderman Tinnion, Councillor Locker, Council- lor Wilkinson, Councillor Skelland, Messrs. A. Andrews (hon. secretary), L. Hughes (hon. treasurer), E. A. Sanders, and many prominent Junior Liberal workers.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY presented • a report on the steps which led up to the calling of the meeting.</p>
        <p>story." As to the past, the story of this coun- try was a marvellous story, but he asserted, without fear of question or contradiction, that from first to last, the conduct of every Brit- isher in the field—nay, of every member of the British Empire fighting for his country—in face of the present situation had been such as to add fresh laurels, to enhance The lustre of the race, and to raise to a still higher pitch of glory the traditions of Britain and the British Empire —the Empire upon which the pen of the future historian would delight to dwell. (Applause.) Speaking not as a party man, but as a Britisher, he contended that they had a right to feel proud of the sagacity, the true statesmanship, the reso- lution and the foresight with which the affairs of the Empire, especially during the present , European upheaval had been grappled with by the present Government. (Applause.) He had I referred to national traditions. There were noble Liberal traditions, too, splendidly upheld —yes, and he would say added to—by those at the head of affairs in this country, who were help- ing to make history to-day. (Applause.) At such a time, when all were under deep and per- manent obligation to those who piloted the ship of State through these troubled waters, it was unthinkable that any Liberal could slacken in his sympathy for the cause' or for any institution to which it looked for support. (Hear, hear.) Every day of his life he felt prouder that he belonged to the Liberal party—to a party that had been able to do such great things, not for those within its own ranks, not for one section of The State, but for the whole State. (Applause.) It was something of which every Liberal might, indeed, be proud that his party had been able to render such invaluable service as it had rendered in these days of stress, and was still rendering, through its .representatives in Lon- don—representatives who were carrying on their shoulders such a weight and burden of re- sponsibility, and who had so splendidly risen to a great occasion. (Applause.) Realising all that, he said that any little bit of spade- work that they, or he, could do to support the people who were doing so well for the country, ought to be done whole-heartedly. To their honour, be it known, many young men who, in the ordinary course, might be expected to join such a club as the Junior Liberal Club, were away serving their country—(applause)—and that made it rather difficult for institutions that depended for their support upon the young manhood to tide things over until they came back again, and, by their membership, once more enabled things to go full steam ahead. LIBERALISM'S SERVICE TO THE STATE.</p>
        <p>It would be a great pity, from the point of view of the future of Warrington Liberalism, if everything possible was not done to make the Junior Club a big success. (Afar, hear.) He could, of course, say a good deal from the point of view of the party politician, but, as a matter of fact, he did not intend to say a single word from that point of view.. Every one of them knew what to think of the last eight years of the party's record in the State, and they knew what the preparation made in those eight years of work was worth to the State in the present crisis, and where the country would have been but for that record of invdluable work. Any Liberal could say' a great deal about that if he liked, but, personally, he would rather not dwell on it that evening, because he did not want to trespass by so much' as an inch on the ground that he thought was very wisely barred at the present time—the ground of party politics. He recognised to the full the• admir- able work which the Junior Liberal Association had done since the Junior Club was formed in the town. Many most successful and useful meetings had been held under its auspices, and he looked forward to the time when that Junior Club would be one of the most prosperous institutions in Warrington. ESSENTIALS TO SUCCESSFUL CLUB-LIFE. A club, to be as successful as it ought to be, had to provide for other things besides politics. Young fellows, if they were patriotic and public- spirited, would take an active interest in politics, but at the same time they must realise that they did not live by politics alone. There were other sides to their lives, for the development and pleasant exercise of which the charming quarters in Horsemarket-street afforded admir- able facilities. (Hear, hear.) He had had a little bit of experience of clubs in his time, and he did not think he had ever heard higher 'praise given to any club than that it had about it all the charm, refinement and attraction of a private house. He had heard people apply that description to clubs of very high standing as being the best praise that they could bestow, Well, he never entered the headquarters of the Junior Association in Horsemarket-street without feeling they had about them just that same mark of distinction. (Hear, hear.) He thought the club quarters were delightful. Mr. Crosfield went on to sug- gest means by which the Junior Club could enhance its popularity, and increase its mem- bership. For instance, he said, they might make occasional use of the Memorial Hall for dances, conversaziones, whist drives, concerts, Ike,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>GOOD CLUB AND A HELP TO THE PARTY.</head>
        <p>A GOOD CLUB AND A HELP TO THE PARTY.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN then detailed passages in the brief history of the Junior Liberal Club in War- ringten, and told how Mr. Crosfield undertook to furnish the club premises in Hersemarket- street, and did the furnishing so thoroughly that it was doubtful whether there was a Junior Liberal Club in Lancashire that had better- appointed rooms. Not only did Mr. Crosfield furnish the rooms magnificently, but Mrs. John Crosfield, Mrs. Arthur Crosfield and Miss Truda Crosfield among them provided a billiard table, and in June, 1912, the club' was formally de- clared open. The Junior Liberals had done good work for the party in Warrington. (Hear, hear.) They had held a good many useful meet- ings, and in other ways they had been of ser- vice. (Hear, hear.) The time had scome, how- ever, when it was felt that a reorganisation of the Club was necessary, with a view to making it more successful and of more use to the, party with which they were proud to be allied. Mr. Crosfield had been good enough to suggest lines along which they might add to the up-to-dateness and attractiveness of the club. One suggestion was that the 'club might go in for a licence enabling the committee to sell alcoholic bever- ages, as well as tobacco and cigarettes., Another was that luncheons and suppers might be served at reasonable charges; a third, that it might be made into a select middle-class club, with a subscription of, say, 78. 6d.; a fourth, that the tables might be stocked with a careful selec- tion of weekly and monthly periodicals, as well as daily papers; and a fifth, that chess,' draughts, and domino tournaments be ar- ranged. They wanted to get young Liberals in greater numbers to realise what a great boon a well-managed and comfortable club could be; they wanted to build up an institution that would appeal to the hundreds of young fellows in Warrington, who were working away from their homes and who wanted a place of resort where they could spend sOme of their evenings pleasantly and profitably. They wanted also to make the Junior Club even a greater source of help and strength to Liberalism. And a good many of them had come to the conclusion that it was no use trying to run a club on anything else but club lines. :If the idea was to make a Y.M.C.A. of the Junior Liberal Club, let them make a Y.M.C.A. of it, but do not let them try to run it as a kind of half-way house between Y.M.C.A. and Liberal Club. If a fellow joined a club he naturally expected to participate in those amenities that a club was intended to provide. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD'S</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD'S</p>
        <p>A Great Thing To Be Brifish In These</p>
        <p>Days.</p>
        <p>Mr. CROSFIELD, who was cordially received on rising to speak, said that he was pleased to have the chance of addressing the Junior Liberals, and of saying that he, personally, felt much encouraged at seeing such an excellent gather- ing. lie felt the more encouraged and the more proud from the fact that it was within the knowledge of all of them that many men who, otherwise, would also have been there that night were away serving their country. (Applause.) When they came to think of the kind of service that Englishmen in their thousands were giving to their country just now, it made them feel prouder than ever of the fact that they were Englishmen. (Applause.) Every page they read in the daily Press, every account that came to them from abroad, intensified that feeling of pride in the heart of every Englishman. When he was invited to that meeting it occurred to him that it might be in season if he said a word or two about the national traditions that were being so magnificently maintained, and about the Liberal traditions that were, too, being splen- didly upheld, not in any narrow, any improper party spirit. At the present time there existed a truce in party warfare; and he did not desire to break any truce or to tear up any "scrap of paper." They were Englishmen, and were not given to tearing up "scraps of paper" to which they had put their signature; they stuck to their bargains. He might be pardoned a few words on the point ot national traditions.</p>
        <p>GERMANY'S INSUFFERABLE ARROGANCE Those of them who had travelled a bit in Europe would know that Englishmen had not been accustomed to going about in foreign parts wearing their courage on their sleeves, and affronting the people of other nationalities that' they came across with a kind of upstart insolence—with a puffed-up attitude, as if they belonged to a race of supermen. That, for a long time past, had been the insufferable atti- tude on the part of a nation—a nation he need not mention, for its name was in everybody's thoughts—that had come very rapidly to the front in the last fifty years. They would recall an old saying, oft quoted in England, "Sit a beggar on horseback, and he'll ride to the devil." He hardly liked to put it quite so bluntly as that, but still one could not help recognising the bearing that old saying had on what they had witnessed on the Continent—the rapid rise of Germany from the condition of a very poor nation to the condition of a very wealthy nation. He was sorry to have to say it, but truth compelled him to, that accom- panying that rise to prosperity was a species of purse-proud insolence that had been offensive to most of the other nations of Europe, and had ,led to the overbearing, overweening attitude which was to be discerned reflected in the whole course of the diplomacy wilich led up to the pre- sent war. That war was the culmination of Germany's amazing recklessness.. Truly, the people of -Europe were to-day living in tremen- dous times, and in the presence of the most stu- pendous conflict that had been witnessed in the whole history of the world. And Englishmen had, indeed, reason to be proud of the conduct of fellow Englishmen in this great crisis—proud of their modesty; their quiet courage and un- flinching determination; their cheerful endur- ance of hardship and all the dangers and risks of war, of the sacrifices that they had made and were making—alas in many cases, the greatest sacrifice that any man could make, viz., the sacrifice of his life. 'Twas a splendid spec- tacle; that spectacle of a courageous, self-sacri- ficing England.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>OUR WONDERFUL ISLAND STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR WONDERFUL ISLAND STORY.</p>
        <p>From the very cradle they, as Englishmen, had bass brought up to feel proud—very proud —if what was cal/T(1 tLeir wonderfu/ island</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>MRS. CROSFIELD'S HOPE.</head>
        <p>MRS. CROSFIELD'S HOPE.</p>
        <p>When she built that hall, Mrs. Crosfield hoped that all Warrington might, one way or another, benefit by the entertainments, &amp;c., that would take place in it, although, of course, the first place in her heart was always for the Liberal party. (Applause.) When she told him that the memorial to his father was to take the form of the noble building in which they were assembled, he told her, at once, that she could not possibly have built anything, or done anything which would have appealed more to his father or been so fitting a memOrial to him. (Hear, hear.) In conclusion, Mr. Crosfield ex- pressed the hope that the Junior Club would go on and prosper; that, indeed, it would be a humming success. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>ASSETS OF THE FUTURE.</head>
        <p>ASSETS OF THE FUTURE.</p>
        <p>Councillor LOCKER said that after having had a number of years on the committee of the Senior Club, he would be only too glad to offer the Juniors the benefit of his experience in their task of reorganisation. lie was certain that it was possible to make the Junior Club a big success, and he was full of enthusiasm for the ' Juniors, and was convinced that the Seniors should give them what help and advice they could, realising that it was to the young men that the party had to look for the future. Most difference of opinion seemed, from what he had heard, to centre round the tentative pro- posal to have an excise licence for the Junior Club. Well, they had had several controversies round the same point in the Senior Club, but he was glad to say that for the last ten years there had been no trouble in that respect what- ever. Everybody in the Senior Club had been thoroughly satisfied, the drinking facilities hav- ing been well and carefully regulated; anii he submitted to the consideration of all who had at heart the welfare of the young Liberals that they should not attempt to force upon them opinions which they did not conscientiously hold. It was not right that they should be dic- tated to as if they were members of a Young Men's Christian Association.</p>
        <p>PLACE OF THE CLUB IN POLITICS. Alderman Timor?, speaking of the value of clubs as adjuncts to political organisations, said he did not hesitate to say that had it not been for the Warrington Liberal Club in Bewsey- street there would have been little or no Liberal- inn Warringto4, Mt Liberal Club had</p>
        <p>VENO'S LIGHTNING -t COUGH CURE ' The Ideal family remedy. Contains no opium, morphine. paregoric. or 7 other harmful drug. Cures at all ages. C OUGHS,COLDS St INFLUENZA an i ee Veno's is the surest d pe d t cure for these winterill s, the best prso- tc:ction against more serious dangers.</p>
        <p>' HILDREN S COUGHS - •• Soon yield to Veno's—even Whooping cough. And there is no trouble in giving it, children simply love Venots. Other sizes 1/11 and .1/9, Large d 'rue: Trial 2 " .tipetcs, they are not Bottle "just as good as Foto,l s."</p>
        <p>been the home of local Liberalism. He knew that there were times when the Liberal Associa- tion did not think so—(laughter)--but he could soon prove to them that the fact was as he stated. The Liberal Club had been the most healthy part of the body politic in Warrington, and was still so. He could quite understand the desire to go in for an excise licence. He was a teetotaler himself, but he did not object to other fellows having what they liked—(hear, hear)—indeed, far from objecting he was a " Wshat'llyer"; he liked, when he met a friend to be in a position to say "What'll you have?" (Laughter.) He did not object if the friend called for a whisky, though he contented him- self with cold and comrortless lemonade. (Laughter.) They 'hit upon a happy compro- mise in the case of the Senior Club. They said: " Yon can have your drink in the bar; but in the billiard and reading rooms you will have no drink," the result lieing that their teetotal friends still had, at least, two rooms that they could use in the club without having their sus- ceptibilities offended,. Under proper manage- ment, he thought the inclusion or a bar among the amenities of a club was not objectionable, but he would be among the last to say "Have a bar and cards, and let every member have a licence to do just as he liked."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>PROUD OF THE JUNIOR ASSOCIATION AND CLUB. _</head>
        <p>PROUD OF THE JUNIOR ASSOCIATION AND CLUB. _</p>
        <p>_ On the other hand, he counselled that any club that had a bar and sanctioned the use of cards should see to it that both the drink and the cards were used in such a manner that even the most fastidious member could not find the slightest cause for objection. He was very proud of the Junior Liberal Association and Club, and he could not but feel that the Liberal- ism of Warrington in the future would be much benefited by a well regulated club, with a social side to it, because they must remember that young men especially did not always want to be absorbed in politics. They liked a little recrea- tion mixed with them.</p>
        <p>RESOLUTION CARRIED. Mr. P. H. BROWN thought it would be a calamity to local Liberalism if the Junior Club were allowed to languish, and said that the beautiful rooms they held in Horsemarket-street wee a credit to the generosity of the Crosfield family, and ought to be a standing memorial to their generosity. (Applause.) Mr. S. H. WALKER said that the Junior Club was put forward for the welfare of Liberalism in general in the town, and they ought to be able to make it successful, politically, apart from any other question.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. D. WARD urged all interested in the progress of Liberalism in the town to do all in their power to put the Junior Club on a new and even sounder footing.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN .then put to the meeting the question as to whetlif,r the club shall or shall not have a bar for the sale of alcoholic refresh- ments, and the overwhelming majority of those present voted in favour of providing such an adjunct. Only four voted against.</p>
        <p>THE OPPOSITION TO A LICENCE. Alderman TINNION remarked that it was a pity that the meeting could not have been unani- mous on the point. If he thought for one moment that the introduction of intoxicating liquors was to be made a leading line in the club, and was likely to prove a trap to any young man, he would have been the last.man in the world to have supported the proposal. Mr. CROSFIELD said that although he claimed to be a temperance reformer, he had always felt that they were not going to further true tem- perance by introducing a spirit of intolerance and by trying to force teetotalism down the throats of those who did not approve of it.</p>
        <p>Mr. PATRICK O'BRIEN explained that he was not a teetotaler who objected to another man drinking a glass of beer, but he felt that his scruples would not allow him to be the means of _passively promoting the drink.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. JOHNSON, who voted with the minority, said he was unalterably opposed to drinking and gambling. Some people seemed to think that because a man was a teetotaler and could not get his own way, he was not going to do his best for the cause of Liberalism. That was not so. He thought he could claim to have worked as hard for Liberalism as-any Wan in Warrington.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. PEACOM Jun., said he was not a teetotaler, but he voted against this proposal. He thought they might trust for success to a newly-constituted committee and body of officials. He would do his best to make the club a success.</p>
        <p>Mr. BROWN moved: "That a meeting be called for thO purpose of electing officers and re- orgavising the Junior Liberal Association and Club on the lines of the vote that has been taken to-night." This was carried.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was passed to the Chairman, on the motion of Alderman TINNION, seconded by Mr. CROSFIELD, who said they had had the right man in the chair at a gathering for the promotion of the interests of Junior LiberalisM (Applause.)</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was aceorded the ladi'es who had served refreshments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>To End at Last.</head>
        <p>To End at Last.</p>
        <p>The preacher was eloquent, the congregation patient and the discourse very long. A stranger entered and took a seat in a back pew. Pres- ently he whispered to the man at his side, evi- dently one of the old members:</p>
        <p>"How long has he been preaching?" "Thirty or forty years, I think," answered the elderly man. "I don't know exactly." "I'll stay then," said the stranger. urfe must be nearly done."</p>
      </div>
