        <p>A ..... .. . . ~-w7 -,,0r 2: 4 . . . ' • t ,- • " ~-I.;'-'" ' ~i'.4.7 .- Al` . '.._ , •.., d. / _d, . 4 '_l4%•ct de —_ • •</p>
        <p>THE TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>CONCERT AND COFFEE.</p>
        <p>Towards the end of last week the men of the South Laneashires quarteredj in the South were invited to a smoking concert, over which Sir John Bromhead Matthews, K.C., pre- sided. The programme was sustained by a local concert party of high repute, and during an interval the hundreds of guests were regaled with cake and coffee, thoughtfully provided by Lady Matthews. Before the merry company broke up the " Tommies " carried with acclama- tion a vote of thanks to the Chairman and her ladyship, and in reply Sir John expressed the pleasure it gave his wife and himself to be able to do a little to brighten the lot of their soldier friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>BOXING TOURNEYS.</head>
        <p>BOXING TOURNEYS.</p>
        <p>Following the lead set by their comrades quartered in the North, the men billeted down South are making arrangements to hold a series of boxing tournaments when they get back from leave. The first of the tournaments has been fixed for January 29th, and the proprietors of the largest picture hall in the place—a building capable of seating a thousand people—have kindly offered the use of their premises rent free. Two challenge cups will be put up for competition between picked men of the South Lancashire Brigade, and the keen rivalry be- tween the different regiments will assuredly be productive of some exhilarating boxing. Gold medals wilt be given to the rummers-up. A por- tiox of the proceeds is to be devoted te the Blue Cross Sooiety. The dimple" will he under the potroaego is Briganier-Geoteral soul the i *Amu* of the Wirath toweawkiro *riga.lo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 23, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 23, 1915.</p>
        <p>BIRTHS. MARRIAGES. DEATHS.</p>
        <p>lordeal for the waiting parties as brief as possible. True, as our correspondent says, the Registrar endeavours to make the arrangement work as well as pos- sible by fixing the order in which each interment shall take place according , .to the distance of ,each open grave from the church, but it is out of the power of anyone so to manage six or seven funerals, conducted by one clergyman, as to cause no dis.pomfort. We are sure the local clergy are anxious to meet the desires of their parishioners, and that they will be open to review this arrangement in the light of the , experience that time has brought:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>EMMA.</head>
        <p>EMMA.</p>
        <p>"On this bed Les a lad with blue eyes and soft yellow dowi. un his unshaved chin; his race is white as chalk, with a kind of misty pallor glistening with cold sweat. One bare arm is extended with the finger pointing upwards to the ceiling, the other is curved as if it were holding someone in a long embrace. The fingers are plucking, plucking, as though at an invisible rope, which is for ever escaping his grasp. His teeth are clenched—it is a case of tetanus—but through them there escapes a mournful and imperative cry that never ceases— Emma! Emma! Emma!'" . .</p>
        <p>From Professor J. H. Morgan's discription of a visit to a French hospital.</p>
        <p>Upon his bed of pitin he lies, A wounded lad, with features set, The gathering mist before his eyes, The death-dews on his forehead wet. About him, with an angel's grace, The gentle nurse on tiptoe moves, While through the silence of the place He calls and calls on one he loves.</p>
        <p>He sees the little village far Beyond the waters of the Rhine, Where all his heart's best treasures are. And where the soft sun used to shine Upon the cottage where the maid He worshipped sweetly came and went, And proudly at her feet he laid His love with all a king's content.</p>
        <p>Once more he sees her standing there And hears the distant waters run, Her sad young face more strangely fair Than in the happy autumns gone. From war's red hell, as life's last hours Slip slowly past, his spirit turns To that bright home amid the flowers And all his heart within him burns.</p>
        <p>One arm he pitifully lifts With clutching fingers, turning cold, To where the yellow lamplight shifts Upon the ceiling ere the gold Of morning floods the world again; The other in a fond embrace About the shadowy form in vain lie curves, and seeks her pitying face</p>
        <p>And through the night with anguished- voice Upon her name he calls and calls, And "Emma! Emma! Emma!" cries Aloud to the unanswering walls. Poor boy! her soft eyes seem to shine, ' Through Death's dark shade, as heretofore, Alas ! his fingers shall not twine Her golden tresses any more!</p>
        <p>The da'w'n creeps up, the room grows chill, The blue eyes slowly glaze and close, The outstretched fingers clutching still As forth the yearning spirit goes. The wounded, on their beds of pain, Turn as the long cry faints and dies, And watch the daylight creep again On wings of gold up azure skies.</p>
        <p>And Emma, in her home afar, Kneeled by her soft white couch and prayed And wondered at this hideous war Which cruel kings and kaisers made; And, fondly dreaming of her love, She reached out answering arms and slept. While God from His blue heaven above Looked at His suffering world and"wept.</p>
        <p>ARTHUR BENNETT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>AMBASSADORS OF COMMERCE,</head>
        <p>AMBASSADORS OF COMMERCE,</p>
        <p>Warrington Commercials Hold Their Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the Warring- ton and-District Branch of the United King- dom Commercial Travellers' Association was held at the Lion Hotel on Saturday evening. Mr. P. O'Brien presided over a good attend- ance of members, which included Mr. W. P. Ashton (vice-chairman and hon. treasurer), Mr. T. Percival (hon. secretaa), and Mr. W. L. Walker (assistant hon. secretary).</p>
        <p>THREE MEMBERS WITH THE COLOURS.</p>
        <p>The Hon. Secretary, in presenting his annual report, spoke of the splendid manner in which members of the executive had attended the meetings during the year, but said he would like to see the general members take more in- terest in the quarterly meetings. At least three of the members had joined the colours, viz., Mr. A. Spinks, Mr. T. Richardson, and Mr. E. Devlin, and two sons of Mi..- O'Brien were serving with the Fleet under Admiral Jellicoe, and a son of Mr. H. L. Taylor had joined the Lancashire Hussars.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>SATISFACtORY BALANCE-SHEET.</head>
        <p>A SATISFACtORY BALANCE-SHEET.</p>
        <p>'The Hon. Treasurer presented the balance- sheet, which was considered very satisfactory. although there was a small (leficlron the general account. The Unemployment Fund and the Local Benevolent Fund were shown to have sub- stantial amounts in hand. During the year the branch had contributed five guineas to the Prince of Wales' Fund, and it was hoped to make a donation shortly to the Local War Re- lief Funds, as the result of a whist drive and dance, to be held on the 28th inst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Peter Rylands was re-elected presi- dent for the year, and the Vice-Presidents elected were: Theq Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), Sir Gilberk Greenall, Bart., Councillor J. T. Locker, and Messrs. H. R. Armitage, A. E. Boardman, A. H. Crosfield, P. Caldwell, John Dolan, F. A. Frost, J. Fulton-Smith, Henry Greenall, John Lee, W. Milner, J. A. Ransome, W. Sloane, J. Reid Walker, and W. Webster.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. P. Ashton was aleded chairman, and on taking the chair presented Mr. O'Brien (the retiring chairman) with a gold badge as a memento of his year of office. Mr. O'Brien was also elected a hon. vice-president of the branch.</p>
        <p>There were three candidates for the office of Via-Chairman, viz., Mr. H. Walsh, Mr. W. IL Owen and Mr. D. J. Price.</p>
        <p>On a vote being taken, Mr. Walsh was elected after a close contest.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. P. Ashton was again elected hon. treasurer; Mr. T. Percival, hon. secretary; Mr. W. L. Walker, hon. assistant secretary; Mr. D. J. Price and Mr. A. 0. Hilditch, auditors; and Mr. T. S. Steel, hon. solicitor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>NEW EXECUTIVE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>THE NEW EXECUTIVE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The following were elected to form the Execu- tive Committee: Messrs. A. J. Allen, G. Box, W. Dakin, W. G. Hayes, A. 0. Hilditch, Lunt, 11. Mercer, P. O'Brien, W. H. Owen, D. J. Price, D. Slater, B. Smith, J. H. Singleton, H. L. Taylor and J. A. Warburton. A vote of thanks to the retiring officers was passed, on the motion of Mr. Walsh, seconded by Mr. W. G. Hayes, Mr. O'Brien responding'.</p>
        <p>"Kindness which is not boundless does iot deserre the name." ,</p>
        <p>"The more thou loyest thyself, the more art then thine own enemy."</p>
