      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>-' AL;</head>
        <p>-' AL;</p>
        <p>THE SIN OF TREATING SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>What Should Be Done?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>SUGGESTIONS EY VARIOUS CORRESPONDEITI S.</head>
        <p>SUGGESTIONS EY VARIOUS CORRESPONDEITI S.</p>
        <p>We have received a number of comments upon the coma to which we, last week,' drew attention in our leading article. It will be recalled that two military guards at Walton Bridge were fined for assaulting a police-con- stable, and that it was stated in Court that the men had been given whisky and beer by some unknown persons. _ _</p>
        <p>The following are the letters that have so far come to hand :</p>
        <p>eternally as blots. At each diet of worship led my congregation in prayer for the score of young men who have gone from our circle of Christian life in response to the national call, as well as for all their comrades at home, on the wave, and in Europe, that they may be preserved from all that stains body and soul, and we are compelled to look on while our prayers are made of non-effect. Russia, regarded by many of us as little more than half-civilised, has been quick to see in which direction her advantage lay (to give to her action its lowest value) and has grasped the nettle with apparently the reputed result. Neither our intelligence nor our patriotic spirit has, as yet, influenced us sufficiently to touch it with the finger-tip, though the other way would undoubtedly .-bring the least regrets in the long run, but, so point the indices, we be not able.</p>
        <p>In. Hebrew legislature it was provided that when a person wished to pass as a patriot and gratify the spirit of selfishness at one and the same time, so bringing, as he invariably did, and does, some measure of disaster on the fight- ing forces, they spared no pains to unearth him; they searched through families, clans, tribes, yea, even through the whole nation, and re- fused to give up the search until they had found him, for precisely the same reason that an engineer refuses to seal up a c6sspool, and just as a doctor finds it necessary, if a plague is to be stayed, that every germ should be destroyed, so they recognised in such a one— be he a Saul or an Achan—a " troubler," and in the interests of the community he was re- moved from his place. It seems a pity when a town so fortunately placed in many respects as Warrington is, lacks moral decision. It is as much the sin of not doing right as of doing wrong that will be judged; nay, that is being judged; and the issues on the Continent will be the aggregated result of the one as much as of the other, aria if we were to cease to refrain from doing right, it would help many not to do wrong. I am surprised that national and civic authorities show themselves unprepared to deal with the question of the sale of "drink" to any extent. I regret that the various branches of the Chris- tian Church are unprepared to speak and act decidedly, even if separately, but I am afraid that not the least measure of the blame must rest with the Free-Churches, and the men who ought to be inside of them, behind which and whom stand- traditions—the position, I am afraid, of too great a number of their ideals also—which, if clothed upon to-day, would give Warrington a freer and sweeter life.—Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>W. REID,</p>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>RECTOR AND THE REAL TRANSGRESSORS.</head>
        <p>THE RECTOR AND THE REAL TRANSGRESSORS.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—My attention has been .called to the leading article in your paper of Saturday last on "The Sin of Treating Soldiers." I entirely agree with it. .</p>
        <p>tlii,nk it is a shame and disgrace to us as a community that any member of it should be capable of such an act as that of giving intoxi- cating drink to a soldier on duty, and I hope that the man and woman who have so trans- gressed will have the courage to atone for their fault by the public confession of their names and addresses.—Yours, &amp;c., FREDERIC W. WILLIS. The Rectory, Warrington, January 20th, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>NEED OF THE HOUR OF</head>
        <p>THE NEED OF THE HOUR OF</p>
        <p>CRISIS.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Allow me to thank you for the leading article in "The Examiner" list Saturday. You speak in words not a whit too strong on a matter of critical importance. It is well that attention should be drawn to the most serious case of the "treating" of the sentries at Wal- ton Bridge, and it is well that you should point out that this case ig typical of the widespread and calamitous temptation to intemperance 4hich menace t the men who have joined the colours. .</p>
        <p>Some of Us have done all that lies in our power to awaken public opinion on this mat- ter. Some of us feel .that the Government itself might have dealt 'much more stringently with the question. The earlier closing of pub- lic-houses might have been made compulsory throughout the community without violent opposition. Restriction of a more far-reaching rtature still might well havb been attempted. I am not concerned for the moment with what may. constitute a reasonable demand with re- gard to the liquor traffic in normal times; I am concerned with what may 'be an absolute neces- sity in an hour of. crisis. The plain fact is that many men who enlisted with the best intentions have succumbed, long before they have come within sound of shot or shell, to the enemy within our own gates with which we refuse to deal. There are many fathers and mothers who will not readily forgive the fact that, in some quarteA, we have refused to abate by one iota the temptations in the way of the lads they have spared in our houn of need.</p>
        <p>Here in Warrington we have responded well in many ways to the call of our country. Great meetings have been held and eloquent speeches have been made; large numbers have enrolled themselves bravely and readily for active ser- vice; much sacrifice in lnoney and gifts, in time and labour, is being honourably made by men and women of all parties and classes in the community. The truth remains that what we construct with one hand we destroy with the other. We have btill to strike the first blow at an enemy more destructive than famine or pestilence or war.—Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>T. T. JAMES Wycliffe Congregational Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>SURE AND EFFECTIVE METHOD.</head>
        <p>A SURE AND EFFECTIVE METHOD.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—As an Army, our soldiers, regulars and non-regulars, are a splendid lot of men—a finer set of fellows cannot be produced by any country. The best of men, however, can be tempted—some more readily than others. Our</p>
        <p>soldiers are no exception; they, perhaps, more easily succumb than tho ordinary civilian. The victim is more often sinned against than sinning, as in the case under discussion, for the two delinquents, we are assured, had an absolutely clean record until this unhappy affair.</p>
        <p>Those who tempt our brave, self-sacrificing soldiers, who persist in giving them drink, are both wicked and cruel. They may shout "God save the King" for all they are worth, and wave a patriotic flag, from time to time, but they are neither loyalists nor patriots—they are friends of the enemy. If hands can be laid on these tempters and seducers they should be treated as traitors. "The Examiner's" lead- ing article of the 16th, on the Walton Bridge affair, is very much ad rem throughout; it is none too strong; indeed, I think it hardly strong enough.</p>
        <p>Cases of this kind, in which men appointed to important duty are supplied with drink, reveal the danger we are in through our un- happy drinking customs, due to the exaltation of the imaginary, bogus virtues of alcoholic drink. What is to prevent the giving of drugged liquor by a supposed friend, but, in reality, by a bona-fide enemy? s At a time when espionage is rife and traitors abound in our midst, when we know not whom to trust, can we be too careful, too watchful? A sure and effective method would be tem- porarily to suspend the drink traffic during the war time. What has been done, and so well done, in Russia .can be as readily accomplished in Great Britain. The difficulties in this country cannot be greater than the difficulties which have been faced and overcome in Russia. —Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>A. M. MITCHELL. The Vicarage, Burtonwood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>REV. W. T. JAYE AND</head>
        <p>THE REV. W. T. JAYE AND</p>
        <p>RESPONSIBILITX,</p>
        <p>To the Editoe 61 "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—l think your leading article in last Sat, urday's `f Examiner" splendid, I subscribe, to every word, and pray that much good may be done by your outspokenness. Is it WA possible to identify the "beer woman and the virhisicy man" who brought disgrace upon two lOjrisl soldiers? You well remark that "they are, by far, the greatest sinners." They richly deserve punishment for their , contemptible behaviour. During the past five months I have been greatly pained to see so much drunkenness and rowdy- ism: amongst our soldiers. The responsibility rests upon those mean men who, to quote from "Punch," take the soldiers round "from bar to bar, and Make him drunk, for England's sake."</p>
        <p>I, for one, thank you for your article.— Yours, &amp;c.„ •</p>
        <p>W. T. JAYE. Emmanuel -Church, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>04 WE LACK MORAL DECISION?</head>
        <p>04 WE LACK MORAL DECISION?</p>
        <p>To the Editor of ',The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—The unfortunate affair to which you make reference in your leading article last week is perhaps best thought of as an index to our conditions of life. Another one is seen in the attendances of Free Churchmen at the meetings sailed for prayer in tho first eomplete week of the year. If they lead to amendment they wilt YellOMe7 • Otell Whit* hkey themselves Aft*</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>RESTRICTION ACT SHOULD BE</head>
        <p>RESTRICTION ACT SHOULD BE</p>
        <p>HE-CONSIDERED.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of ',The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your leader dealing with the treating of soldiers certainly calls attention to a serious state of affairs. The temperance workers who have for a long time tried to teach that alco- holic drink interferes with efficiency have been called fanatics, &amp;c., but I hope that, coming from your pen, it will do some good at this national crisis, and also bear fruit afterwards.</p>
        <p>It is nothing short of a national disgrace that our Government have replied to questions in the House of Commons, that, owing to an agreed measure not being possible, they could not promise to do more tlfan had been done. Where is the patriotism of the Trade? It is changed, I am afraid, by the spelling,— P-r-o-f-i-t-s. Certainly it commences with the "</p>
        <p>What surprises many citizens in this town is that so many cases of drunkenness get into the streets, and some of them into the Police Courts, and yet the drink sellers are so seldom brought to answer any charge. In your issues of this year there have been reported cases each week, of members of H.M. Forces, naval and military, before the Bench for drunkenness. In your issue of January 2nd the Chief Con- stable, in dealing with one case, is reported as saying: "The local drunkenness records were going up by leaps and bounds." I notice that he was dealing with a " stranger " case, but, even so, the urgent call of our naval and Mili- tary authorities ought to be listened to, and I hope the authorities of our/town and district will again consider the Restriction Act. If the said Act was put into force, it would be the best agency to teach the people the sin of treating our soldiers and alga, the men of our Navy.—Yours, &amp;c., BENZ T. PARKES. lion. Secretary Warrington and District Temperance League.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>HARM DONE BY "TREATING" SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>HARM DONE BY "TREATING" SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>At Old-street Polioe Court, yesterday, Henry Wilton Robinson, '29, was charged as an absen- tee from the Duke of Cornwall's Light Infantry at Witley Camp. The prisoner, who wore the three stripes of a lance-sergeant, in defence, said he had been home On furlough and had learned that since he enlisted in August one brother had lost his life in Belgium, another in the wreck of the Formidable, and a third had died from illness at home. On his way to Waterloo Station on Sunday he met some friends, who condoled with him and gave him several drinks, causing him to miss, his train. He was exceedingly sorry for what he had done as his lapse probably meant the loss of his promotion. The magistrate said that if people only knew the harm they caused by "standing drinks" to soldiers they would never do it. He remanded the prisoner to await an escort.— "The Times" for Friday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>GROSFIELD'S PROFITS.</head>
        <p>GROSFIELD'S PROFITS.</p>
        <p>New Building Additions to be Made.</p>
        <p>INCREASED BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>lln their report on the balance sheet to the 30th November, 1914, the directors of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., state:— "The Board have pleasure in reporting to the eligreheitiore a large increase. in the busi.;- ness and prat's of the company. These in- creases, while WOO prOliOnnced up to the end of July, have continued thtoughout the year, which, in view of the • distUfliance to trade caused by the outbreak of •arar, is particularly satisfactory. The company has benefited by fewer prices of materials, apd its policy of cavrying large stocks has been of great assist- ance in overcoming the difficulties with regard to supplies occasioned by the war. "Since July the oil departments have been worked' &amp; &amp;lir fullest extent and additions to those plants. are .now being made. In order to obtain rally/ay, facilities on the company's pro- perty on the Cheshire side of the River Mersey —where the pregent arrangements for handling' a large and incriOing tonnage are inadequate— a contract has 13(4* placed for a Transporter i Bridge over that Ater, which it is hoped will be completed during .next summer. The accom- modation provided in'the works for social pur- poses, such As dining: And dressing rooms, gymnasium, &amp;c., being inadequate and required for manufacturing puhioses, a substantial building combining , those features is being erected on property acquired .by the company in Baxter and PowYs-streets, adjoining other property of the company. . "The direetom recommend that of the bal- ance of profit after payment of dividend on the pretertataa wag ardinary aaPitat .821.01111 be pi/teed be reserve. 490,0•41 to depreelatios, and 131• Th catrfai ferweri bo Ike milbit aißsOinkt." ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>HELP THE WOUNDED!</head>
        <p>HELP THE WOUNDED!</p>
        <p>Our columns have from week to week borne eloquent testimony to the many acts of patriotism that have been done in the way of helping those who are away fighting our battLes. Most people who have money have given liberally; most people who have no surptus money have given their services. Some have given both money and services. In doing all they can in any way they can the people are only discharging their bare duty to the land that provides them with the money or the means of subsistence, a little of which they are now asked to return. But the list of givers and workers when studied closely shows some nasty-looking gaps. There are wealthy people who stick to their wealth, and there are others who give grudgingly, or as the result bf appeals made to them. There are some who let anyone work who will so long as they may get through easily and unnoticed. These people are more despicable than the young men of fighting age who prefer to stay at home and allow someone else to go forth to defend that home.</p>
        <p>The response locally to the appeal that has been, made for funds for the establishment of a St. John Ambulance Brigade Hospital in France has been meagre in the extreme. The splendid voluntary organisation, the St. John Ambulance Brigade, has supplied 8,000 men for the military and naval hospitals at home and abroad. No. 4 District, to which Warrington belorlgs, has contributed no fewer than 2,600 of this total. The WarriVon Headquar- ters Division and the drosfleld Division have sent every available man—all volunteers who have been trained at their own expense. 'Others are in course of training. But the Brigade wish to add to their grand contribution of personal service a much-needed base for 520 beds, and £50,000 is re- quired. That, in the wholç of Eng- land, should be an easy surft to raise, and it will doubtless be raised; but in Warrington monetary help has not been forthcoming in anything like proper proportion to the personal ser- vice that is being given. Other divi- sions in No. 4 District have done extremely well. Rochdale has supplied £5OO, Crewe £300,. Fleetwood £2OO, and so on. Warrington has not yet raised £4O, and a large part .of this has been given from the Ambulance Brigade funds. The local division is seriously handicapped by the death of Chief Superintendent WARING, who was a past-master at, raising funds for ambulance work, but the preSent urgent need ought to make personal appeals quite unnecessary, and we hope that Warringtenians, now they know of the need,_ will hasten to meet it.</p>
        <p>" WAITING " IN THE CEMETERY.</p>
        <p>It is, we think, high time that some -reform was effected in the method of interment at the Warrington Cemetery. Under an arrangement by which one Church of England clergyman alter- nately takes duty for the whole of his colleagues in town, it is sometimes necessary for sorrowing relatives to wait a considerable time before their turn comes round, and, in the very wet weather we have experienced this winter, the system has been exceed- ingly trying. Burial grounds, at the best, are chilly places, and only too often mourners have reduced their vitality and powers of resistance by long watches and continuous labours at the bedside—so that funerals have the bad reputation of making other funerals. A correspondent calls our attention to the matter in a letter as follows : Some time ago the Church of England clergy came to an " arrangement " regarding their duty at the Cemetery, and though this may be satisfactory to them, it is not equally so to the public, particularly during inclement weather. It is quite true that no minister ever refuses to attend if requested, but why should it be neces- sary to ask a clergyman to do what is undoubt- edly his duty—viz., to bury the dead of his own parish? The method of the Registrar leaves nothing to be desired, but this arrangement of the Church of England clergy a lot.</p>
        <p>It is scarcely dignified, and it cannot be comfortable, for a clergyman who happens to be on duty for a particular day fa be running about from grate to grave in Ike wet ia ardor to make Ike,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Id -,"%',* I -it' t , -r:;_,,: 4:-• --, ~ `, I ' -!:,;;;;,,,, .- -; s-,1 •t , – ' –</head>
        <p>Id -,"%',* I -it' t , -r:;_,,: 4:-• --, ~ `, I ' -!:,;;;;,,,, .- -; s-,1 •t , - ' -</p>
        <p>OUR DEBT TO BELGIUM.</p>
        <p>Are We Doing Our Duty As a Ohristiax Nation?</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—We are in danger, as time goes on, of getting too familiar with the thought of the measureless national calamity which has over- taken Belgium. Let us be careful that this familiarity does not result in a slackening of our practical sympathy and a relaxation of our effort in the discharge of what is a national duty, as well as a Christian privilege. When the German legions, like a horde of barbarians, ravaged, plundered and destroyed the country they. had sworn to protect, they left a people to whom long-continued help and consolation will have to be given. The tragic fate that over- whelmed thi, unoffending nation continues to appeal to us in her appalling and undescrib- able sorrow.</p>
        <p>So far England has found hospitality for about 90,000 destitute Belgians. The little country of Holland, with a population of about 5,000,000, has received about 1,000,000. To feed and house such a number is a tremendous task. No doubt this original number has been con- siderably diminished by those who have re- turned to Belgium. To such the love of home and their desire to safeguard what little is left has proved stronger than their fear and dis- like to living under German rule. Still, after allowance for returns, Holland is still carry- ing a weight much beyond her strength.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>RE-BUILDING SCHEME.</head>
        <p>RE-BUILDING SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Mr. Percy Alden, M.P., who has three times visited Holland at the instance of the Local Government Board, says:— " The Dutch cannot provide anything more than bare shelter and food for these people. The food consists of rye bread and margarine twice a day, with soup about four times a week at noon. The Dutch are doing all that lies in their power to help the children. In those towns where the school accommodatidn is limited it has not been found possible to place any large proportion' of the Belgian children under instruction, but in some the Dutch have surrendered their own schools for half the day, and others have closed their kindergarten schools and handed them over to the Belgian Relief Committee. A.They, have also found warm garments for the nildren for the winter."</p>
        <p>The British Golrnment have now made arrangements for the conveyance of some 2,500 refugees a week to England from Holland. All the accoritmodation offered has been made use of, and new homes must be provided for this weekly influx. Mr. Alden makes a practical suggestion, and proposes that it should be applied in Holland. It is that the men, ars many of the skilled workmen as possible, should be set to work in the, construction of wooden houses, built in sections, so that when the time comes W return these might be taken to pieces and put up again in Belgium to house the'people while permanent buildings are being erected. To re-build the shattered towns, vil- lages and farms in Belgium will take a con- siderable time. Could not this plan be carried out in England? If all were built to one pattern, transporting by water would not be costly. But whether this is practical or not the main question which locally we should ask ourselves is whether we are doing what we ought, and doing what we can? It is not alone the question of what is called our debt of honour to the Belgian people, it is rather what is our duty as a Christian nation to these home- less people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>SHORT-LIVED SYMPATHY.</head>
        <p>SHORT-LIVED SYMPATHY.</p>
        <p>I am astonished to find amongst some that their sympathy for the Belgians has been very short lived, and is of such an. unstable charac- ter that paltry discouragements have quenched their zeal. The Belgian people have . not turned out to be all angels. Did anyone think they would? Some of them are not evincing-thir constanegratitude which seems to be a neces- sary condition required by some, if the flow of their charity and discharge of their duty is to continue. A sympathy and a recognition of a duty dependent on external stimulus is of very little use. Surely the people of Warrington have a greater staying power than this indi- cates, and their continuance of well-doing does not depend on externals.</p>
        <p>Of this there is no question, that however well Warrington may have done in connection with recruiting, War Funds, and hospital accommodation, in regard to the Belgians we have done poorly. Surely it is possible for. a town of our population, with industries on the whole busy and prosperous, to find homes for more than forty-two homeless Belgians. I do not know whether the committee claim that they have done all that is possible to secure the co-operation of the Warrington people in this much-needed department of service. I wonder if the committee itself is sufficiently representative to enlist the co-operation of all sections of the community. It is doubtful if the homes already established are being worked to the best advantage. Could not accommodation elsewhere be found for those families, the heads of which are earning ample to provide for the family, and ought not to be dependent on charity? Could these net be moved into cot- tages or lodgings and thereby relieve the expenses of maintenance, and make room for other Bel- gians who 'cannot at present sustain them- selves? Even if some of the furniture haa to be hired, this charge would net be great. The ladies and gentlemen forming the committee have no doubt given a great deal of time and energy to their work, but the results so far achieved have scarcely been commensurate with the effort expended.</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER. Penketh, January 20, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WAR ITEMS.</head>
        <p>WAR ITEMS.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF .FOOTBALLER-TERRITORiAL.</p>
        <p>A well-known local footballer-Territorial has passed away at Tunbridge Wells in the person. of Private J. Rigby, who was formerly the cap- tain of the Warrington Excelsior Football Club, which last year won the championship of the Second Division of the Warrington and Dis. tricp Football League. He was very popular wit);lh.g plaevers, and was highly respected by all who 1.-tew hint.—The funeral took place at Warrington on ViednesdaY•</p>
        <p>BURTONWOOD BOYS Flik. ?NE FRONT, The number of young meii i'iSin Burtonwood who have joined the colours stiq i 6 utbietik of the war now stands at nearli 4), quite, considerable number having enliste slii'6o. OA beginning of the New Year. ,</p>
