        <p>SafurdaY, 9 14 94. Please Note'.— Watson attends on Monday' and Tuesday from 1 , to 4130; Wednesday, li to i-30; Thurmday, 11 'to' 11; • Friday and Saturday, lit. 5-30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>What "The Force" Costs.</p>
        <p>During last year the total cost of the police service of the town was £10,528 2s. 3d., as com- pared with .29,725 Os. Bd. the year before.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wynne Jones As Recruiting • Speaker. Mr. Wynne Jones, chairman of the Progres- sive Labour League, has recently addressed a series of recruiting meetings in Wales, along with Mr. W. Crooks, M.P.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>More Cases of Scarlet Fever.</head>
        <p>More Cases of Scarlet Fever.</p>
        <p>Twenty-four fresh cases of scarlet fever were muitified during the week ended January 16th, irad are now being treated, along with 66 cases previously reported, in the Borough Hospital, Where also are 22 phthisicat patients, ' four diphtheria cases, and two cases of enteric fever.</p>
        <p>Recent Weather Colder. Meteorological records taken by the Medical Officer of Health during the week ended Janu- ary 16th show that the mean temperature dur- ing that period was four degrees lower than in the preceding seven days.. The coldest day during the period under review was the 16th inst., when only 30 degrees were registered on the glass, the warmest day being the 13th, when the temperature, touched 56 tdegrees.</p>
        <p>Shorthand Writers' Association. The members of the Warrington Shorthand Writers' Association spent a highly successful evening, from the point of view of hign speed writing, on Friday of last week. The reader for the evening, instead of taking the usual</p>
        <p>commercial and political matter, diet itei SOY- ' eral passages from Macaulay's 'Essayß, hich gave the members an opportunity a proving their phonographic skill. Several a the men- hers had little oreno difficulty in reading back passages read out at 140 words per minute..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>New Mission and Clergyman:</head>
        <p>New Mission and Clergyman:</p>
        <p>A large attendance marked the Parish 'Church parochial tea, held on Thursday evening in the Fairfield Schools. _ _ _</p>
        <p>After an excellent repast had been partaken, the Rector (the Rev. Canon Willis) gave a brief address, in the course of which he referred to the need of a fourth assistant clergyman and a third mission church. The Church Council, he said, had agreed to contribute £l5O per annum to pay the stipend—Xloo from the offertory and ~t5O from the parochial sale.</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Church Mothers' Union. The annual -social gathering .in connection with the above was held in the Institute on Monday evening, when a good number attended and thoroughly enjoyed the entertainment arranged by Mrs. A. Gandy and Mrs. W. Ather- ton. , The Incumbent (the Rev. W. T. Jaye) presided. The following friends gave vocal and musical items, which received the hearty ap- preciation of one and all :—The Misses Cook, Whitmore and .Pollitt, and Messrs. W. Ray, G. Lloyd, T. Hughes and A. Davis. The following little folk also contributed to the programme: The Misses M. Whiteley, E. Landor, K. Bibby and P. Jaye, and .Masters J. McEiven and J. Thompson. Miss Pollitt very kindly acted as accompanist throughout the evening. During the interval refreshments were served by lady workers.</p>
        <p>Town Council Photographs., This week there has been on. view in the window Of Mr. Birtles, Sankey-street, a large picture, framed in oak, consisting of the photo- graphs of the members of the Town Council and the principal officials of the Corporation who IVere serving the town during 1914, the first year of the present Mayor's occupancy of the civic 'chair. They are fifty-two portraits-36 members of the Council with two who died—Alderman Forshaw and Councillor T. Birtles—and the offi- cials. The Mayor (Alderman Peacock) occu- pies the centre place in a gold mount, and round • him are grouped the aldermen, the councillors and others being tastefully arranged on either side. The picture will be hung in. the Town Hall to accompany others of a simi- lar kind. which were presented by the late Mr. T. Birtles at intervals of ten years. It will, as l*ual, be a gift to the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>Sunday's Pleasant Afternoon service at Wycliffe Church was very well attended. The Rev. H. Ogden gave the address, taking as his text "Forgetting the things past and pressing forward to the things before us." It was, he said, difficult for most of them to forget all the wrongs and slights of the past, all the real and imaginary evils done, bat it would be wise, as well .as helpful, for them to. cultivate a, disposi-. tion of forgiveness and forgetfulness in this respect, having ever before them the great ideals of Christ—Faith, Hope and Charity. The Rev. T. T. James was in the chair, and 311.- Wm. Rutter rendered two cdrnet solos—" When the swallows homeward fly" and "The freed bird." Mr. Payton presided at the organ. At the close the Chairman expressed thanks to the speaker, the soloist 'and the organist, and heartily welcomed a new member.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's Centenary.</p>
        <p>• A beautiful brochure has just been issued by Messrs. 'Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., giving a short account of the manufacture of soap and the conditions of labour under which it is car- ried on. There are a number of extraordinarily good photographs of the various departments of Messrs. Crosfield's Works, and also several re- lating to the comparatively, recent visit of the King and Queen to the factory. In, a brief in- troduction mention is made of the admirable position of the works, which was chosen by Mr. I Joseph Crosfield (grandfather of the present I chairman and deputy-chairman), "who founded the firm in 1815 The growth of the firm's business is shown by the fact that whilst 30 years ago there were less than 250 employes on the books, there are now about 3,000, and during the same period the sales of finished goods have increased from 10,000 to over 100,000 - tons per annum." - •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>N.S.P.C.C. League of Pity.</head>
        <p>N.S.P.C.C. League of Pity.</p>
        <p>In aid of the Children's League of Pity in connection with the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, a cafe chantant was held in the Parish Room, Latch- ford, on Saturday. Excellent arrangements had 'been made by the Honorary Secretary of the League, Miss Broadbent, along with Mrs. Tickell, who were ably assisted by members of the Ladies' Committee. Members of the League, with soldiers from Raddon Court and the St. Ann's Day School childriAn, trained by Mr. W. H. Rigby, combined to make the entertainment most enjoyable and a substantial tea was served during an interval.. Miss Gornall and Miss Lockerbie delighted the audience with violin solos and the dances by the Misses Gornall were graceful and artistic. Several entertaining items were also creditably rendered by the Misses 80yd.., Wilkinson, Hutton, Grocott and Hewitt, Mr. R. Gornall, Privates Froggett, Boodle, and Turton, Rifleman Buss, and Mr. and Mrs. Sephton. The costume songs by Mr. Rigby's party were delightfully pretty and in- eluded: "Tell me, pretty maiden," "Japanese butterfly land," "Shade of the palm," "Moon- light," and "I'll telephone for Santa Claus," .and the tableau "National Anthems."• The .cake stall, to which contributions were invited, -was in the charge of the Misses Ethel Webb And L. Dunne.—The proceeds amounted to about ..t5.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Local Leather Trade.</head>
        <p>Local Leather Trade.</p>
        <p>"The demand for most classes of leather was very active last week," says the Warrington correspondent of "The Leather Trades Review." " Suitable lines moved freely, and ti satisfactory number of inquiries were received. Altogether the output was easily maintained at full figures. Trade is now much more evenly balanced than it was a few months ago, for while the demand thas grown, the production has also increased, :And is now equal to most of the calls made upon it. A shortage still exists in certain lines. particularly in shoulders and heavy bellies, aim prices of these sorts are no means stationary. There is very little of either on the spot, end .only small lots offered forward. As to prices senerally, there is nothing new to report. The market is very strong, and values are easily xpaintained, it no advances are quoted, except for special lines. An indication of the general belief in the stability of current quotations is the readiness with which buyers will book for- ward. Supplies of raw goods are fairly plenti- ful, although prices are high and constantly advancing; for instance, South Americans are s.bout 3d. higher than they were three months ago. Deliveries of tanning extracts Ire coming through in better volume. than was anticipated. America sending over large quantities, and while this is not equal in quality to the bebt Erench chestnut, it is a- fairly efficient substi- tute. The shipping congestion gives rise to some trouble, as it is impossible to determine vaien goods will be delivered after a vesiel has orived in cloak. A week sr me-e may elapse *fore it is unloaded, semi when the geode are es the quay labour is se swab that it nay be ;Mau firratett tit 7 gait- Ire !OWL"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Is It Your Estate?</head>
        <p>Is It Your Estate?</p>
        <p>The Torn Clerk has received an inquiry from Chicago as to the relatives of John Southwell, who was born in Warrington in 1830, and who has died in U.S.A., leaving "valuable real estate in County Ford, Illinois."</p>
        <p>Fewer " Drunks " During War. During the last five months of 1914 (the war months), says the Chief Constable of Warring- ton in his annual report, 66 fewer persons were proceeded against for drunkenness than in the corresponding period of the previous year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>" Come7to-Church "_Campaign.</head>
        <p>The " Come7to-Church "_Campaign.</p>
        <p>'A "Come to Church" Campaign meetixg. will be held in the Emmanuel Church, Bold-street, to-morrow, when the Right Rev. Dr. P. X. Eldridge (Presiding Bishop of the Reformed Episcopal Church) will occupy the pulpit both morning and _evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>To-morrow's _Adult School Rally.'</head>
        <p>To-morrow's _Adult School Rally.'</p>
        <p>An Adult School Rally will take place to- morrow afternoon at three o'clock in the Park Picture Palace, 'Sankey-street. The chair will be taken by the Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock), and the speakers will be Mr. David P. Boyd, of Manchester, and Mr. William Grimshaw, of Blackburn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Dr. Wilson's War Lecture.</head>
        <p>Dr. Wilson's War Lecture.</p>
        <p>At the St. Mary's Schools on Tuesday evening a large audience assembled to hear Dr. Wilson (of Liverpool, brother .to the Rev. Father Neville Wilson) lecture upon the war. A series of excellent limelight illustrations were thrown upon the screen, Father Austen Hind operating the lantern.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Vital Statistics.</head>
        <p>Vital Statistics.</p>
        <p>During the week ended January 16th, 38 births (20 males, 18 females) and 32 deaths (16 males, 16 females) were registered in Warring- ton, as against 41 births and 23 deaths in the corresponding week of last year. One of the deaths recorded last week was due to suicide, and two others to accidents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Year's Crime.</head>
        <p>The Year's Crime.</p>
        <p>The annual report of the Chief COnstable (Mr. Martin Nicholls), just issued, sets forth that the number of indictable crimes detected in the borough during the past year was 227, or 25 less than in the previous year. The num- ber of persons proceeded against for non-indict- able offences was 1,504, or 231 less than last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>"Ecclesiastical Words."</head>
        <p>"Ecclesiastical Words."</p>
        <p>In the Patten Hall on Tuesday evening, Mr. Henry_ Brieriey (chairman of the Wigan Branch of the English church Union) gave the War- rington Branch a very instructive lecture on the origin and meanings of familiar ecclesias- tical words. The Rev. W. J. Stanton (of Thel- wall, president) occupied the chair. The Rev. J. M. Buckmaster and Mr. W. H. Stansfield were elected members of the committee in the places of the late Rev. J. Holden (of Antrobus) and Mr. F. M. Darby (who has resigned).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Esperanto on the Battlefield</head>
        <p>Esperanto on the Battlefield</p>
        <p>The wa-r, like many other out-of-the-way events, is proving the value of Esperanto. A wounded Russian officer has been recounting his Esperantist experiences, giving instances of a conversation between an Armenian from the Caucasus and a Pole from Radom. He him- self, when captured,' was able to converse in Esperanto in the Austrian hospital with a wounded Hungarian soldier. Last week a Bel- gian Esperantist from Ghent visited the War- rington Esperantist Club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Wesley Guild Festival.</head>
        <p>Wesley Guild Festival.</p>
        <p>The annual Guild Festival in connection with Bold-street Wesleyan ,Church will be held to- morrow. The preacher, morning and evening, will be the Rev. Thos. Miller (formerly of this circuit). The anthem, "0, for a closer walk with God," will be sung in the morning, when Miss E. Taylor will be the soloist, and in the evening "By Babylon's wave" will be given by the choir. In the schoolroom on Monday, at 7.45 p.m., an address will be delivered by the Rev. Thos. Miller on "The War and Our Ideals." Mr. F. A. Harris will preside.</p>
        <p>Not Long Divided. We regret to record the death of Mrs. T. Preston Taylor, aged 58, of 9, Leaden-street, which ,occurr4d in the early hours of Monday morning. -Mrs. Taylor's husband predeceased her only 9 weeks ago, succumbing in the War- rington Infirmary to injuries received whilst engaged in shunting operations at the London and North-Western Railway sidings in Dallam- lane. Mrs. Taylor was actively connected with the Working Men's Mission, Bank Quay, and was a member of the Women's Class. The in- terment took place on Thursday afternoon at the Warrington Cemetery, following a service at the Mission. Messrs. T. and G. Dale con- ducted the last rites both at the Mission and in the Cemetery.</p>
        <p>Church Lads' Brigade at Supper. At the Kendrick-street Schools on Thursday evening of last week about 80 members of the St. Paul's Company of the Warrington Battalion of the Church Lads' Brigade (which is under the command of Captain F. Bunch) sat down to their annual hot-pot supper, which was pre- pared by lady members of the ~congregation. Mr. J. H. Smethurst presided. Mr. E. Wilkes had kindly arranged a concert, and Mr. W. H. Rigby's pierrot troupe, Mr. Dutton, and Miss Blackburne helped to make the evening a bright and merry one with songs, &amp;c. The Chairman, Major Brookes, Captain Ford and Captain Bunch gave brief addresses, and hearty cheers were given for about forty ex-officers and lads of the company who are now serving with the colours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Church Pastoral Aid Society.</head>
        <p>Church Pastoral Aid Society.</p>
        <p>At St. Peter's Church, , Birchall-street, on Sunday, the annual sermons in aid of the Church Pastoral Aid Society were preached. The special preacher was the Rev. 0. E. Hill, curate-in-charge of Earlestown, who spoke of the good work which the society had done, and expressed the desire that the people would keep up their support.—ln. the Schoolroom, on Mon- day night, the annual meeting of the parochial auxiliary was held. In addition to Mr. T. S. Steele who presided, there were present on the platform the Rev. B. Percival (vicar), the Rev. J. Martin (curate), Mr. T. Reddish (people's warden) and Mr. Armitage. In the .course of a short opening speech, the Chairman spoke of the benefit derived by such parishes as St. Peter's from the work of the society. The Rev. G. Johnson (vicar of St. Andrew's, Litherland), who was the special speaker, gave 'an account of the work of the Church Pastoral Aid Society, especially referring to the services it had been able to render to Litherland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Firemen at Dinner.</head>
        <p>Firemen at Dinner.</p>
        <p>Warrington's sturdy fire fighters held their annual dinner at the Mason's Arms on Friday evening of 'last week. The ex-Superintendent of the Fire Brigade (Mr. Frank Turner) presided, and he was supported by Mr. F. H. Turner, superintendent, and other members of the bri- gade.—A gold medal was presented to ex-Fire- man Joseph- Archer "as an appreciation of valuable services rendered to the Brigade for over 13 years."—During an interval in the har- mony Supt. Turner gave a brief report of the year's work, and said there were 25 fires and four false alarms. Nine of the fires were out of the borough. The total damage was less than £5,000. Firemen H. Spencer and W. Pownall had left to join the colours. He was sure ,they all regretted that through ill-health Alderman Burton had retired from the Fire Brigade Committee,, for the Alderman had dope much to bring' th-e—Brigade up to the present standard of elliciency.—Several entertaining items were rendered. by Masers. Gored (who also acted as accompanist), J. Carter, J. Areker, Triremes. G. Ternon, B. Cook Hoary Siam, Tf . Sclidirii tali It lihdigitm</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>EXAMINER, S A TURDA•Y, JANUARY 23, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, S A TURDA•Y, JANUARY 23, 1915.</p>
        <p>" JOIN THE SOUTH LINCS."</p>
        <p>"OF KITCHENER'S ARMY THE CREAM AND THE PICK."</p>
        <p>The recruiting officer will accept men for any regiment that is open; but there is a certain pride under the Territorial system in belong- ing to the regiment that bears the name of the district in which a man's home and business interests lie. Famous traditions also have their attractions for the man who is making his decision how and where he can best serve his country. When Sir John French sent his "contemptible little Army" into action, he told them that they ' Had a just cause;,. That all the regiments in the Expeditionary Force had glorious traditions to maintain; and That the individual intelligence of the British soldier would prove a greater strength than numbers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>SOUTH LANCASHIRES' TRADITIONS</head>
        <p>SOUTH LANCASHIRES' TRADITIONS</p>
        <p>A Great Past and a Greater Future.</p>
        <p>"All the regiments," ran Sir John French's Order, "comprising the Expeditionary Force bear on their colours emblems and names which constantly remind them -of glorious, victories achieved by them in the past. I have the most complete confidence that those regiments, as tliey stand to day in close proximity to the enemy, will not only uphold the magnificent traditions of former days, but will add fresh laurels to their standards."</p>
        <p>What are the traditions of the South Lanca- shire Regiment that have already been so valiantly maintained on many a hard-fought .field in Belgium and France? A mere list will give a good idea. This is the glorious record, in brief, of battles and campaigns in which our famous regiment has borne an honoured part : Louisburg. Martinique. (1762.) Havannah. St. Lucia. '0778.) Monte Video. Rolica. Vimiera. Corunna. Talavera. Badajoz. Salamanca. Vittoria. Pyrenees. Nivelle. Orthes. Toulouse. Peninsula. Niagara. Waterloo. Candahar. (1842.) Ghuznee. (1842.) Cabool. (1842.) Maharajpore. Sevastopol. Lucknow. New Zealand. Relief of Ladysmith. South Africa. (1899-1902.) Now the men are en route for Berlin. "OURSELVES."</p>
        <p>The spirit that animates the South Lanca- shires of to-day is roughly but expressively dis- played in the following lines which have been written by a Corporal in the' Bth Battalion, which is in training down South and straining at the leash :</p>
        <p>It's a fact since the war turned the poor Kaiser's brain, To see Castles and Thrones in the air; That our Country's been busy arranging a cure, To heal up that sore place 'neath his hair. So our War Lords discussed and arrived at this plan: " call on our Navy and Banks, We have got ships and money, but still we need men— So to get them call out the South Lancs." So they called on us all and we turned up like men, For our Flag and our Country we came; Some of us left kids, and wives, and our homes, But the mob called us out just the same. For we hold Pride of Regiment dear to us all,' From the C.O. right down to the Ranks, And we know there's no mob that is just quite as good As ourselves, who are known as South Lanes. We're of Kitchener's Army the cream and the pick, How we stuck C hist'ry will tell; And the man who could, stick C—'s muck, dirt and rain, He could almost find comfort in H-11. We can soldier with. pleasure—our officers know, For they're gentlemen, not blatant swanks, We wilt go with them smiling to Hell or Berlin— Either place would not bother South Lancs. So all you civilians who'd like to enlist, In a mob that's the pride of their day, Just call at our barracks and if you're a man, We'll enlist you, so now don't delay. You won't rue it, for shortly to Berlin we'll go, And we'll earn King and Country's best thanks, So if in at the death you would like to be seen, Hurry up, man, and join the South Lancs. J.W.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>WARRINGTON RECRUITING.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RECRUITING.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant-Colonel Mocatta, head of the re- cruiting department at the Central Police Buildings, told our representative, ypsterday, that he had been disappointed with thelresponse of the shop assistant class in Warrington. He was also surprised tilat the airship outrage had not stimulated recruiting, locally, whereas the Scarborough raid caused quite a boom. The Colonel drew attention to the fact that those joining the colours now would train at home during the winter, and, if the war con- tinued so long, they would be going abroad in the best of the weather.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>BRISK RECRUITING FOR LORI)</head>
        <p>BRISK RECRUITING FOR LORI)</p>
        <p>DERBY'S "PALS."</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding the large contributions which Liverpool and the district has given to His Majesty's forces in response to Lord Kitch- ener's demands, the supply of willing volunteers seems by no means exhausted. A few days ago Lord Derby announced the desire of the War Office that the four battalions of Comrades—or " pals " as they are more popularly known— should be reinforced by four eitra companies, a company to each battalion. Recruiting for this purpose was at once set about, with the re- sult that a stream of new-comers has been steadily flowing in, and the required number is now nearly complete. The recruiting offices are at Cooper's Build- ings, Church-street, Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>660 FIGHTERS FROM CROSFIELD'S.</head>
        <p>660 FIGHTERS FROM CROSFIELD'S.</p>
        <p>Firm Pay Out £l,OOO Monthly To Dependents. f</p>
        <p>A sum of over .21,000 per month is being paid by Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., to the dependents of employes., who have joined the Army. Over 660 men from the works are now serving with the colours, and included in these are the following eleven officers :—Major G. R. Crosfield (deputy-chairman of the company), 2nd Lieutenant E. 0. Crosfield, 2nd Lieutenant C. IL Clarke and Lieutenant B. T. Dickson, 4th South Lancashire. Territorials; 2nd Lieutenant H. E. Huffam, 6th Cheshire Regiment; Lieuten- ant R. A. Seymour Jones and Captain A. B. Layton, 4th South Lancashire Territorials; Lieutenant-Colonel H. K. Oram, 3rd Manchester Regiment; Lieutenant L. M. Sandison, 3rd King's Own Scottish Borderers; Lieutenant P. H. Tallock, Royal Naval Reserve; and Captain J. H. B. Wigginton, A.S.C., sth London Bri- gade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Medal for a Plucky_ Rescuer.</head>
        <p>Medal for a Plucky_ Rescuer.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Hippodrome on Friday evening of last week, the Liverpool Shipwreck and Humane Society's medal was presented to Captain W. Tindall Bell, of ,Fairfield-road, Stockton Heath, who is employed by Messrs. Crosfield and Sons on one of their barges, and who in November last heroically jumped into the river at Bank Quay and, in spite of the strong ebb tide then running, rescued from drowning a man named Bramwell. The pres- entation wa,s made Troth the Stage by Connell- ler Boborie, vi. praigiett GIP rosipieWo ptuoi and lunge. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>COTTAGE HOMES HEADMASTER.</head>
        <p>COTTAGE HOMES HEADMASTER.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. Lawrance Passes Away</p>
        <p>After a Brief Ilhaess.</p>
        <p>POOR CHILDREN LOSE A GOOD FRIEND.</p>
        <p>A sad event took place at the Padgate Cottage Homes on Friday last week, when Mr. Charles Edwin Lawrance, "the head master and super- intendent of the Homes and Schools, passed away after an illness of only a few days' dura- tion. The end came somewhat suddenly at 11 p.m., death being due to valvular disease of the heart and acute nephritis.</p>
        <p>For several weeks Mr:Lawfrance had not been in quite his- usual state of health, but he had been able to perform his duties, and a week before his death attended a committee meeting dealing with certain alterations which are to be carried out at the Homes. It was then noticed that he looked quite ill. The following day his condition became so serious that he could not continue his duties and became confined to his room, in which he remained to his death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>AN ASSIDUOUS WORKER.</head>
        <p>AN ASSIDUOUS WORKER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lawrance, whose age was 37, was only appointed to his position at Padgate in August, 1912; having, together with Miss Alice Ethel Hart then assistant matron at the Warren Farm School, Brighton, applied for the joint appointments of superintendent and head master and matron of the Padgate Cottage Homes and Schools. This application having been favourably considered out of 72, they were married just before commencing their new duties amongst the 180 children at the Homes— to whom they have since greatly endeared them- selves. Baring the two and a half years he held the position, Mr. Lawrance had worked 'assidu- ously for the welfare of the children, and the Guardians have on many occasions expressed their high appreciation of his efforts, and grati- fying reportsi have also been received from H.M. Inspector of Schools. The administration has been wonderfully improved, and many urgently needed reforms have been successfully carried out. The head master was extremely popular with the officials of the Union, and their deep syncipathy is felt with the widow, who is left with an infant son.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>DECEASED OFFICIAL'S CAREER.</head>
        <p>DECEASED OFFICIAL'S CAREER.</p>
        <p>, Commencing iu the teaching profession upon the staff of St. Luke's Parochial School, Chelsea —where he gained his certificate and was made master in charge of the lower department—Mr. Lawranee had, before coming to Warrington, also held the responsible position of principal of the County of Lancaster and Bolton Industrial Schools, at which his work afforded general sat- isfaction.</p>
        <p>Mr. • Lawrance was born in London, and his father, Mr. ' William Lawrance, is steward to the Duke of Devonshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The mortal remains of the deceased gentle- man were laid to rest at Christ Church, Pad- gate, on Tuesday afternoon, in the presence of members of the family, Board of Guardians and Union officials, Cottage Homes staff and chil- dren, and friends. The Rev. E. E. Chamber- lain (curate-in-charge) conducted the services both in the church and at the graveside. At the former the choirboys were present, and Mr. F. Blackwell presided at the organ, the hymns "Peace, perfect peace" and "Now the labour- er's task is o'er " being sung. The Dead March in " Saul " was also impressively rendered. The principal mourners were: Mrs. Lawrance (widow), Mr. William Lawrance (father), Messrs. Lionel and Arthur Lawrance (brothers). The Board of Guardians was represented by: Mr. W. Furness (deputy-chairman), the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, Miss Broadbent, Mrs. Hall, Messrs. G. Allen, W. C. Brooks, T. Dale, W. Demiett, J. A. Hughes and G. Mather. ' The Union officials present were: Messrs. A. Bottomley (clerk), R. I. Partridge (assistant clerk), B. Shearer (superintendent relieving officer and collector), F. Gidman -(relieving 'officer), W. Bowes (registrar), W. Loxham (Workhouse master), L. Jones (engineer), and J. Burke (hospital porter). The Cottaiti Homes Staff was represented by: Mrs. Oddy, Mrs. Hilditch, Miss Chadwick (teachers), 'Mrs. Jones, Mrs. Vavasor, Miss Ben- nett and Miss Clegg (foster mothers), Mrs. Worrall (lodge keeper), Mr. F. J. Hampson (superintendent's clerk), Miss Griffiths and Miss Jeffers (laundry department). Amongst others present were : Dr. Bennett, Messrs. J. H. J. Hampson, H. T. Houghton (vicar's warden at Christ Church), T. J. Lee, 0. Hilditch and A. E. French.</p>
        <p>The following employes at the Cottage Homes acted as bearers: Messrs. F. Gagan, J. Jones, J. Worrall and E. Vavasor.</p>
        <p>Floral tributes were sent Hy the following: "His sorrowing wife," "To dad from dear little Bill," "Father and mother," "Flo, Charlie and family," "Arthur and Jennie," "An old friend E.M.C.," Messrs. Barnes, Openshaw and Jesse. Mr. and Mrs. Loxham, Miss Emerton and Dr. Jagger, clerk's staff, relieving officers and registrars of the Warrington Union, Mr. and Mrs. P. H. Aukland, officers of the Cottage Homes, "In loving • remembrance from the children of the Cottage Homes," staff of the Warrington Union, Mr. George Mather, Mr. and Mrs. W. C. Brooks, " Will " and Mrs. Stephens, Reginald Chalmers, Mr. and Mrs. Oswald Hilditch, and Mr. and Mrs. A. E. French. ,</p>
        <p>Mr. Edward Connor had charge of the funeral arrangements. Preaching at Christ Church, Padgate, on Sunday morning. the Rev., E. R. Chamberlain said Mr. Lawrance had been engaged in one of the noblest works which any man could per- form—that of training and moulding the char- acters of the young. Re had done that work well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>GUARDIANS' BY-ELECTION.</head>
        <p>GUARDIANS' BY-ELECTION.</p>
        <p>POLLING IN BEWSEY WARD NEXT THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>The polling for the election of a Guardian for Bewsey Ward, in the place of Mrs. Simpson, who has left the district, will take place be- tween 12 uoon and 8 p.m. on Thursday. - As the vacancy arises through the retirement of a lady Guardian, no doubt the electors will take the opportunity of electing another lady to fill the gap. The lady candidate has had much experience of public \ work, and female repre- sentation at the Workhouse is most essential. The candidates are : Mrs. Mary Roberts, widow, 2, Kendrick-street. Mr. William J. Shaw, master plumber, Grey- hound Inn, 89, Dallam-lane. The polling stations will be St. Ann's School, Dannett-street, and No. 98, Hoyle-street. We stated last week that Mrs. Roberts, who takes a keen interest in charitable organisations, was on the committee of the Soldiers' and Sailors' Families' Association. We should have said the Soldiers' and Sailors' Wives' Fund (Friends' Meeting House).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>PREPARATORY TRAINING CORPS.</head>
        <p>PREPARATORY TRAINING CORPS.</p>
        <p>FAIRFIELD AND • HOWLEY COMPANY. , The usual week-end company 'firing on the rifle range at Rylands' Recreation Ground was very interesting. A variable wind upset several of the marksmen, but the results were, never- theless, satisfactory. Firing eight rounds and counting the best five shots, with a possible of 50, the results were as follows :—D. Plinston, 42; H. Gaunt, 40; T. E. Peters, 37; R. Gibson, 37; S. Williams, 36; W. Paskin, 35; W. Delooze, 34; J. Langhorn, 33, S. Garnett, 32; J. Howarth 32. The best aggregates were: H. Gaunt, 56; T. E. Peters; 51; S. Williams, 49; D. Plinston, 48; R. Gibson, 45; W. Paskin, 45. The stew- ards at the firing point and butts were S. Oddy and T. H. Newton.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening at the Municipal Range the-results obtained did not reach the previous average. Of course, the altered conditions made a difference, and the company will require to show their best form if the Whitecross and Bewsey Company are to be overcome in the proposed inter-company shoot at the Legh-street Range cm Tuesday evening next.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY, *let at tie Pvill IlJi at 2.5 i.a. etrtaix.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>WAR STORIES: BY LETTER AND INTERVIEW.</head>
        <p>WAR STORIES: BY LETTER AND INTERVIEW.</p>
        <p>RECOVERING AT RADDON COURT.</p>
        <p>Frost-bitten ." Tommie" Arrive for Treatment.</p>
        <p>A further batch of seven wounded soldiers arrived at the Raddon Court (Red Cross) Tem- porary Hospital on Sunday. When an " Ex- aminer " representative visited the hospital the other day he heard several vivid battle stories from the men, and also accounts of the hard- ships endured by our brave fighters in the trenches during the cold wintry days now being experienced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>BOWLED OVER BY JACK FROST.</head>
        <p>BOWLED OVER BY JACK FROST.</p>
        <p>Private Ashton, who is suffering from frost- bite, related his experience as a rifleman in the trenches, and, describing how his regiment took ,a German trench which was only 50 yards away, he said: "Our artillery kept up a heavy fire during the morning, and about 5 o'clock in the afternoon we received the order to fix bayonets. We rushed at the Germans about 100 strong, and wiped out over 200 of the enemy." • Talking of shell fire, Private Ashton said: "I saw one shell drop and make a very large hole. A piece of shrapnel from it knocked one poor fellow's brains out. An officer rushed up to him immediately afterwards, but, of course, could do nothing for him." "We were up to the waist in water on and off for four days at a time," continued the Private, "and, of course, it is no wonder there is a lot of frost bite."</p>
        <p>Do you think the Germans are getting short of ammunition?" our representative asked. "No," was the reply, "I don't think so, although our artillery undoubtedly fire more shells than they do."</p>
        <p>AEROPLANE AS TARGET FOR THIRTY SHELLS.</p>
        <p>Rifleman Parkin, of the 3rd King's Royal Rifles, who is also suffering from frost-bite, was enjoying a cup of tea with friends when asked to relate his experiences. "Well," he said, "I have nothing much to tell because I was only out a month and only in the trenches for 48 hours. I saw the artillery and aeroplanes at work, and one incident which struck me was where an aeroplane managed to escape, despite the fact that thirty shells were fired It it at once. I don't know however the pilot could have managed to get off scot free, hut he did."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>TREACHEROUS HUN.</head>
        <p>THE TREACHEROUS HUN.</p>
        <p>Despicable Ruses Which " Don't Come</p>
        <p>Off" Now!</p>
        <p>In conversation with a representative of "The Examiner,-", a wounded sergeant of • the 2nd Battalion Of the South Lancashire Regiment, now recovering from his injuries at Orford Bar- racks, recounted several vivid stories of the battles of Mons, the Marne, Le Bassee, the Aisne, and Ypres, in which he took part.</p>
        <p>Little, But Not Contemptible!</p>
        <p>With regard to the Germans and their meth- ods, the sergeant said: "Everyone is aware that they are not 'above board,' as the saying goes. At Mons they resorted to none of the tricks that they used thereafter. I think it was taken for granted by their officers that their great num- bers would completely crush so small a force as our 'contemptible little Army,' but what a surprise was in store for them! Just fancy six of their army corps being held in check for 36 hours by a portion of our Expeditionary Force. A good many of our poor chaps 'went west,' but quite a lot of the enemy went the same way. Ultimately we had to retire a matter of 250 miles and thy were just beginning to think that they had the game well, in hand when the French and ourselves did ViM 'about turn,' and in the hattle that ensued got them on the run-.--and they went at quite a decent pace, too!'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Ready-witted British Generals.</head>
        <p>Ready-witted British Generals.</p>
        <p>"A good many times they must have had us almost surrounded, and when they did get us like that it was a case of an incessant bursting of shells above and about us. But we always managed, thanks to the quick and ready wit of our Generals, to give them the slip. The hardships endured by our troops were terrible— a snack once a day, perhaps not at all—and, taking into consideration the forced marching and the fighting of the rear-guard actions that had to be done—well, I leave you to imagine what it was like. However, the spirit of the troops was noble. During the time we were driving the Germans back we saw the most hor- rible sights imaginable. It was really heroic, the way a few of the Belgian and French peasants stuck to their homes, in spite of heavy rifle, machine-gun and shell fire." "There is no doubt," the Sergeant continued, "that the Germans have a very methodical way of working, and the thoroughness with which they do everything is deserving 'a great credit, but our way of doing things cannot, to my mind, be beaten.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Blood-and-Otherwise-Thirsty!</head>
        <p>Blood-and-Otherwise-Thirsty!</p>
        <p>"The number of Church spires the German Artillery have knocked down and the great piles of empty bottles, the contents of which have been consumed by the Germans, can hardly be imagined or credited. Their favourite trick is to have machine-guns in their ambulances, and, with these,' they did some deadly work until we twigged their little game, and, since then, of course, there is n 4 mercy shown to the bogus Red Cross' ambulances. Another trick the Germans were fond of playing was to send a small party with a white flag to cover, the ad- vance of a strong force. The idea of this, osten- sibly, was to lure our troops out of the trenches for the purpose of taking the flag-party prisoners. As soon as they got within a con- venient distance, however, the flag-party would drop flat, and the stronger force behind would pour in a murderous fire. The most treacherous thing imaginable," added the Sergeant, "was the bringing of machine-guns into action on the stretchers. This we only found out recently. But, then, in spite of all their cunning, we can still show them the way battles are won."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>GERMANS CAUGHT NAPPING.</head>
        <p>GERMANS CAUGHT NAPPING.</p>
        <p>A Night Raid on One of the Enemy's</p>
        <p>Trenches.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Whittaker, hairdresser, Winwick-road, has received a short letter from Private T. Cog- gin% who is serving at the Front with the Engineers. The writer explains that he indited the epistle in a small farm just to the rear of the firing line, and adds, by way of explaining the brevity of his communication, "We never know when we shall get shelled out. Last night," he continues, "I was working clean in front of the firing-line. We dug from under our trench up to the enemy's; in Army phraseology we were 'sapping' under their barbed wire entanglements. When we have pushed the trench far enough we blow the enemy up, and, you bet, we have to duck when the explosion occurs. . . . Our fellows ' copped ' the Germans napping the other night. Twenty-five of them stole into the enemy's trench in the dark and caught the beggars asleep and put some cold steel into them. It wasn't German hollow-ground steel, but good British We see some sights out here—cattle lying about dead, farm houses blown down, and churches knocked to pieces." The writer goes on to say that he came across two brothers named Broderick, whose mother resides in Warrington, and who are serving with the East Lancashires. "Thy look fit and well," he adds.</p>
        <p>ACHILD IS SURE TO BE FRETFUL if his stomach is out of order. For .this trouble there is no medicine so useful as Steedman's (EE) Powders. Full directions in every packet. ,Booklets free on application to J. STEEDMAN &amp; Co., 272, Walworth Road, -Lond.)n, S.E.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>"ACCOUNTS TO SETTLE."</head>
        <p>"ACCOUNTS TO SETTLE."</p>
        <p>Why a Merry Marine Wants to Meet</p>
        <p>the Germans.</p>
        <p>Private Arthur Gamble, 336, Knutsford-road, who at the outbreak of war was a marine on• board H.M.S. Cumberland, but is now on H.M.S. Challenger, writing to his sister, Mrs. L. Oliver, 10, Barnard-street, Sankey Bridges (whose hus- band and two other brothers have enlisted since the war began), says : " Our ship has gone —, but we are still needed at Duala (West Africa), as there are a lot of Germans there who want shifting. I have been down with fever. . . . Money is not much use to us, as we cannot spend it, and I don't care, now that I have got over the fever, so long as I can have a shilling or two to spend when I get leave—whenever that will be! I hope you had as nice a Christmas as possible under the circumstances, but I am afraid it was a poor Christmas for everybody. But we must make the best of the times. At the moment of writing,.we have not had it, but we can well imagine our Christmas dinner—corned beef and ship's biscuits. But we shan't grumble, for there are a lot worse off than us. . . . We are patiently waiting for "the dirty dogs," and I, personally, am wanting them to turn up, as I have a few accounts I would like to settle with them."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>GLAD THEY WERE CAPTURED.</head>
        <p>GLAD THEY WERE CAPTURED.</p>
        <p>Famished and 111-Clad German Prisoners.</p>
        <p>During the past few days letters have been received from Private Stanley Wright, of the Army Service Corps, by two of his brothers in Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>"Shells Falling All Round Us."</head>
        <p>"Shells Falling All Round Us."</p>
        <p>Writing to Mr. James Wright, of 93, Cart- wright-street, he. _says:—</p>
        <p>"-We are still kloing splendid work. Leaving England we landed at , and stopped there for four days, the place being so drowded that we slept under .the waggons. We then started moving about from place to place, stopping a day or so here and a day or so there. One night we stopped in a brewery. After our divi- sion had been through the battle of • , we were given lour days' rest, and I can tell you we wantell it, too. Our next move was to-, and before we shifted from there the place was reduced to a mass of ruins, the shells from the enemy's guns destroying a beautiful church and several other grand buildings. Whilst we .were there shells were falling all round us, and I can assure you that it was sufficiently excit- ing to satisfy the most 'ardent craving for ex' citement. Not only were our poor fellows being kid, but, of course, some of the Inhabitants met their fate also. . . lam pl6ased to say that I came out of it all right."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>AN EXCITING NIGHT RIDE.</head>
        <p>AN EXCITING NIGHT RIDE.</p>
        <p>In a. previous letter written to his other brother; Mr. George Wright, of 14, Wilkinson- street, the gallant Private says: "We are right amongst it again. Our troops took a lot of prisoners the other night. We 'chatted with several of them, and they told us that they were glad they fell into our hands, as for a long time they had been unable to get enough to eat or to wear. There were about two thousand of them, and most of them did look "sights," I can tell you. . . . A night or so back we were out, when the Germans shot one of my horses. There were four of them, and we gave chase and succeeded in accounting for the whole quartet. Next day we buried them, and I can assure yau that it was not a nice job to have to undertake. I got a German helmet as a souvenir, and was looking forward to getting it home as a trophy, but coming back to our bivouac I lost it in the dark. And, my word, it was dark, being in the middle of the night. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Nearly Captured in the Dark.</head>
        <p>Nearly Captured in the Dark.</p>
        <p>We could not possibly see where we were going and had to trust to good fortune. One of our drivers was shot on the way, but the wound was not a serious one. He got it in the leg. Phew! When I come to think of it, we have had some narrow escapes. About a week back the whole lot of us nearly got captured. In the dark we drove on too far and we got to within comparatively a few yards of the enemy before we realised where we were going." "I am in the 'pink," says the writer, by way of a cheery conclusion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>LICENSING LEVY.</head>
        <p>LICENSING LEVY.</p>
        <p>Brewers Appeal to Justices for Suspension.</p>
        <p>REQUEST NOT GRANTED.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Licensing Justicesp held yesterday afternoon, there was a discussion as to the continuance of the licensing levy for the extinction of redundant licences. Letters were received from Messrs. Peter Walker and Co., Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., and Messrs. Bolton Bros. suggesting that, in view of the additional taxation imposed by the war, and in view of the provision that the firms named had been called upon to make foi• the dependents of men at the Front, the compensation levy should be allowed to remain in abeyance for the year 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>WOULD CRIPPLE FUTURE ACTION.</head>
        <p>WOULD CRIPPLE FUTURE ACTION.</p>
        <p>After a long discussion a proposal was made that the full compensation levy be imposed. To that an amendment was moved that only half of the usual levy be asked for this year.</p>
        <p>The amendment was lost and the resolution carried.</p>
        <p>The majority of the magistrates felt that as there is still said to be a number of redundant licences in the town, it would be crippling their future action if they suspended the, levy for the ensuing year, as there would then be created a shortage of funds, which would tie them as to future action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>INTERESTING COUNTY COURT CASE PENDING.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING COUNTY COURT CASE PENDING.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thurs- day, before his Honour Judge Gurner, an appli- cation was down for hearing for the review of the award of 7s. per week compensation made in favour of James Ward, Hall-street, against Messrs. Dolan and Sons, builders, Warrington. —Mr. Healey appeared for the applicants, and Mr. C. 11. Unsworth for the respondent —Ward, who was awarded the compensation in June last, has since joined the Army.—On the Judge taking his seat, Mr. Healey applied for an ad- journment of the case on the ground that Mr. Adshead Elliot, who had been briefed for the applicants, had been taken suddenly ill and was not aible to appear. As there was an im- portant point of law involved, said Mr. Healey, he particularly wished Mr. Elliot to argue it.— After hearing Mr. Unsworth on the other side, the Judge adjourned the case until February 4th, on condition that the applicants paid the respondent's costs for the day, and re-instated the compensation from that day until Feb. 4th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>hi Full Dress.</head>
        <p>hi Full Dress.</p>
        <p>Lucas Cleve, the novelist, tells of an old nurse, who had a very pretty daughter. The girl met a millionaire broker at Broadstairs one week-end. The man proposed and they were married. An excellent match. Lucas Cleve saw the mother a short time after ,the wedding. "Molly has done well, hasn't she?" she said. "She has that, ma'am," said the old woman.</p>
        <p>"Her husband is very rich, isn't he?" "Rich ! Save us, yes. Ye should see, ma'am, -brougham, her coachman and footman, her motor . ilia and her diamaqs and pearls. O. she live. higlL gaits like the nobility Alla VIII fry. errant. Way. eke stripe kr einity•T'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>DRINK-WRECKED HOME.</head>
        <p>A DRINK-WRECKED HOME.</p>
        <p>Horrible Story of a Mother's Callour Neglect.</p>
        <p>WHY A BOY STOLE.</p>
        <p>"The home surroundings of this poor boy are not what they ought to be, if he is to be given a 'fair chance in life," was the statement made to the Borough Magistrates on Thursday mornhig by Supt. Williamson, whep they had before them a diminutive boy on charges of having stolen a dandy cock and hen\ from a fowl-house on January 11th, and of stealing in October last a number of articles from a clothes line in the vicinity of Wellington-street. At the same time the boy's mother—Sarah Stringer. of 55, Wellington-street—stood in the dock to answer a charge of having received the articles from the clothes line, well knowing them to have been stolen, and also for having neglected four of her children—Jessie, aged 15; Fred, aged 14; Isabel, aged 6; and William, aged three months —in a manner likely to have caused them -un- necessary suffering. The boy pleaded guilty to the charges of theft, and the woman admitted receiving the goods, but said that she did not know they were stolen. To the charge of neglecting her children she replied "Nothing out of the ordinary."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>Where the Stolen Fowls Were Found.</head>
        <p>Where the Stolen Fowls Were Found.</p>
        <p>The Superintendent told the Court that the boy admitted stealing the two fowls from the backyard of 57, Wellington-street. The birds were seen safe in their shed at 5 o'clock in the evening, and at 6.15 they had gone. At 6.30 the same evening the boy offered them for sale to a man who kept a bird shop in Howley-lane. He said the birds were-his own, and the man gave him 10d. each for them. The man kept a stall in the Market, and the following Saturday he had them on offer. The owner chancing to go to the Market that day recognised the birds, and reported the fact to the police. When ap- prehended the boy admitted taking the birds, and said that he had given 6d. of the money he received for them to his mother, telling her that he had borrowed it. It happened that there had been a series of clothes line robberies in the vicinity of the boy's home last October, and the sergeant detailed on the case had reason to believe- that the boy knew something about the thefts, so questioned him. The boy ad- mitted stealing a sheet on October 14th and pledging it for a shilling, adding that he had destroyed the ticket. On October 15th, he ad- mitted, he stole a sheet and took it home to his mother, telling her where he had got it from. She, thereupon, put it into a drawer, and said she would take it back to the owner. To the police the woman said that it was only a half-sheet, and that she had torn it up for house-cloths. On the other hand, the boy said' that a fortnight later she sent him to pawn it for a shilling. With regard to four children's garments stolen by the boy from a clothes line, he said that he—had pawned them, and after a prolonged search two of them had been found included in a bundle of articles sent for pledge by the mother on -January 6th. When Sergt. Iteddington went to. see the „mother with regard to the boy, he found her tiffder the influence of drink, there being an empty whisky bottle on the mantelpiece and a jug of beer in the cup- board. With regard to the boy, the Superin- tendent stated he had been instructed by the Chid' Constable to suggest that in his own in- terests he should be sent to a school. He was a fearful liar. He only left school in September, and in October he started this thieving, dispos- ing of the property himself. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>Shocking Story.</head>
        <p>A Shocking Story.</p>
        <p>_ On behalf of the N.S._ P.C.C., Mr. Browne, solicitor, appeared to conduct the prosecution of the woman on the charge of child neglect. He told the Court that the society did not so much blame the boy for what he had done, because his offence arose out of his surround- ings. The father was a hard-working man and was away from home a great deal, being em- ployed on a barge. The mother had, for the last twelve months, given way to drink. Inspector Wainwright, of the N.S.P.C.C., had had to warn her from time to time as to her treatment of her children. When Sergt, Reddington visited the house to make inquiries about the stolen fowls, he found it in a shockingly filthy condi- tion. The mother was under the influence of drink, and she told him that some whisky he saw on the mantelshelf was for the three and a half months old baby. Witnesses could be called to show that she had ctually given that tiny baby whisky, "to cure it of the wind," as she herself had explained.</p>
        <p>Boy Says He Stole To Buy Food. When the woman was confined in September last,* Miss Edwards, the Corporation health visitor, called on the woman and found the house in a'bad condition; in fact, the place was not properly fit to be inhabited, and \that despite the fact that 355. a week was coming in. The boy said that he had committed the thefts, with which he had been charged that day, it' order to buy feod. His dinners—although he was at work and earned 9s. per week—chiefly consisted of. a "penorth of chips" and a round of bread. The father was altogether blameless. He was paid 15s. a week as a bargenian, plus an amount .for tonnage, and he was away from home all the week through. Miss Edwards spoke to visiting .the woman at the time of her accouchement, and to finding both the house and the baby in a neglected con- dition. At the time she thought the woman was very poor, and felt sorry for her.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>Son's Evidence Against Mother.</head>
        <p>Son's Evidence Against Mother.</p>
        <p>The son who had figured in the previous charge, was then called to give evidence against his mother. On entering the witness-box he cried bitterly, but amid his tears managed ro answer Mr. Browne's questions. _ _</p>
        <p>"Have you had enouih food?" he Was asked, and he answered, "Sometimes we had!"</p>
        <p>What did you steal 'those things for?—My mother had no money at all, and I had to go to work and wanted some food in my basket. You took them to buy food with ?—Yes, sir. What did you feed on?—I used to have " chips" for dinner. Any meat at all?—Sometimes, on Saturdays and Mondays. When was the last time you had a wash all over?—When I was in the Infirmary. And that was twelve months ag6P—Yes, sir. Sergt. Reddington deposed that when he called at prisoner's house about the robberies referred to in the previous case he found the place in a filthy condition, the ashes from the fireplace in the living room overflowing from the grate to half-way down the floor, whilst on other parts of the floor there was dirt half an inch thick. Ile called attention to the shocking condition of the place and the woman said, "Ii you had been ill like me you could not clean the. house up."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>"Only Fit for a Pig to Live In."</head>
        <p>"Only Fit for a Pig to Live In."</p>
        <p>Subsequently he made a-search of the house, and found the rooms upstairs in such a condi- tion that they were literally not fit for a pig to live in. A cupboard downstairs was simply full of dirt; if the house had been empty for ten. years it could not have been worse. All the baby had to wear was a neck-shawl wrapped round it. When he questioned the boy about the thefts, he said that he took the fowls and sold them, using part of the money to buy food. He also went to a place of amusement with some of the proceeds, and gave his mothe,,r pence, telling her that he had borrowed it from "a boy named Jones." With one of the shirts he pawned, he said he bought coffee and some bread and cheese to take to work with him, Inspector Wainwright described the revolting condition of the house, and said that the hus- band had stated that his wife was all right when she kept away from the drink. He sent her 21s. a week and the boy was bringing in Bs. 6d. or 95., whilst one of the girls also contributed 4s. The husband was a decent, hard-working man, and did not drink at all, and he seemed afraid to say anything against his wife. The boy was ordered to be sent to an indus- trial school, and the mother—who received her sentence with tearful protettations—was color. mitted to prison for ix months.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>* • ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>HOW TO MAKE YOUR OWN BREAD.</head>
        <p>HOW TO MAKE YOUR OWN BREAD.</p>
        <p>Small delicious tooyes and rolls can be made easily and quickly by using BORWICirg - BAYING POWDER. Full directions es evilly package. Oloirainablo at stil Grocers.</p>
        <p>;</p>
