        <p>tZt-'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Reticent Husbands.</p>
        <p>Last week's little story of the man who tem- . pararily withheld from his wife the, news of his advance in salary in order that he might have a fortnight's enjoyment with the surplus reminded me of certain Comity Court experi- ences. Some judges whom I have known used a form of wages certificate for service on employ- ers of debtors coming before them, and masters readily supplied the information in order to avoid an unpleasant alternative. According to almost invariable rule, the person summoned would be represented\ by his wife, and his Honour, ,with the man's average earnings for the previous three months before him, would question the unsuspecting woman respecting her husband's wages. She would state truthfully haw much he gave her weekly, and would earnestly persist that it represented the utter- most farthing when the Judge's manifest in- credulity raised the alarming prospect of an order with which she would be unable to com- ply. Finally, his Honour would say, "Well, I'll make an order for ss. a month, and you may tell your husband to pay it out .of the 3s. which he has been keeping out of his weekly wages, evidently without your knowledge." Only the other, day there wits a case of a married man who had enlisted, and subsequent inquiries respecting the allowance to be made to his wife -during his absenec led to the revelation that he had kept bacli from his weekly wages no less than 10s., whereupon his indignant spouse exclaimed, with a significant gesture, "But wait till I get him home again!"</p>
        <p>" Treating " Belgians. Last week's leader on "The sin 9f treating soldiers" reminded me of recent disagreeable experiences of Belgian refugees in Mid-Lanca- shire, where I spent a few days of the New Year. With the mistaken kindness that is unfortunately so common in this great county, some of the townspeople had taken into public- houses such of the male foreigners as were wil- ling, and had given them drink to excess. Cer- tain of the Belgians who had been accommo- dated with others in a large house went home in a quarrelsome mood, and eventually' began fighting, with the result that furniture was smashed. Two tthers living in a different part of the district were also intoxicated, though quiet enough. I spoke to one of these about it afterwards, and his explanation was that the English beer was very strong, and that, accus- tomed as they were to the light drinli of their own country, they had not anticipated any such effects and would certainly avoid it in future. Unreasonable Critics.</p>
        <p>Let me hasten to assure readers that so far as I know the lapses referred to were quite ex- ceptional. I have happened across several parties of refugees, and have had not a few under constant observation for many weeks, and have had reason, not only to pity them in their sore extremity, but to marvel at and admire the fortitude with which they bear *heir dire misfortunes. I have met only one little company that betrayed anything like despair; the women wept and wrung their hands, and the men looked very sad. On the whole, however, they are a light-hearted people, and their troubles do not seem to weigh at all heavily on them for any length of time. This, curiously enough, appears to be misunderstood by some of my friends. Said one of these to me the other day, "I've just met some of yond refugees strolling about, and they were laugh- ing like all that; they didn't look to me as if they cared so much !" "Well," said I, "it's very thoughtless of them; they ought to culti- vate a woebegone expression for open-air use. I can quite understand that there are odd sub- scribers to the fund who, unless they have evidence that these poor exiles are at present incapable of smiling, are not satisfied that they are getting the worth of their money." "That's all very well," pursued my acquaintance, "but I called at my butcher's as I came along, and he told me he had just had a couple of Belgians in and they had bought quite a substantial piece ,of mutton, so, as he said, they're not doing ,so badly." Fancy! Awful, isn't it? After all, such critics, like the intemperate indivrduals who were led astray, constitute a small minority indeed. Anglers' Yarns. - - - -</p>
        <p>By way of contrast with the adversities re- vecting which " A.S." had to report in last week's angling column, let me set some of Mr. Carter Platt's 'reminiscences of an abnormally dry season. Naturally (remarks he) in times of severe droughestrange things do happen, and we have all heard of the river where all running water had disappeared, and even in the pools there was nothing left but a little moist mud at the bottom, in which all the trout were standing on their heads in order to keep their gills covered, so that whenever the local resi- dents fancied a dish of trout all they had to do was to stroll down to the river bed and pluck them by the tails like gathering spring onions. I think it would be about the same time of phenomenal dryness that several of the Yorkshire tributary streams dried up, and one acquaintance of mine, who lived on the bank of such an unfortunate stream, and w4o, with a fancy for amateur piviculture, had stocked the water with trout of his own hatching, said he was waked out of his sleep one .night by a per- sistent knocking at his door, and on throwing</p>
        <p>up the front window to ascertain the cause of the row discovered seventeen trout on his door- step, feebly knocking at the door with their tails, and asking for, a drink of water. Here is another of his weather stories, told, by one of a party in whose company he had fled to an inn for refuge from a summer storm : " I -was fishing up at Hawes one day, when a thunderstorm came on. I'd just charged my pipe, and was about to put a match to it when I saw' a trout rise a few yards in front of me. I seized the rod and made a cast. I missed the fish. For just at that moment a flash of light- ning struck the point of the rod, ran down it, up my arm, and lit my pipe. Curious, wasn't it?"</p>
        <p>" Y-e-e-s," replied another slowly, with the air of a man who has set his mind upon achiev- ing some great feat; "but not very curious. Ones, when I was fishing above Grassington, I'd just hooked a big two-pound trout, and was playing it, when a flash of lightning struck the point of my rod. Instead of running down the rod it ran down the line to the fish, and killed it, and split it open, and cooked it to a turn, so that when I landed it it was ready for eating!" Now let us get back to the refreshing candour and plain matter-of-fact of our local angling correspondent—or at least to the weather con- cerning which he lias had to write of late. Welcome Rain!</p>
        <p>I have actually 'discovered an optimistic farmer. By way of opening the conversation, I touched upon that very unusual topic, the weather; and of course I expected a growl. But no: his only comment respecting the almost continual downpour of the previous couple of months or so was—" Well, it'll every drop on't be needed afoor haytime." By-the-way, years ago I told some of my farmer friends about the Norwegian method of hay-making, as mentioned by Mr. C. Robinson in his lecture to the Philo- maths, but I cannot !say that the former were ag much impressed..as the latter. My fault, no doubt.</p>
        <p>Singing to the Poor. The report as to the concert party at Dutton Workhouse reminded me how, in one Union I knbw, it was customary to entertain the Work- house inmates weekly throughout the winter months. Local choirs and other musical organ- isations freely volunteered their services, and there was never any difficulty about the paupers' Saturday "pop." As a matter of fact, girigage- ments were always booked well ahead, and the enthusiasm of performers led to positive em- barrassment. The artistes were accompanied by friends, and the parties ultimately assumed such proportions that the Master had to beg the Guardians to limit the number to 40. The visitors invariably distributed quantities of tobacco, sweets, &amp;c., and altogether the inmates —who of course on these occasions remained up much beyond the usual hour for bed—had a high old time on Saturday nights.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>BUILT "WITH CLEAN MONEY."</head>
        <p>BUILT "WITH CLEAN MONEY."</p>
        <p>Rev. A. M. Mitchell on Burtonwood</p>
        <p>Church Building Scheme.</p>
        <p>Writing in the current issue of the Burton- wood Parish Magazine, the Vicar (the Rev. A. M. Mitchell) says : " It is upwards of ten years now since the inauguration of our Burtonwood Church Build- ing Scheme. In all these years, in some re- spects, weary years, we have striven to be true to a matter of principle. This has proved no easy thing, so strong have been the forces brought to bear against us. The Building Fund was originated with the distinct under-</p>
        <p>standing that t6ose methods of money-raising only, which might be deemed strictly lawful and right, consistent with the dignity of Holy Church, should be adopted. Bazaars were not to be held, whist drives and dances were not to be countenanced, other doubtful means of fill- ing Church coffers, such as raffles, draws, &amp;c., were not to be resorted to. It is with extreme satisfaction, after a decade of weekly collect- Avg, appeal, and prayer, that we are able to report the practical completion of the Church Hall Fund without any deviation whatever from the original intention and plan. There is, perhaps, as much as „2500 anti-bazaar money in the 1'2,200 (or more) which has been raised, and there is not, to our knowledge, in the gross amount, one penny of tainted money— money, that is, got together on false pretences, or by extortion, through bazaars, drink, carni- Vorous feasting, or gambling in any one of its many seductive forms. It is not, maybe, much to say, but we rejoice to say it all the same. Fierce and Long Temptation.</p>
        <p>"The temptation in ten long years to turn aside, to abandon principle, to fall into the ordinary Church line, to resort to worldly ex- pedients at the suggestion of well-meaning friends, at the rebuke of others equally sincere, has been both fierce and long. . . . . • . Whatever its future history may be, the Church Hall can always be pointed to as a standing nibnument to money-raising, under extreme difficulties, without recourse to those worldly tactics so generally welcomed by religionists of all denominations, who, in fact demand a quid pro quo for their money, a something in return for something given. Not a penny of illicit coin is in that substantial building; it has, thank God, been erected from foundation to cupola with clean money. We remember a note of ours on the bazaar question, which was reproduced in the London Press, bringing us by first mail a lady's cheque for £5O. Oppon- ents to the bazaar system of money-raising for religious purposes are not extinct—believers in the true system of giving and hoping for noth- ing again, from time to time, give unmistak- able.proof of their existence."</p>
        <p>.4.. .4</p>
