      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>k – '4"‘'-*kk 1..</head>
        <p>k - '4"‘'-*kk 1..</p>
        <p>_ - • "" ,„ •• • """•,..,'• ); • ‘.IZ". • ;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 23, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 23, 1915.</p>
        <p>DIST ICT ripw3</p>
        <p>Death Follows Accident.—About a fortnight ago Mr. Alfred Whitley, general hawker, 14, Chapel-lane,-Stockton Heath, Met with an acci- dent whilst at work with his horse and cart. He was admitted to the Warrington Infirmary and died on Thursday.</p>
        <p>Big Task for a Sub-committee.—At the Paro- chial Committee meeting on Tuesday evening, Mr. Diggle's report with regard to sewerage questions was referred for consideration to the following sub-committee: Councillors Pearson, Dukes, Bottomley, McDermid and Mclntyre.</p>
        <p>Present at Parochial Committee Meeting.— There were present at the Parochial Committee meeting on Tuesday evening Councillors G. Pilling (presiding), W. Mclntyre, H. J. West- brook, J. McDermid, C. Dukes, J. H. Clarke, and F. Rose, with Messrs. W. E. Hough (deputy clerk), S. Wright (surveyor), and E. Bradshaw (clerk to the Parish Council).</p>
        <p>Wesleyan "At Home."—The annual 'Church "At Homt " wps held in the Wesleyan School, room on Thursday evening. The Rev. W. T. Derry (pastor of the church) and Mrs. Derry Cordially welcomed the members of the congre- gation, Mr. Derry also giving a short address on Church work. • Refreshments were served by the lady workers. At the outset of the pro- ceedings the Rev. W. T. Derry referred to the absence of the Senior Leader of the Church (Mr. S. Moss) through illness, and a vote of sympathy was passed, coupled with it being the wish that he would speedily be restored to health. A most interesling programme was given, consisting of songs by Mr. A. Forster and Mr. A. E. Penketh ; a glee by Messrs. F. E. Pen- keth, T. Brereton, A. Forster and A. E. Pen- keth ; a reading by Mr. C. Wilcockson; and a humorous recitation by Mr. G. Whitfield. After an expression of thanks to the ladies and artistes, .a very pleasant - evening was brought to a close by the singing of the National Anthem. ,Mr. G. A. Whitfield acted as accom- panist.</p>
        <p>Band of Hope Entertainment.—The Hill Cliffe and Stockton Heath United Band of Hope gave an enjoyable entertainment in the Wesleyan Schoolroom on Monday evening, the programme being mainly sustained by the members of the Hill Cliffe Sunday School. The Rev. George Curtis presided, and a short address was given by Mr. William Drewitt. Songs were given by the Misses Grounds, Capel, Gladys Pemberton; Hilda Taylor, Emily Williams, Sarah Gamble and Ada Ramsbottom, and Master Eric Pember- ton. Miss Starkey and Miss'E. Holt delighted the audience with a couple of pianoforte duets, and Mr. A. Mayhall contributed a violis solo. Recitations were given lay'S. Cartwright, Phyllis Moss, .N. Frazer, S. Vamblle, •C. France, J. Williams, B. Nicholls, E. Rirstage, M. Rams- bottom, E. Travis, Miss J. Moss and 'Mr. R. Pemberton. Mr. R. Pemberton contributed a couple of humorous songs which were much en- joyed. Miss Challoner, Miss Byfield and Mr. ,G. A. Whitfield were the accompanists. A , hearty vote of thanks was accorded to all who had taken part on the proposition of Mr. G. Whitfield (secretary).</p>
        <p>STOeIiTON HE A, TH.</p>
        <p>Best Outdoor Classes Yet!</p>
        <p>THE SOCIETY'S FINANCES.</p>
        <p>spring season many more members would com- pete.</p>
        <p>Of the other outdoor classes, it was perhaps suffi- cient to say that they were the best the society had yet ,had. Sevpral alterations were being made in the new Schedule, and that fact would perhapl,inducelmany others to take part. All sections/were being catered for, and the com- mittee hoped that, especially in the small gar- den- classes, they would have next year a large increase in the number of entries.</p>
        <p>The Chrysanthemum Show usually held at the close of September was abandoned this year. There seemed to be a general disposition that, under the exceptional circumstances, it had better not be hcit.l.</p>
        <p>Much Done, But More Remains.</p>
        <p>As to the general work of the society, it was felt that though much had been accomplished during the thirteen years of existence, and that comparatively great things had developed from the humble efforts of its enthusiastic founders, yet it was plain to see that there was much work to be done. There was still need for a larger membership; there were still too many gardens in prominent positions neglected; and there were still many vacant plots waiting attention. More genuinely sportsmanlike com- petition should be forthcoming, and a real love of plant life, in its many interesting aspects, should be cultivated. Until those things hap- pened, the work of the society was only partly accomplished, and called for the continued help and enthusiasm of its members.</p>
        <p>The report was adopted, on The proposition of Mr. Robert Jenkins, seconded by Mr. Ellison.</p>
        <p>The Treasurer (Mr. Jas. Holt) submitted the balance-sheet, which showed receipts on the general account totalling £221 18s. ld. Included among the items on the credit side was one of £62 155., representing subscriptions, and an- other' 'of £l3 4s. 6d., representing donations. There was a balance brought forward from the previous year of £7l Bs. 5d., and the sum of .275 9s. 7d. was being carried forward to next year. The gate money at the August shoiv totalled £47 us., and £53 is. 6d. was spent in prize money for garden produce, &amp;c., and .R 5 18s. 6d. on prizes for the sports. A donation of £lO had been made to the Stockton Heath War Fund. There was a balance in hand of £6 12s. id. on the tree planting account, and the plant account showed a balance on the right side of £ll Bs.</p>
        <p>The balance-sheet was adopted, on the pro- position of Mr. F. Timperley, secqnded by Mr. D. E. Watson, the latter remarking that in view of the period the country was passing through the balance-sheet was a splendid- one, showing, as it did, an incre4sed balance, as com- Pared with the previous year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>GARDEN SOCIETY</head>
        <p>GARDEN SOCIETY</p>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL YEAR REPORTED AT ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>Shows "As Usual," If Possible.</p>
        <p>"There are still too many gardens in prominent positions neglected, and there are still many vacant plots waiting attention."</p>
        <p>This sentence from the report of the com- mittee read to the members of the Stockton</p>
        <p>Heath Garden Society, at the 13th annual meeting, held in the Institute, Stockton Heath, on WedneSday night, drew forth a chorus of "Hear, hears," from all parts of the room. The President of the Society (Sir Gilbert Greenajl, Bart., C.V.0.) occupied the chair, and the "meeting was numerously atterided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>VERY SATISFACTORY YEAR.</head>
        <p>A VERY SATISFACTORY YEAR.</p>
        <p>With'tliriemaiic that Re tl'—ing=ttrerii—ley had heard it read they would agree that the society had had a very satisfactory year, the President called upon the Secretary (Mr. G. E. White) to give the annual report, which set forth that the year had in many respects been a prosperous one, and the enthusiasm of the members seemed in no sense to have abated. The principal feature of the year was the annual show; which was held, as on several previous occasions, in the Wilderspool Park, which had been generously placed at the disposal of the committee by Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., Ltd. For the third time the show was held on the Bank Holiday in August, and the large attendance testified to the wisdom of the selection of date. The weather outlook in the morning was very unpropitious, heavy rain falling at intervals, but later on in the day a delightful change took place, and from the time of opening to the close there was continued sunshine and delightful atmospheric conditions. Tht attendance was large, 2,447 persons Paying for admission, so that including members and helpers, over 3,000 persons must have been pre- sent during the day. But for the war cloud, which was hanging threateningly over the country, it was possible there would have been a much larger attendance, and as war was .declared on the following day, it was really the last day the shoe could conveniently have been• held.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Increased Number of Exhibits.</head>
        <p>Increased Number of Exhibits.</p>
        <p>The exhibits were much more numerous than tn previous years, a fact which was due to some extent to the better weather of the previous summer, and also, no doubt, to the revision of the schedule, the 'introduction of more classes, and the increase in the prize money. It Was gratifying to find a large increase in the com- petition in the vegetable classes, thus giving a much more :balanced show than the society had had before. A novel feature which proved entirely satisfactory was the popular vote in the table decoration class. Over 1,000 persons by ticket expressed their opinion on the re- spective tables, and so well had the tables been set out that each received a quota of votes. The winning exhibits were much admired, and it was hoped that in future more competitors would appear and make the class a really strong feature of the show. It was also interesting to note that for the first time classes were arranged for dressed poultry and eggs, and though on this occasion they were not largely patronised, they ought, when better known, to produce much competition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>Hint to Youthful Exhibitors.</head>
        <p>A Hint to Youthful Exhibitors.</p>
        <p>The juvenile exhibits were only moderate. It was hoped the children would learn to select their flowers for exhibition much more care- fully. They should be in good condition, of a proper size, and in sufficient variety ,to make the exhibit really fit for competition. It was reasonable also to expect a much larger number of entries, especially in these days when Nature study •formed such an important part of the curriculum .of the day schools.</p>
        <p>High-water Mark Reached. Taken is a whole, the show, in both arrange- ment and number and quality of exhibits, was a great success, and reached the high-water mark of the society's efforts. The spring classes were very poorly filled. It was surely possible to interest a larger circle in that section of the society's work. Window boxes and beds, small or large, of flowering bulbs ought to be much more largely grown than they were. Two or three of the exhibits were very good—really models of what was z:q.ected -aid it was hoped that in the coining</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>PRESIDENT RE-ELECTED.</head>
        <p>THE PRESIDENT RE-ELECTED.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. Collins proposed the re-election of Sir Gilbert Greenall as president, remarking that the society could not possibly find a better man for the position. Mr. McDermid seconded, and said that it was largely due to the influence and support ex- tended to the society by Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall that it was so flourishing and success- ful. (Hear, hear.) The proposition having been carried unani- mously, Sir Gilbert briefly returned thanks, re- marking that it had been a great pleasure to him to do what he could for the society. The past year had been a most satisfactory one, and although they could hardly expect tli coming one to be as good t they must do the best they could to keep the society together, and—if things were favourable and circumstances per- mitted—to hold some sort of a show to keep up the interest among the members and keep them satisfied. (Hear, hear.) The Vice-Presidents were re-elected en bloc. and Mr. L. Voisey was re-appointed hon. auditor, on thq proposition of Mr. S. Wright. Mr. W. H. Woodcock proposed, Mr. J. Ben- nett seconded, and it was carried unanimously, that Mr. James Holt be re-elected•hon. treas- urer, and be thanked for his past services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>SECRETARY AND "STIFF BATTLE"</head>
        <p>SECRETARY AND "STIFF BATTLE"</p>
        <p>AHEAD.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. H. Woodcock proposed the re-elec- tion of Mr. G. E. White as hon. secretary, re- marking that he had performed his duties in an exceedingly conscientious manner, and that a great deal of the success the society had at- tained was due to him.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. A. Jenkins seconded, and the proposi- tion was carried with applause. In returning thanks, Mr. White said that at one time he did think, owing to domestic and other circumstances, of giving up the position, but on second thoughts, and because he did not think it would be quite fair to hand over the job to another man in, these war times, he decided to carry on for another year, at any rate. (Applause.) During the coming year there could be no doubt but that the society would have to fight a stiff battle to keep its end up. With the exception of one or two members, the committee had worked exceedingly hard; indeed, he had never been so well assisted as he had been during the past year. Still, there were some members of the committee who had only made three attendances, or so, out of eleven. The society was a business proposition, pure and simple, and unless it was run on busi- ness lines they coVld not hope to, make it the success they desired. It was pleasing to note that the society was getting its name up round the district outside Stockton Heath itself. He was afraid it would be a harder matter to col- lect subscriptions during the coming year than it had been in the past, and for that reason he counselled making an early start on the job. When they came to think of it;:the society had a work in connection with the war, especially from the vegetable growing point of view. Everything was very, very dear nowadays, and it behoved those who had a little patch of land at their disposal to cultivate it, and cultivate it well. Again, they must do a bit of trench- ing; it might come in handy. (Laughter and applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>NEW COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>THE NEW COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The result of the ballot for committeemen resulted in the election of the following:— Messrs. F. Timperley and D. S. Birrell (33 votes each), W. H. Woodcock (32), W-Timperley (31), J. McDermid (29), J. Colley and W. Hankey (28 each), J. Bennett and G. Houghton (25 each), W. Howard (24) and J. Bretherton (19).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>RULE AMENDED.</head>
        <p>A RULE AMENDED.</p>
        <p>It was decided to make an addition to Rule 7 to the effect that exhibitors .making misrepre- sentations in regard to any exhibit, or obtain- ing any prize or prizes by misrepresentation, shall forfeit all prize,money and be disqualified from membership and from exhibiting.</p>
        <p>SHOWS "AS USUAL"-IF POSSIBLE!</p>
        <p>Mr. W. H. Woodcock proposed that the annual show be held on August Bank Holiday and the chrysanthemum show—which was abandoned last year on *account of the, war—on October 2. They ought, he thought, to proceed with "busi- ness as usual," in the expectation that the shadow at present overhanging them all would have passed away by the time . the summer came round. If, nearer the times named, cir- cumstances were such that it was felt that it would not he wise to have .the shows, they Could then abandon them, but he thought it was too early to decide on abandonment that night. The Secretary seconded, and the proposition was carried, after having been amended to pro- vide that the date of the chrysanthemum show should not be definitely fixed, but should be left to the committee, to fix the day later on, when, judging by the season experienced, they ~would be better able to decide whether to hold it,. as heretofore, in October, or rather on in the fall of the year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>TROPHIES FOR TRADESMEN.</head>
        <p>TROPHIES FOR TRADESMEN.</p>
        <p>The committee were empowered to discuss the advisability of offering for competition among the trade exhibitors at the annual show some- thing in the shape of a trophy or a gold medal. The meeting concluded with a rote of thanks to the President for taking the chair, it being accorded on the proposition of Mr. Roger C. Parr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>CONCERNING RATEABLE VALUES.</head>
        <p>CONCERNING RATEABLE VALUES.</p>
        <p>Small Houses Objected To On Arley</p>
        <p>Estate.</p>
        <p>A question relative to the erection of certain houses in the vicinity of London-road was dis- cussed at the monthly meeting of the Parochial Committee held on Tuesday evening.</p>
        <p>Councillor Westbrook said that it would be within the recollection of the members that some time ago plans for .the erection of certain houses behind those fronting London-road, on the easterly side, were submitted for the ap- proval of the committee. They had no power to disapprove them, as they complied with the bylaws, but many members thought that the owners of the estate had probably forgotten that the 4xisting houses on the same portion of the estate had been erected under a requirement that houses of a certain class should be built. It did not seem quite fair that houses of less value should be erected behind those of higher value, and a representation was made to the owners or the agent of the estate. A reply was received to the effect that the proposed buildings would riot be proceeded with, though no explanation was given. He (Councillor Westbrook) understood there was a rumour in the village that the same land was about to become available for the erection of houses of less value than those already erected. In the absence of the Town Planning Act, which would, have given them a say in the matter, a representation from that committee to the owners of the estate was desirable, to the effect that they had . heard with regret the pro- posal to erect such houses, and hoped they would see their way not to proceed. It was not quite fair to those who had already embarked on building that anything should be done to cause the value of their property to deteriorate. Councillor Westbrook then proposed the fol- lowing resolution, *hich was carried: "This committee regrets that a part of the unbuilt- upon portion of the Arley Estate lying south of Grappenhall-road_ and east of London-road is, or is about, to be available for the erection of houses of a value considerably below the re- strictions imposed upon existing houses, and ventures to call the attention of the owners to the fact that the erection of the class of houses proposed would work detrimentally against the owners_ of existing houses."</p>
        <p>The Chairman: It is quite a right proceeding. If we can only manage to keep this as an open space, as some of the inhabitants desire, it would be all right. Councillor Westbrook: If they go on with the houses we are directly affected in regard to the rateable value. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>SERA ‘Nrs SAD END.</head>
        <p>SERA ‘Nrs SAD END.</p>
        <p>Dr(m ned Herself In Bridgewater</p>
        <p>Canal.</p>
        <p>PAINFUL INQUEST STORY.</p>
        <p>The s.ml circumstance attending the death of a young Stgekton Heath woman were inves- tigated by Mr. Deputy-Coroner White and a jury tan inquest held in the Stockton Heath Polic Station on Wednesday morning. The naiu.;,; of the young woman was Jessie Taylor, and her body had been recovered from the Bridgewater Canal the previous day. Evidence of identification was given by John Hamblett, of 12, Dundonald-avenue, Stockton Heath. The deceased, he said, was his half- sister. She was 27 years of age and was un, married. She resided with witness and fol- lowed the occupation of a domestic servant. Her father, George Taylor, was formerly a cellarman at the Wilderspool Brewery, but was now living retired. Witness last saw Jessie alive at about 7.30 on Tuesday morning, when she was in the kitchen at his home. She had a cup of tea and then went out. Before she did -so witness spoke to her about putting too much tea out. She was very quiet in her manner; indeed, ,she had been so for two or three days before'that, and at the time he put her quietness down to a very bad cold, which had troubled her for some time. He did not learn that she was in a certain condition until after she had gone out on the Tuesday morn- ing. When she left the house he thought she was going to her employment, though it did occur to him that she was leaving somewhat earlier than usual.</p>
        <p>Annie Gaskell, wife of James Henry Gaskell, of 4, Dumlonald-avenue, who,gave her evidenee under stress of great emotion, said that the deceased was her, sister. She had not been very well for the rast two or three years, being troubled with bad headaches. During the last few months she had been specially poorly. Wit- ness knew of no special reason for that -until last Sunday evening, when she put the question to her sister. Witness mentioned a man named Hooper, and her *sister agreed. She also said that she had not told the man of her condition. Her sister and Hooper had not been keeping company, but they used to meet. On Monday evening, her sister stated, with reference to a proposal that the matter should be talked over with Hooper, that she had seen the man's mothel-, who had- told her that he had had to go to his club that night and that he would meet her at seven o'clock on the following (Tuesday) evening. William Hooper, a sharp-maker, of 6, Smith- ern-street, Stockton Heath, said he knew the deceased.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Did you know of her condition? Witness: Not until the Monday morning, when my uncle told me. When your uncle told you, did h also tell you that you had been said to be responsible for her condition?—He asked whether I would meet her at our house that night and I said "Yes."</p>
        <p>Did you meet her on the Monday night?— When I got home I thought things over and I asked my mother if she would see Jessie and ask ler to come to our house the following day. And she did not com4?—No, sir. When was the last time you had any conver- sation with her?—The last time I spoke to her was in August last, save that I Might have spoken to her just in passing. Prior to August, were you keeping company with her?—No, sir. • Were you seeing her 'pretty often?—Yes,, be- fore last July. Were you going out .with her?—No, sir. Had she ever suggested anything to you in regard to her cqndition?—No. I After you were told as to her ,condition did you say anything suggesting that you were not prepared to take your Share- of the responsi- bility?—No, sir.</p>
        <p>Harry Nicholls, a milk boy, of 17, Farrell- road, demised that at: about 7.45 on Tuesday morning he was delivering milk in Alfred-street when he heard screams coming from the direc- tion of the Red-lane Bridge, which crosses the Bridgewater Canal. He' ran to the railings and saw two arms just showing above 'the surface of the water of the canal. He ran back to his master and told him what lie had seen. When he saw the two arms the persrin to whom they belonged was apParently Joe Bradford, a milk dealer, of Ivanhoe, Whitefield-road, Stockton Heath; said he was with the previous witness when the screams were heard. He did not take much notice of them, because he had heard noises like them before and nothing had been amiss. However, his boy went to the canal bank and came rush- ing back and told him that somebody was in the water. Witness immediately took the reins off his horse and ran to the canal side. When he got there the screams had ceased and he could ply see the arms of a woman, whose head was under water. He threw the reins to her, hoping she would grasp them, but she did not do so and sank from sight. She was not struggling when witness saw her, and was, pro- bably, unconscious.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Could you have got her out of the water?</p>
        <p>Witness: Not very well, sir. Can you swim?—No, sir.</p>
        <p>Whai did she do?—Simply fell over on her back and sank. People then came running from the houses near by, and a man arrived on the scene with boat-hooks.</p>
        <p>Robert Hampson, captain of a tug-boat ply- ing on the canal, and living at 39, Albert-street, Runcorn, said that as his boat was proceeding down the canal on Tuesday morning he was asked to stop as there was a woman in the water. At that moment he saw the woman's head under the water and immediately halted his boat, at the same time telling his mate to get out the grappling hooks. The body ' was recovered at about 8.15 and artificial respiration was tried, but without success, and Dr. Howard, arriving a short time later, pronounced life extinct.</p>
        <p>Suinming up, the Coroner said the woman kept her condition to herself until practically the last moment and to have done so must of itself have involved an enormous amount of mental strain- With regard 14 thepan in the eases et WIMP. Iker bad as 'Wawa that kb</p>
        <p>was responsible for the woman's condition. It was something in his favour that, since she discovered her condition, the woman did not seem to have given him the opportunity of acting honourably towards her. Ele expressed himself as quite willing to meet the young woman at her own house, and before her own people. The unfortnnate girl had kept the matter to herself until the last moinent, and then, on being discovered, seemed to have worked herself into a state of mental frenzy, and, whilst in that state, rushed out of the house and committed the rash act of suicide. THE VERDICT.</p>
        <p>The jury returned a verdict to the effect that the deceased drowned herself "clifring a time of strain which caused' temporary insanity." They also expressed their condolences with the relatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>LYMIVI.</head>
        <p>LYMIVI.</p>
        <p>Rev. J. A. Penaliggon.—We regret to hear that the Rev. J. A. Penaliggon is again indis- posed, and trust that he will soon be able to resume his ministerial duties at the Lymm Congregational Church.</p>
        <p>Captain Hartley's Illness.—Captain Hartley, of Lymm, who is in charge of a number of those training in the South, has recently been ill, but we are glad to state that he has now recovered.</p>
        <p>On the Bench.—At the Lymm Petty Sessions held on Wednesday morning, there were pres- ent on the Bench: Mr. A. W. Callender (in the chair), Vice-Chancellor D. Stewart Smith and Melasrs. T. J. Ridgway, S. Davies and T. 11. Warburton.</p>
        <p>Local Liberal Association.—A meeting of the Lymm Liberal Association, to which all local Liberals are invited, will be held in Hirst's Pavilion at 8.15 on Thursday evening next, when representatives to the Central Council will be elected.</p>
        <p>The " Frozen " Lamp.—That the water in hip acetylene lamp was frozen was the excuse offered at Wednesday's Sessions by Cyril Lord, a labourer, of Sandy-lane, Lymm—on whose be- half his brother attended—for riding his bicycle at Heaney without a light at six p.m. on December 24th.—The lamp, it was stated, had been charged the same morning.—A fine of 55., including costs, Was imposed.</p>
        <p>"A Very Serious Offen, e."—When Robert Kemp, of The Cross, Lymm, appeared at the Sessions on Wednesday to answer a summons for having been found at such a distance from his horse and cart as not to have control over the same in Warrington-road, Broomedge, on January 2nd, he explained that he had been in the Jolly Thresher on business. The Chairman (Mr. A. W. Callender), however, described the leaving of horse and cart as a very serious offence, and said it was quite apparent that de- fendant had no excuse at all. The Bench desired him (the speaker) to give the defendant a very strong caution, and fined him ss. and costs, 13s. 6d. altogether. Horse-drivers " Cannot Be Too Careful."—At Wednestlay's Sessions Albert Edward Shaw, van- man, of 14, Hammond-road, Altrincham, was summoned for not having had control over a horse and van at Heatley on December 22nd. He pleaded guilty.—lt was stated that a police officer posted himself fifty yards away from the van as it, stood in Lymm-lane, and from half- past one to 47 minutes past Shaw, who was de- livering bread, left it unattended.—Defendant said he visited the van three times during that time, and pointed out that the horse was a very quiet one.—The Chairman, in imposing a fine of 2s. 6d. and Bs. 6d. costs, explained that such an act meant a terrible risk of accidents, and one could not be too careful. ,</p>
        <p>Why. He Swore.—The use of obscene language in Bartington Village on December 26th caused William Dale, labourer, of Sinderland-green, Dunham Massey, to be summoned at the Lymm S-ssions held on Wednesday.—Dale said he was very sorry. He had been talking with another man about recruiting, and had said he thought the single men ought to go first. He told the other man he had "done" seven years. The other man replied: "If you've done seven years it was in Portland !" It was then that. he used the language. Defendant added that he had belonged to the sth Cheshires.—The Chairman said there had been a certain amount of provo- cation, and announced that the Bench dismissed the ease.</p>
        <p>'• Was There a Wind?-1-high Randall, a labourer, of Agden. Bridge, Lymm, was sum- moned at the Petty Sessions on Wednesday morning for having ridden his cycle without a light in Warrington-road, Bollington, at a quar- ter-past twelve on the morning of December 27th. He admitted the offerice.—A constable in- formed the Bench that Randall, when ques- tioned, said the lamp had not been out long, and as there was nobody about he thought he could get home without lighting it again. He (witness) found the lamp quite cold. It was a nice night; there was no wind.—Defendant, how- ever, said there was a wind, and it had extin- guished the lamp.—He was fined 55., including costs.</p>
        <p>Congregational Parties.—The Congregational Sunday school scholars held their annual parties last week. The elder scholars met on Thursday, and tea was followed by the distribu- tion of prizes and a varied programme. Mr. J. H. Hall (the superintendent) presided. The totals both of scholars on the books and average attendances for the past year are quite satis- factory, showing a slight increase upon the pre- vious year's figures.—The infants spent Ea happy time on Friday evening, Mr. and Mrs. Willan having charge. It had been ?eared that the war might have been the cause of curtailing the usual programme of this annual joy-day for the youngsters, but, thanks to the generosity of several friends, it was carried out in its 'en- tirety quite successfully. The Christmas tree, with a 'toy and a parcel of apparel for each child, Droved—as ever—a source of great pleas- ure. There was an excellent attendance—the best for many years. Thought His Offence "A Very Little Thing." —Fred Wilson, cab proprietor, of Booths Hill, Lymm, appeared at the Sessions on Wednesday to answer a summons for having been in charge of a horse and cab at Lymm oh 16th December, and not being ,in such a position as to have con- trol over them—Defendant admitted the offence for the short time in which lie had been inside a pubric-house on the day in question.—lt was stated that a constable saw Wilson coming out of the Church Inn at ten minutes past five, an asked him why he had left his horse and cab' standing opposite so long. He replied that he had only been in the inn ten minutes.—Defend- ant said the cab did not obstruct the road at all, and it was on its right side. He added that he thought the offence a very little thing to be brought up in Court for, especially when his name had been on the books as a police assist- ant for years. He had often helped the police —even in arresting men.—The Chairman (Mr. A. W. Callender) : We are sorry to see you here, and want to caution you. The case will be dismissed, but you must pay the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>SOLDIERS' HOLIDAY.</head>
        <p>SOLDIERS' HOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>" Examiner " Interview With Sergeant</p>
        <p>H. Clare.</p>
        <p>Half an hour after midnight on Tuesday a special train dashed into Lymm Station; and, when it continued on its journey a few minutes afterwards, it left upon the platform twenty- five Lymm " Tommies" who had left C (somewhere in the South) at five o'clock in the evening. As a matter of fact, they -hid' arrived home for a five-day respite from their arduous life of military routine and discipline. This, however, only one Lymm resident knew. - The fortunate men themselves, having set out for home almost at the proverbial moment's notice, had hardly yet realised their luck. Thug, as they wended their way along the moonlit Station-road and dispersed to their 'respective homes, they chuckled to think of the surprise they had in store for their worthy parents— and others.</p>
        <p>Yesterday "The Examiner" representative travelled over to Lymm to congratulate these " Tommies " upon their good fortune, and at the same time visited the Drill Hall and had a chat with Sergt. Harold Clare (F Company, sth Battalion Cheshire 'Territorials), who served during the South African War, and who is also among them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>PLEASANT SURPRISE.</head>
        <p>A PLEASANT SURPRISE.</p>
        <p>The Sergeant explained that at first the Lymm detachment went for training to a small town on the East Coast, and had only a fortnight ago been transferred down to C , where train- ing proceeded more vigorously than ever—with what results will be seen. Whilst having din- ner on Tuesday, the men heard that they were all to have Ire days' leave, but they had long Immo mg (a !waxing ID-fintided</p>
        <p>rumours that they took no notice of it. Never- theless, the order came through at ten minutes past three for the men to be on parade in five minutes—ready for home! "And it was most amusing," added the Sergeant, "to see them all rushing about so as to be ready within the short time-limit. The thought of home, how- ever, ' bucked ' them up considerably, and they managed to do it. Besides, they are quite used to sudden orders by now, you may be sure !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>SOLITARY TELEGRAM.</head>
        <p>THE SOLITARY TELEGRAM.</p>
        <p>The soldiers departed by the five o'clock train and arrived in Lymm—as stated—seven and a half hours later. Sergeant Clare had sent a telegram to his wife as soon as he heard of the holiday,, but otherwise nobody in the vil- lage knew about it; and, of course, the journey was, a very rapid one under the circumstances. The organisation of the line for the military authorities was excellent, no time being lost anywhere.</p>
        <p>Since their arrival in the village, everybody has been telling the twenty-five visitors how "splendid" they look, and how "wonderfully improved" they all are physically. "Of course, there is nothing like Army training," they say. One Lymm resident, discussing the war with our interviewee, said: "Well, you look fine. But what are you doing? I don't see what use you are at present. It is the people 11,t Front who are doing the work for us at home !" Whereupon the sergeant closed the argument with the rejoinder: "Just put on service dress yourself, and come back with us. You'll soon find out what we are doing!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>ROUND OF VISITS.</head>
        <p>A ROUND OF VISITS.</p>
        <p>How have the men been spending their time? Well, they began by paying a visit to the Lyrnm School, at which—under Mr. R. Oddy's care—they have all been brought up. Mr. Oddy—of whom they all think very highly, as every member of the Lymin detachment has passed through the school under his headmas- tership—was delighted to see them, and has since had a talk with each. They then pro- ceeded to the Infants' Department, and were much affected when all the Fhildren rose to their feet, clapping and cheering. 'Here they conversed for a time with the headmistress, Miss Ilardwicke—who was naturally very proud to see them all, and remarked upon their smart appetwance and manner. Afterwards they could not resist paying a visit to the Girls' School, where each was as pleased as he was surprised to receive a packet of cigarettes from one of the teachers, Miss Dalton. Everybody was anxious to have a word with the gallant "lads," and their welcome was very hearty. On Wednesday afternoon the sergeant trained over to Manchester and visited the, Military Hospital, where he chatted -with the many wounded soldiers and had many exciting ex- periences in the war-zone related to him. The next day he pal 4 a similar visit to Brookfield, the Lyrirm Red cross Hospital. Last night_all the visitors wer6' to fie` entertained to supper by Mr. T. F. W. Dixon.. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>RETURN-AND THEN?</head>
        <p>THE RETURN-AND THEN?</p>
        <p>The men are, of course, now fully • trained, and do not know "whether they will not be drafted across to France upon their return to- morrow to headquarters. Certainly they are all most anxious to go, and it is quite possible that such may be the case, as they never have the least idea whither they are to be moved next until they get there! From this it will be seen how strict is the discipline to which they are subjected. On Thursday evening Sergt. Clare "drilled" the Lymm Defence Corps by request, and he declares himself struck very much by their smartness. He would hardly have believed that they could have improved, so much by their drills, and would now very much like to see them learning to use the rifle. Another batch of 25 Lymm " Tommies " will arrive home on Monday for their leave. Every- body is eagerly looking forward to the visit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>NAMELESS DOG-COLLAR.</head>
        <p>NAMELESS DOG-COLLAR.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. 11, Warburton Appears in a</p>
        <p>New Role.</p>
        <p>The Chairman of the Lymm Urban District Council appeared a new role at the Petty Sessions on Wednesday morning, for, when the last case on the list was called, he left the Bench and took his place in the defendant's stand, amid some laughter. _ _</p>
        <p>Described as Thoma-s H Warburton.. of Heat- ley, Lymm, he was summoned for allowing his dog to run about Heatley on December 13th, 1914, without any name or address upon its collar. A constable described how he caught the un- labelled animal—a brown Irish terfier—and traced its ownership to Mr. Warburton. Defendant said that he did not know the dog was loose. It was certainly his, however, and therefore lie would pay the fine. This amounted to 2s. 6d., including costs. Mr. Warburton, after paying, said he had been the first of his family to take part in public, affairs, and now he was the first to appear as a "prisoner at the bar." (Laughter.) How- ever, it certainly showed that the police were determined to do their duty without favour to anyone, and he was therefore glad to pay the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>This is Your OPPORTUNITY.</head>
        <p>This is Your OPPORTUNITY.</p>
        <p>If every reader of this paper will send a supply of the genuine Dean's Backache Kidney Pills to some friend or comrade in the training cdmps at home or with the Army or Navy abroad he will be doing a kind act and a real service.</p>
        <p>The nature of the training, unavoidable con- dition of the trenches, training grounds and camps—the very weather itself—all tend to make strong backs bad and bad backs worse, and a soldier with weak kidneys soon gets Rheumatism, Sciatica, LumbiLgo, or a bad bagk, and he must give up and berreplaced sooner or later by someone from home.</p>
        <p>When your friends return from the war don't let it be said that for want of understanding, an opportunity to help was lost—a supply of Doan's Backache Kidney Pills should be in every soldier's haversack. They are light to carry and take up but little room. SEND HIM DOAN'S—the Medicine specially prepared for the kidneys and bladder. It has no action whatever on the bowels.</p>
        <p>.. , ~.51f, .==--'----- -, ~ „,,.........,...,.. _. -- \ _.., ‘</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATURDAY. JANUARY 23, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>FIELD OF</head>
        <p>FIELD OF</p>
        <p>A DRAW IN THE MUD.</p>
        <p>Warrington Get Some of Their Own Back on Leeds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>SHOULD THE CAME HAVE kiErN.PLAYED ?</head>
        <p>SHOULD THE CAME HAVE kiErN.PLAYED ?</p>
        <p>Notes by 66 Centre."</p>
        <p>It fa a moot point as to whether Saturday's game at Wilderspool ought to have been played. On the Friday the state of the ground was quite "impossible." Big patches of water dotted the pitch; indeed, more of the turf was under water than showed above it. Those in authority cogitated as to what should be done in the circumstances, and they decided to re- quisition the services of the motor_ fire engine to pump off as much of the surface water as Possible. No doubt the engine did its best, ,but there were still plenty of big puddles left— especially in the vicinity of the Wilderspool- road end goal—when the referee's whistle sounded for the line up; whilst those portions of the ground that were not actually inundated Were in so muddy and slippery a state that any- thing like the serious football that, under more normal conditions, one would expect from teams of such calibre as Leeds and Warrington was quite out of the question. "It isn't Rugby; it's mudlarking," was the comment of one speotttor, after looking on for a few, minutes. And his description was not very wide of the mark.</p>
        <p>MORE OR LESS FARCICAL FOOTBALL. Far al football may amuse a crowd—the exposition in question did—but it is a question whether two teams with so much at stake as had Saturday's protagonists should have been. allowed to disport themselves, for the merri- ment of the throng, on a treacherous, water- logged pitch. However that 'May be, the referee put the question before the players and they said "Play," and play it was, and at least one satisfaction remains to the local en- thusiast, and that is that his own particular team were in a measure enabled to revenge themselves upon the side, who, only a few days ago, gave them the worst drubbing they have so far experienced this season, and, it is to be hoped, the worst that they will experience. That disastrous, just-after-Christmas game will he fresh in the memory. Leeds, on their own ground, put up 35 points, and Warrington failed to respond with so much as an unconverted try. It may be—though we only submit it as a more or less untenable hypothesis—that Warrington are more at home when it comes to " mudlark- ing" than are their Yorkist rivals, but after watching Saturday's rough-and-tumble,- slip- and-slide affair the thought that occurred to most of the home team supporters. ran some- thing after this wise: "How on earth did that Headingley debacle come about?" For in this, the return fixture, Warrington not only had a fair share of the attacking, but, taken by and large, seemed to be about a match for their Opponents at most points, the result—a draw of three points each—being about a fair indica- tion of the run og the play. Still, after looking at tile matter from all points of view, one is, to be On the safe side, so to speak, driven back upon the admission that the conditions under which he game was played were such that the form of neither team could be taken .as a criterion of aught save their ability to adapt themselves to abnormal conditions.</p>
        <p>some excitiiii -se'iiinimaging took place on the Leeds line. The ball was subsequently dribbled over the line, and Brookes was accredited with a try. Dainteth failed to kick the goal. Give- and-take play—Warrington doing more of the giving than the taking—marked the rest of the first half, and when the cross-over was called the teams were still on even terms, viz., three points each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>WHAT LEEDS WANT TO KNOW?</head>
        <p>WHAT LEEDS WANT TO KNOW?</p>
        <p>....... _ _ For 65 minutes only was this semi-sub-aqueous battle waged, and one must confess to being somewhat at a loss to know, if it were right and Proper for it to be allowed to last so long, why it was not equally right and proper for it to continue the full measure of minutes. The teams, apparently, did not object to being fur- ther be-muddied, and the touch-judges gave no visible indication of a desire to "chuck up the Sponge"; but the referee—as, of course, he had a right to do, exercised his prerogative and— well, when a referee asserts himself it is a cage of " their's not to reason why; their's not to make reply"; though one may reserve to him- self the right to criticise and to ask questions. Talking of questions, • one learns that, at the first available meeting of the League Commit- tee, the Leeds men intend asking why, after having to stand their full " whack " of mud- splashing in the first half—for their end of the field during that moiety was decidedly the worse of the two—they were deprived of the right to avail themselves to the full, in the second moiety, of the somewhat better conditions ob- taining at the other extremity of the pitch. One cannot help thinking that there is something of equity in their question, for, if it were right to have played the match at all it was a pity that it was not allowed to be fought to a close, for the conditions were apparently little or no worse when the final halt was called than they were when the whistle sounded for the cross- over. However, that is only our opinion, and those in charge of the game held another, and, contrary view, and, perhaps theirs was the more justifiable seeing that it is stated that the Leeds men were anxious that their journey from the broad-acred shire should not have been under- taken in vain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>JOLLEY'S WELCOME RE-APPEARANCE.</head>
        <p>JOLLEY'S WELCOME RE-APPEARANCE.</p>
        <p>Jolley- made a welcome re-appearance in the Warrington ranks, and he was quite the old Jolley—fully recovered from his injury of four or five weeks ago, and as alert and active as ever. It was good to see him back again at his post, as it was, also, to see Dainteth at his; for Dainteth is essentially a half-back, and a good one at that. Both sides fielded their 'strongest strength," and, given normal con- ditions, it would have been a battle royal. War- rington commenced operations and led off with an attack, which afforded them an opportunity to score, but it was not taken, more's the pity! Campbell sent the ball across in leisurely fashion, to Davies, and Brookes intercepted. He should have run in a try under the posts, but he let the opportunity go by, and it was long before such a good one occurred again. Lewis, with a hefty kick, sent the ball to the other end of the field, where some water-polo-cum-football amused the crowd, the spectacle of players ploughing about in the decidedly liquiferous mud making an undeniable appeal to the risible faculties. A fine dribble by the home forwards placV the Leeds line in jeopardy, and Jolley had a shot at goal, the ball going just wide. There was a surfeit of scrummaging at this stage and the men Ira Yorkshire dominated most of the " heads-down " tussles, but their backs found much difficulty in handling the ball. HOW WARRINGTON EQUALISED.</p>
        <p>A fine passing movement on the part of the visiting backs came as a welcome relief after an unexciting spell of play amid the miniature lakes. Ward cantered down the wing :in fine style, but, alas for him, his hopes of .getting. through were summarily destroyed, Bradshaw butting in at the critical moment and putting finis to the run with a great tackle. Warring- ton then tried their hand at a grand assault, but they were held at a safe distance by the visitors' defenders and presently play settled down in mid-field, and for a while was uninter- esting. At length the Leeds forwards livened matters up. They were allowed, without serious opposition, to dribble to the line, where Mirfield took the ball over and scored a soft try. Lewis just failed at goal. The home team redoubled their efforts after this reverse, but Lewis, the visiting full-back, was in great form, and, time after time, sent the attackers to the right-about Eventually, however, their dogged persistency met with rewarcf. Campbell knocked on and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S NARROW ESCAPE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S NARROW ESCAPE.</p>
        <p>The change-over was effected without an in- terval, and Leeds at once took up the running, but Warrington soon .responded through their forwards and the visitors' • line had narrow escape. By this time the ball was in such a greasy - condition that the. players Made- little or no attempt to handle it, and the game, ready-.reminiscent of the sister code, grew more like Association than 'ever. Early on in the moiety the visitors rejoiced the hearts of the home supporters by missing a fine chance of taking the lead. Bradshaw failed to play the ball near the posts and a penalty wa§ -but Lewis sent wide. Hereabouts the visitors were having the lion's sharp of the attacking, but the, defenders, aided by/the boggy turf and the pools of water, managed to keep them at a safe distance, -though, time and again, they had all their work cat out to do so. Fearnley and Mather raised the hopes of the crowd high when they broke clear and dashed three-parts of the way up the field before their little adven- ture was brought to a drastic • close and the ground they had gained was recaptured. Just afterwards Warrington had a narrow squeak. As the result of a series of mulls their line was reached. The ball was quickly returned to the centre, when Lewis put in a brilliant run. He beat player after player and was with:n an ace of scoring when Young laid him low in a parti..: cularly juicy puddle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>REFEREE STOPS PLAY.</head>
        <p>REFEREE STOPS PLAY.</p>
        <p>A kick awarded them for obstruction enabled Warrington to get going on their best effort of the second half, but their attack lacked finish and they were soon driven out of the dallger zone. In stopping a forward rush Young was injured and had to leave the field, •hut he was not abrnt more than a minute or so. Play hung about the half-way line and was practi- cally devoid of incidents-'--save funny ones, as, for instance, when one of the Leeds players having given an exhibition. of raud-pleughing with his face, stopped to wash his bespattered visage in one of the pools. Shortly afterwards the referee consulted with the linesmen as to stopping the game. It was, however, decided to allow it to proceed until sixty minutes had been played, so that it would count in the League. When the final halt was called there had been no change from the half-time score, the game, therefore, ending in a draw of three points each:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>HOW THE PLAYERS PLAYED.</head>
        <p>HOW THE PLAYERS PLAYED.</p>
        <p>By fully holding their own against the team who only a few short weeks ago gave them such a severe drubbing, Warrington may be said to have covered themselves with a measure of glory —as well as with a - coating of mud! • They were triers all the way through, and thougti they certainly allowed one or two likely open- ings to go a-begging they gave very few chances away to their opponents. The front rank, on the whole, worked with understanding, whilst Dainteth and Young were, as usual, an excel- lent pair of halves. The threequarters were as useful as they could make themselves under the terrestial conditions, and Jolley, as we have already said, was quite the old Jolley, so well and favourably known to Wilderspool habitues. Mirfteld was especially conspicuous in the Leeds front line, who, .by the way, spoilt many a promising attack by their over-eager- ness.to put on points. Campbell, of, the Leeds threequarters, and Lewis, their full-back, played reliably in their respective positions, a great run by the last-named being one of the outstanding features of the second half. "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>SALFORD CHARITY CHALLENGE SHIELD.</head>
        <p>SALFORD CHARITY CHALLENGE SHIELD.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECS. v. CADISHEAD.</p>
        <p>This match, in the first round of the Salford Charity Challenge Shield Competition, was played at Cadishead on Saturday, before a good gate. Cadishead won the toss, and, Brandwood kicking off, Horrocks returned. Holbrook, for Cadishead, broke away, but on reaching Hor- rocks .kicked the ball dead. Good play by Gallop took the ball to the other end, and Potter passed to C. Povey, who was pushed into touch .at the corner. Rylands' continued to press, but could not break through the defence, and half-time arrived without a score. Moores commenced the second half for Cad- ishead, and Rylands' at once began to press. Gallop, breaking away, fed Backhouse, who, however, was tackled on the line. Griffiths and .Potter experienced hard luck, the ball just rolling dead when they looked like bringing off a score. From a forward rush Baguley scored, the goal-kick failing. Rylands' still held the upper hand, and Potter, feeding C. Povey, that player, outpacing his opponents, scored. Potter failed at goal. Cadishead next paid a brief visit to the visitors' goal, but Horrocks drove them back. Good play by A. Horrocks and Griffiths nearly ended in a score. Shortly after- wards F. Horrocks dropped a goal. The game ended in favour of Rylands' by 1 goal 2 tries (8 points) .to nil. Gallop, Povey, F. and A. Horrocks and Grif- fiths played well for the visitors, whilst Hol- brook, Smith and Moores did well for the home team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>WARRINGTON AWAY TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AWAY TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Third ,Meeting With Salford.</p>
        <p>To-day Warrington meet Salford for the third time this season, journeying to Manchester in order to do so. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The two previous encounters resulted in wins for Warrington. The first took place on Octo- ber 3rd at Wilderspool, Salford being beaten by 5-0. The second meeting was on October 31st, in the second round of the Lancashire Cup, Warrington being victorious by 22 points to 10. Warrington's team for to-day has been selected as follows:</p>
        <p>Jolley. Bradshaw, Tranter, Brookes (eapt.), Baker Daint,eth, Young.</p>
        <p>Cox, W. Cunliffe, P. Cunliffe, Pearnley, Green,</p>
        <p>Mather.</p>
        <p>Reserves : Higham, Laws and Hai met. The Warrington "A " team are without a fixture.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION CUP.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECS. v. ST. HELENS RECS. ' At Gorsey-lane, in the fourth -qualifying round; kick-off 2.45 : Rylands' : F. Horrocks ; J. Griffiths, F. Potter, A.. HorroCks and. C. Povey ; G. Warren and J. Gallop; J. Whittaker, J Brandwood, W. Shaw, E. Smith, F. .Bagguley and J. Dumhell. Re- serve, A. Penn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <p>Motor-cycles and Cars.</p>
        <p>Accessor,ca. Repairs by competent men. Petrol and tyres. W. KNIGHT, STOCKTON HEATH GARAGE, LONDON ROAD.</p>
        <p>Telephone 63 Stockton Heath.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
