      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>St. James'</head>
        <p>St. James'</p>
        <p>Arpley-street Beamont Evelyn-street Bolton C. Silver-street Heathside Hamilton-street St. Mary's Wycliffe</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCHES. Silver-street v. St. Mary's. Heathside v. St. James'. Bolton C. v. Wycliffe.a Arpley-street v. Hamilton-street. peamont v. Evelyn-street;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>INTERMEDIATE RUGBY LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INTERMEDIATE RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Table up to January 16th. P. W. D. L. For. Ag't. Ps Parochial 0.8.8 7 1 0 143 4 15 St. James 0.8. Ileathside Frog'l 0.8. 11 7 0 4 - 110 39 14 Bolton C. 0.8. Wycliffe 0.8.7 4 1 2 96 41 9 Arpley-street 0.8. Sacred Heart 0.8. .., 8 4 0 4 63 79 8 Beamont U. 0.8.6 4 0 2 48 21 8 Silver-street 0.8. Beamont Ath. 0.8. St. Ann's 0.8.8 2 1 5 38 65 5 St. Mary's 0.8. St. Barnabas' 0.8. - 4 1 1 2 14 29 3 St. James' Jun. 0.8. 8 1 0 7 43 172 2 St. Alban's "A" 0.8. 8 0 0 8 3 333 0</p>
        <p>HEATHSI DE BEWSEY CLUB DISBANDED. At the January meeting of the Warrington and District Rugby League the St. Alban's Club were given permission to amalgamate their teams, the fixtures of the "A " team to be carried out. • Heathside Bewsey, it was reported, have dis- banded, and the players have been transferred to the Froghall team. The Heathside Club has lost 14- players, and it was found impossible to carry on two teams. The records of both teams will be expunged. No matches will be played at Wilderspool this week, owing to the state of the ground. The</p>
        <p>Parochial v. Sacred Heart match will be played at Victoria Park, the kick-off being timed .for 1.30 p.m. The Bolton v. St. Mary's match, which re- sulted in a draw, 'and concerning which a pro- test was registered, has been awarded to Bolton.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S GAMES. In Victoria Park. St. Barnabas' v. Heathside Proghall; referee, Mr. J. Beardsell. Wycliffe v. Silver-street; Mr. W. Jones. St. James' v. Beamont Athletic; Mr. Ileesom. Beamont United v. St. Mary's; Mr. C,rnzier. Sacred Heart v. Parochial; Mr. B. Willia,ms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>FAIRFIELD UNITED v. PADDINGTON.</p>
        <p>At Paddington. Paddington: Harrison; Holmes and Brain- hail; Leigh, Garnett and Taylor; E. Vickers, Taylor, Darbyshire, Wrench and Challinor. Fairfield United: Rigby; P. Challinor and Bebbington; Gleave, Hunter and Roberts; S. Woodhouse,. Short, Millward, Vernon and A. •Woodhouse.</p>
        <p>The game was keenly contested from the ' kick-off, the ball travelling very quickly from end to end and a very fast pace was kept up. Following a splendid passing movement by Hunter and Gleave, the ball was sent to Vernon, who, in turn, transferred to Millward, when the latter sent in a good shot which Harrison failed to get away. " Dobbie" Woodhouse rushed in and scored. Following this reverse the home team assumed the aggressive, and Taylor and Darbyshire were prominent. Owing to P. Chal- linor mis-kicking, S. Taylor sent in a shot which Rigby failed to get away, and Wrench breasted through and equalised. The visitors again took up the running and Gleave cleverly trans- ferred to A. Woodhouse on the left. The latter sent in a swift cross-shot, which beat Harrison. Half-time: Fairfield United, 2 goals; Padding- ton, 1 goal. After the interval the home forwa-ds im- proved and made severll keen attempts to lower the visitors' defence, Gleave being a stumbling- block to all their efforts. Smart securing the leather, transferred to Alillward, who was well- placed, and he again scored with a long drive. Following even attacks by both sideS, Wood- house and Vernon broke away in fine style and A. Woodhouse registered the fourth point. Directly afterwards Smart missed a very easy chance. of increasing the score. Resalt : Fair- field United, 4 goals; Paddington, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>ELMWOOD RESERVES v. CAIRO-STREET</head>
        <p>ELMWOOD RESERVES v. CAIRO-STREET</p>
        <p>WESLEYANS.</p>
        <p>The teams met on the ground of the former on Manchester-road. The visitors won the toss and took advantage of a strong breeze. From the kick-off they had much the best of the ex- changes, and very quickly put the home team , on the defensive, but owing to bad shouting they failed to score. The Congregationalists made. a few visits to their opponents' end, but their efforts were easily defeated and the interval arrived with no score being made. After changing ends the Congregationalists had the advantage of the wind, but the visitors adopted different tactics to suit the conditions, and after repelling several attacks by the home forwards they assumed the aggressive and gained the lead after 15 minutes' play. Follow- ing this reverse, the Congregationalists played desperately, and in a few minutes they suc- ceeded in equalising. The home forwards now seemed to• have spent their- energy, and the visitors had the best of the remaining play, and added two more goals. The home team were very well served in the closing stages by Phoenix in goal, and his backs, and were successful in repulsing many of the visitors' attacks. Re- suit: Cairo-street Wesleyans, 3 goals; Elmwood Congregationalists' Reserve, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>ELM WOOD-AVENUE v. ST. BARNABAS'.</p>
        <p>On the Avenue ground : Elmwood : A. Holt; Jos. Phoenix and W. Finch; Jos. Pritchard, W. C. Ashton (eapt.) and W. Hall; Jos. Smith, W. Phcenix, E. I(en- driek, T. Wells and A. Lamb.</p>
        <p>ELM WOOD-AVENUE RESERVE V. SANKEY</p>
        <p>ATHLETIC,</p>
        <p>At Sankey : Elmwood Reserve :.Jas. Plicenix; H. Main and R. Wallington; H. L. Clayton (capt.), James Mellor and H. Norke; T. Hunt, John Moli- neaux, N. Ellam, E. Darbyshire and W. Little- wood.</p>
        <p>ALTRINCIIAM AMATEUR CUP COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL v. ALTRINCHAM ST. MARGARET'S.</p>
        <p>At Knutsford-road, Grappenhall, in the first round of the above competition. As both teams</p>
        <p>will be at full. strength a keen struggle is anti- cipated. Kick-off 2.45 p.m. : Grappenhall : M. Ford; H. G. Sandy and B. Evans; 11. E. Hankey, Ford and F. Parr; J. Thomas, J. Ford, H. Shakeshaft, J. Peters and G. Owen. Reserves: H. Parr and F. Woodcock.</p>
        <p>Finesse.</p>
        <p>Riggs: I understand that you encourage your son to practise on the cornett</p>
        <p>Griggs: Yes, sir. He's only been playing two months, but to-day r bought the house next door to zlie for one-half or-its value.. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
