      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <p>Fairfield United Too Good for</p>
        <p>Paddington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH WIN AT ALTRINCHAM.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH WIN AT ALTRINCHAM.</p>
        <p>In Victoria Park the. return fixture between the Central and Elmwood Congregationalists was .played under favourable conditions. The Central had a ,representative eleven out, but the Congregationalists, owing to injuries, were not able to put their best team in the field. The game was fairly well contested, but at no time were theiv any of those exciting incidents %which stimulate the spectators to a high degree of enthusiasm. As the " score indicates; the Central proved themselves the better team and avenged ape defeat which they sustained in the previous meeting. The visitors' goal was sub- jected to more attacks than the home citadel,', and Holt acquitted himself fairly well, and probably would have done better had his backs been more reliable. Of the remainder of the team .there was not much tq choose in their display. Latham, in the Central goal, did ,well what Work came his way, and the backs played a fairly good game. The half-backs were the strongest line, and their forwards occasionally gave a fair exhibition of the passing movements in the game. The score of four goals against none fairly represents the .run of the game, although the Congregationalists certainly should have scored once at least.</p>
        <p>At the League meeting last week (which was adjourned owing to several important items to be decided and the absence of the Honorary Secretary on account of a family bereavement) the Grappenhall Club made application or ad- mission to the League, and were accepted, Latchford Baptists having resigned;, but it was not decided that the. Grappenhall. Club should be debited with the results of matches played by the Baptists (as. erroneously stated in a local newspaper [not The Examiner"] last week). Their .first engagement was on Satur- day, when they had Newton Congregationalists as opponents. They proved themselves superior to the visitor's and gained a splendid victory by five clear goals. The home defence was strengthened by Burns, who is a member of His Majesty' g Forces, and is at home on sick leave, but expects to re-join his regime ni this week; and it was owing to his prominent dis- play that .For in goal Was not often called upon. Their 'forwards are .a capable lot, in- cluding ,Shakeshaft and. J. Ford, formerly of the Latahford Club. "The most notable ab- sentee on the visitors' side was Porter, who otherwise were represented by the usual players. Jones and Tithers, of the half-backs, and Rowbotham and Gleave were the most prominent of the visitors' team, although Clarke deserves mention for splendid effort to gain an equalising goal just before half- time: The Grappenhall Lads are to be con- gratulated on their victory, which should give them confidence in their future engagements.</p>
        <p>A very interesting game was witnessed at Hollins Green in their return fixture with Long- ford, when both teams gave a good exhibition and the defence on both sides specially out- shone the attack, no goals being scored in the first portion. The second half -was similarly contested, and the game was approaching the close when the home 'team scored a penalty kick. -Immediately after ' the visitors had a similar chance to make the scores equal, but they failed to make the be'ett. I.lSe Of their opportunity,' and the game ended in a meri- torious victory for the home club.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath on Saturday journeyed to Altrincham to oppose the St. George's, and a very keen and interesting game ensued, which resulted in the ". Heathens " gaining two valu- able points on foreign soil. They scored once in each half of the game, and the first goal, which was got by S. Lloyd, was voted to be the best goal scored in Altrincham this season. The result of this'match should give the "Heathens" much confiderice in their Altrincham 'Amateur Cup-tie to-day, when they meet Cadishead Wes- leyans; and as these two teams have opposed each other in League warfare, and on both occasions the result has been a drawn game, to-day's cup-tie is sure to be a very keenly con- tested affair and sure to draw a good crowd to the West-avenue ground. Mr. H. Smith, of Latchford, will officiate as referee.</p>
        <p>Another match in the same competition in our district is that between Grappenhall and Altrincham St. Margaret's, and, on present form, this should result in a victory for Grap:- penhall.</p>
        <p>On Saturday a keenly-contested game was played at Paddington, when Fairfield United were the visitors. Owing to neither club bay- ing a League fixture and the Paddington Club having conceded three poihts in the League to the United, there was plenty of flavour attached to the engagement, as the Paddingtonians were anxious to try their luck again. This time their, defeat was heavier than in the League games. Gleave, of the United, the lightest player on the field, was most successful in up- setting the tactics of the home team, and was acclaimed by the spectators as the "Mighty Atom." •</p>
        <p>•" N otos by " Linesman."</p>
        <p>Dagnall; opening the home team's account. The visitors .doubled their efforts and were soon in front if the home goal, but Evans and Burns defended very stubbornly under pressure and were successful in repelling the visitors' efforts. After a brief spell of raid-fieldylay the visitors came within an -ace of equalising, a splendid effort of Clarke's being rendered useless by one of his colleagues getting into an offside posi- tion. End-to-end play followed, and no further goals were scored. Half-time arrived with the score 1-0 in favour of the home team.</p>
        <p>The visitors for a while after restarting had most of the game, but failed to penetrate the home defence. Then gaining relief the ,liome team gradually drove the visitors to their. own end and after several shots had been 'cleared by Dagnall, J. Ford scored the seconfl goal. The visitors, adopting the one-back game, kept the home- forwards at a safe distance for a while, but -Parr broke through and scored the third goal. The visitors relaxed their efforts, and the home team were continually attacking. Further goals Were scored by Shakeshaft and J. Ford, time being •called with the result : Grappenhall, 5 goals; Newton Cons., nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CENTRAL v. ELMWOOD CONGREGATIONALISTS.</p>
        <p>The above teams met in Victoria Park their return fixture.</p>
        <p>Central: Latham;. Ashcroft and Glover; Brown, Gilbert and Williams; E. Mason, Hands, Kennedy, Farrell and I% Mason.</p>
        <p>Elmwood: Holt; Pritchard and Mellor, Clay- ton, Joe Plicenix and Ellams; W. Pheenix. Smith, Kendrick, Wells and Lamb. Referee, Mr. J. Smith. The Congregationalists wen the toss and do- cided to play against the wind. The game opened very quietly, and then the Congregation' alists, by long kicking, made a raid in the home half, but were quickly driven back by Ashcroft. Play was transferred to the visitors' goal and Holt was called upon to save. In the next few minutes the visitors' goal was warmly attacked, but Holt defended well between the uprights, and then play was transferred to mid-field. For a while neither side made much progress owing to infringements of the offside rule. The Central again became aggressive and gained a couple of corner kicks, but failed to improve on them. Play was transferred to the home 1 end, but the visitors' forwards finished weakly and the home defence had not much difficulty in defending their goal. By the aid of several throws-in the home team gained ground, and during another attack on the Congregational- ists' citadel Farrell beat the opposing backs, and had not much difficulty in sending the ball past Holt. A few minutes afterwards the same player increased the lead. The visitors' for- wards now improved in their play, and &amp;Bow- ing a round of passing Wells shot over the bar. Play was in mid-field when the interval was called, the Central leading by 2 goals to none. Upon resuming, the Congregationalists, with the aid of the wind, were seen to better advan- tage, and the home defence had soon to bestir themselves, but they were successful in repel- ling the attack. The home forwards raced to the opposite end and Holt was called into 'action, and he fisted out a good shot by E. Mason. At this stage the home team were having their full share of the play, and owing to the hesitating tactics of the visitors' backs, 'Mason got through and scored the third goal. This success encouraged the home team, who continued to play the strongest game. After gaining a fruitless corner they had to fall back to defend their own goal, where Kendrick and Smith failed to 'score when only about a yard from the goal-line. After this let-off the home team again assumed the aggressive, and a penalty kick was given against Mellor. Glover took the kick and registered the fourth goal. For the remainder of the game the Cons. had the best of the play, but their forwards failed to make the best of their opportunities, and when time was called the result was: Warring- ton Central, 4 goals; Elmwood Congregation- alists, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT</p>
        <p>LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>HOLLINS GREEN v. LONGFORD UNITED. This return fixture was played on Saturday, on the ground of the former club, before a fair number of spectators. Hollins Green: G. Perrin; H. Clarke and J. Buckley; Cleworth, Mellor and Atherton; Wright, Taylor, Etchells, Billingfon and Risson.</p>
        <p>Longford: Stnrgess; A. Sherratt and Mason; E. Sherratt, Fearnley and Shelmardine; Finch, F. Bennett, Stevens, E Bennett and Hughes. Referee, Mr. B. Howell. The home captain won the toss and Stevens kicked off against a stiff breeze. The opening stages of the game were very even and. inter- esting, both teams playing good football;',. The visiting forwards were combining well, when Mellor intercepted and transferred to the home centre, who got past the visitors' backs, but the final shot was weak.: Bennett passed .out to Finch on the extreme. wing. . The latter raced along the touch-line and centred, finely, but Buckley made a timely clearance. At half- time there was no score.</p>
        <p>The visitors quickly settled down to their game in the second half and made a raid on the home goal, where the backs were defending well. The home .forwards, by goal cOmbina- tion, -got into the visitors' territory, and Etchells sent in a shot which caused trouble to Sturgess, to clear. Fearnley, Who was play- ing a splendid game at centre-half for the visitors, got badly kicked, but soon resumed. The home team were attacking keenly, but were met with a resolute defence, Sturgess making several fine saves. The home team were awarded a penalty, which Mellor converted, and shortly after the visitors also got a penalty, which Stevens badly missed. Time arrived /with the score :--Trollins Green, 1 goal; Longford, nil.</p>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL v. NEWTON CONGREGA- TIONALISTS.</p>
        <p>These teams met for the first time on the ground of the former. Grappenhall : M. Ford; Evans and Burns; Hanley, T. Ford and Peters; T. Parr, J. Ford, Shak-eshaft, IT. Parr and J. Thomae. Newton Congregationalists: Dagnall; Way- well and Lawrance; Tithers, Jones and Aitken; Dodd, Clarke, Rowbotham, Smart and Gleave. Referee, Mr. G. Bamber.</p>
        <p>After a quiet opening the home team advanced on the right and Shakeshaft receiving the centre beat the opposing backs and shot'past</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>LEAGUE'S MONTHLY MEETING.</head>
        <p>LEAGUE'S MONTHLY MEETING.</p>
        <p>" Starkey " Cup Competition Draw</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of the Warring- ton and District League was held at the Lion Hotel this week, there being a good attendance of delegates present.</p>
        <p>The chief item on the agenda was the accept- ance of entries for the " Starkey " Cup Corn- petition, and the draw for the first round re- sulted as follows :--,</p>
        <p>Hollins Green v. Warrington Central. Newton Cons. v. Earlestown Mission. St. Barnabas' v. Longford United. Fairfield United v. Grappenhall. Byes: Paddington, Orford White Star, Wal- ton, and Earlestown</p>
        <p>The matches to be played on February 6th, on the ground of the first-named club; kick-off 3 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>\OM II CHESHIRE* LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>\OM II CHESHIRE* LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM ST. GEORGE'S v. STOCKTON</p>
        <p>HEATH. On Saturday the above teams opposed each other at Altrincham. The visitors won Ala toss and from the kick-off they made tracks for the home goal, and for ten minutes kept up a continual bombardment, but the home goal- keeper and his backs defended in fine style and at length relieved the pressure. Following mid- field play, Rylance "received the ball and trans-. ferred to Noden, who in turn placed to Lloyd .on the right, when the latter succeeded in beat- ;lug three opponents and then sent in a splendid shot from twenty yards' range, which completely beat the lioine custodian, this being declared the finest goal seen scored in the League this season. The half-time score was 1-0 in favour of the visitors. The home team were the first to attack in the 4econd half, Init their efforts were resisted by the visitors' defence. The Stockton Heath for- wards gradually warmed to their work and gained ground, but missed Several fine oppor- tunities• to score by being too eager. From a splendid centre by Pearse, Vernon shot just wide of the upright. The home forwards only made occasional visits into the visitors' half, and were not dangerous, the home team being chiefly on the defensive. Vernon succeeded in notching .the second goal. After Lloyd 'had scored the visitors' first goal he was keenly watched and not allowed much scope. Smith, inside right, and the centre-half were the best for the home team. Result: Stockton Heath, 2 goals; St. George's, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
