      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>DESERTERS AND ABSENTEES.</head>
        <p>DESERTERS AND ABSENTEES.</p>
        <p>IN HIDING.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Monday, Private Arthur Howell was charged with being a deserter from tie 276th Battery R.F.A., since December 18th.—A police officer stated that he visited Howell's father's house at 24, Lilford- street, and told the father that he had reason to believe he had been harbouring his son.—The father said he had not seen him, but he ,(the officer) found prisoner hiding in the lavatory.— Howell was ordered to be handed over to an escort.</p>
        <p>DOFFED HIS UNIFORM AND DROVE A LURRY.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, Ernest Grice was charged with being a deserter from the 2nd Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment since December 2nd.—A police officer stated that he saw prisoner, who was in plain clothes, driving a horse and lurry. At first he denied being a deserter, but afterwards admitted the fact.—The Clerk: This is the second time he has been up.—The Bench ordered that Grice be handed over to an escort. OVERSTAYED HIS LEAVE.</p>
        <p>Albert Wright, of the Loyal North Lanca-hire Regiment, was charged at the Police Court on Tuesday with being an absentee since January 16th.—Detective-Sergeant Holland said that he found prisoner, who had overstayed his leave, at his house, 60, Tanner's-lane.---Prisoner: I have " pals " in Warrington: I have been out at the front and it is the first time I have been to Warrington for eight years.—He was ordered to await the arrival of an escort. WANTED TO GO PACK. .</p>
        <p>When Charles Arthur Barker, a private in the 4th Diagoon Guards, was charged at the Borough Police Court on Thursday with being an absentee, he said he had nothing to say to the Bench save that he wanted to get back to his regiment, from which he had been absent since December 12th, as soon as possible. He was remandea 'is) await the arrival of an escort.—Fredts.ck Smith, an absentee from the 4th South Lancashire Regiment 'since January 9th, told the same Court that he also wanted to rejoin his regiment. He went to the Police Station the previous day and gave himself up. —The magistrates gave their sanction to his being sent back without an escort</p>
        <p>, 47.11."</p>
        <p>*A4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 23, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 23, 1915.</p>
        <p>is bound to prove a great draw.. So much has been written in praise of this delightful piece that further eulogy here will be superfluous. Suffice it to say that the story is cleverly con-</p>
        <p>ceived, that tile dialogue is -smart and up to date, that the lyrics are sprightly—being the work of those pastmasters, Basil Hood and Adrian Ross—and that the music is of the most</p>
        <p>ear-haunting and charming description. "The Dollar Princess" is a- sister play to "The Merry Widow," and that fact, of itself, is an irresistible recommendation. The play will be presented by a carefully selected company of artistes, sent on tour by Messrs. McDonald and Young. It includes Miss Nancy Gibbs, a sweet- voiced actress, who has won high encomiums from Press and public, and Mr. Martin Iredale, a fine baritone, who plays the part of the hero- lover. Supporting these are other well-known musical comedy artistes, in the persons of Miss</p>
        <p>Maud Underwood, Miss Iris Beresford, Mr. Chas. Midgeley and Mr. Alfred W. Clark, the last-named appearing as Bulger, and giving a most successful comedy study. The company numbers upwards of fifty, and we are promised setting and costuming worthy of the play. There will be a matinee on Monday at 2.30.</p>
        <p>• • , •• • . • • . . • : .. • . ••• iN kks • ss• • • s • ''...'"'••*sx',• ' • • ' ' • • • .14 j410111,'</p>
        <p>MISS NANCY GIBBS, • As The Dollar Princess.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>POTTED PANTO. AT THE lIIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>A pantomime is an always welcome treat, and ' when it is an exceptionally brilliant produc- tion—as is Jazon and Montgomery's "Dick Whittington at the Hippodrome this week—it should on no account be missed by those who enjoy bright, breezy comedy, dainty dances, charming vocal items and, popular ditties—and who does not? "Dick Whittington" as seen twice nightly at the Hippodrome is a most cap- .tiyating production, and the exceedingly large audiences—which have been the rule at all the "houses "—are unanimous in their opinion that the two-hour panto. is of the most delightful, alluring and captivating order. Laugh! Why, qne cannot help 'but roar with merriment at the antics of the clever comedians, who collaborate in up-to-date comedy and funny burlesques. There is plenty of good singing and the numer- ous songs have been chosen with much taste, patriotic numbers being very conspicuous. For once in a way, leading politicians are spared the "gibes" of the comedians, -but, needless to say, the Kaiger gets his share in full measure. The scenery and dresses are magnificent, and the grouping of the choruses is delightfully artistic, especially that or "The Floral' Bells" and "Belles on the Island of Palms." And of the principals, who work hard in making the most of the many entertaining and diverting items entrusted to them, too much cannot be said. Miss Jennie Reeve makes a most charining and winsome Dick, her breezy style winning for her the approbation she justly deserves. She sings her songs with spirit and fee Ting, and such num- bers as "Are We Downhearted?"" Sons !if the Sea," and "We're Irish and proud of it, too," are rendered by her with much verve. Miss Gertrude Crenston is a capital Alice Fitzwar- ren, and, making the most of a very sweet voice, she scores heavily with her songs, "When Irish Eyes are Smiling" and "You gave me a wonder- ful Rose." Miss Nellie Lewis is a dashing Prince of Moroeco, and is eminently successful with the song "I'll make_you want me." Mr. Fred Ward, that irresistible mirth provoker, leads the bunch of smart comedians, appearing as Idle Jack. His drollery is delicious, and his singing of the parody on "Cock Robin" or, "Who killed the Kaiser?" causes roars of laugh- ter. He appears with Miss Selina Swift, who is the Fairy Beautiful, in a whimsical skit on rag- time, the two being most successful in this quaint and humoursome song and dance. A _ . . .</p>
        <p>dainty exhibition of toe dancing is given by Miss Swift. An excellent " dame " is found in Mr. Will B. Willby, who, as Sarah, the Cook, is very amusing. Mr. William Miller, as AlZer- man Fitzwarren, is also associated with most of ti c humorous situations. The Stevanos are clever acrobatic tumblers and balancers, and play the parts of the Captain and Mate of the Saucy Sal. The Celtics, as the Emperor and Princess of Morocco respectively, have voices that harmonise beautifully in a well-chosen number, "The Old Mill Stream." Mr. Will Cameo appears as the Cat, and his antics are exoeedingly funny. The Seaside Octet sing and dance in rag-time in a pleasing manner, and the chorus girls have been well trained in their work. Mr. E. Edgar Varley leads the chorus and augmented orchestra with much skill. Fine Bill/ for Next Week.</p>
        <p>Du Calion, "the laddie on the tottering ladder," will appear at the Hippodrome next week, in addition to the following artistes: Arthur Ferriss, in comedy character studies; Barth Trio of Belgian wire acrobats; My Fancy, in black and white studies; The Snaefells, vocalists; Penman and Miller, gymnastic mes- senger boys; Rowland, the brainstorm juggler; Lawley and East in a musical comedietta:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>STAR KINEMA'S FIRST BIRTHDAY.</head>
        <p>STAR KINEMA'S FIRST BIRTHDAY.</p>
        <p>To take the opportunity of seeing the many special films booked for the Star Kinema, large crowds have throughout this week been flock- ing to that ever-popular house. Thus it behoves those who intend visiting it this evening to leave nothing to chance but to go early! The special occasion has been the first anniversary of the opening of the Kinema, and all patrons have voted the bills of fare capital. The first included "Bluebeard the Second," a laughable pantomimic subject. "Path's Animated Gaz- ette" this time devoted its attention chiefly to the neutral countries, and was followed by the first edition of "Pathe's Animated War Map" —which, by means of moving diagrams, gives one a clearer idea of the war-theatre operations in a few minutes than can, perhaps, be gained elsewhere in as many hours. Mr. J. Black- burne, of Messrs. Crosfield's Prize Band, gave two beautiful selections upon- the cornet; and later Mr. Jack Babbitt, the popular tenor, roused the audience to a great pitch of enthu- siasm with several stirring and favourite patriotic songs. That celebrated film "Cherry- blossom, or Sacrificial Fires "—founded on "Madame Butterfly"—gave the idyllic love- story of an Englishman and a Japanese maid, the beauty of which- could not be marred even ly its doubly tragic ending. "Bunny's Swell Affair "—which had quite a novel plot—was too funny for words! In addition to the special musical and other attractions, to-night's programme includes "Called Back "—a filmed version of Hugh Con- way's great drama.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>Next Week.</head>
        <p>Next Week.</p>
        <p>The principal attraction for Monday, Tues- day and Wednesday next week will be "The Cruise of the Hell Ship," and for the remain- der of the week "The Lost Paradise."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>GOOD COMPANY IN GOOD PLAYS AT TIIE ROYAL COURT.</head>
        <p>GOOD COMPANY IN GOOD PLAYS AT TIIE ROYAL COURT.</p>
        <p>An excellent little company with a repertoire of first-rate plays is in possession of the boards at the Royal Court Theatre this week, and it is satisfactory to be able to record that goodly and highly appreciative houses have been the order. The company is headed by Miss Cecilia Dare and Mr. 11. Raynor, the former an actress of much charm and depth of feeling, and the latter an actor with a quiet, but telling, style that is quite his own. On Monday, Tuesday and Thursday nights, and at a matinee on Monday afternoon, a well- written and cleverly compressed version of Ouida's famous novel "Moths" was presented, the main theme and purpose of the book being *ell preserved in the play, despite the fact that it was got within the compass of two short hours, in order that two shows nightly might be given. "Moths" is a play that offers plenty of scope for some good acting, especially, on the part of the delineator of the character of the fresh and innocent young girl who is sold to the heartless Russian prince as the price of his silence concerning her mother's shame, and on the part of the actor entrusted with the por- trayal of the role of the somewhat embittered roue who redeems all the lapses of a not-alto- gether desirable past by sacrificing his own life in ridding the poor unwilling wife of a brutal, tyrannical and unclean-living husband.. These two big parts were in the hands of Miss Dare and Mr. Raynor respectively, and both received careful, sympathetic and convincing readings, the work of Mr. Raynor being especially im- pressive. _</p>
        <p>On Wednesday night "The Scarlet Band," a, strong detective play, which we believe, origin- ally hailed from America, and which was re- cently staged at the Comedy Theatre, London— when it met with a considerable measure of success—was put on by the Raynor combina- tion, and, with its strikingly fresh plot and telling situations, proved to be a big draw, the audience following it with the closest interest. "The Scarlet Band" will be repeated to-night. Last night patrons were enabled to see one of • the plays which established itself amongst the first favourites with the theatre-goers of some three or more generations ago; viz.; "Still Waters Run Deep." The splendid old' comedy was certainly deserving of revival and the manner in which it was received should war- rant Mr. Raynor in delving among other Mid- Victorian plays and unearthing some More of the best of them for the benefit of the 'drama lovers of to-day. A dip into the plays of the crinoline-and-bustle period will unearth much that is better than plenty of the tawdry stuff at present foisted on a long-suffering public. At the second house on Friday night a two- hour version of the• romantic French play, "'Camille," was given.</p>
        <p>"THE DOLLAR PRINCESS" NEXT WEEK. That brilliant musical play, "The. Dollar Princess," which set all London a-humming its melodies and a-laughing at its merry jests, is due at the Royal Court Theatre next week. and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S BELGIAN GUESTS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S BELGIAN GUESTS.</p>
        <p>Pleasant Evenings at St. Austin's</p>
        <p>House.</p>
        <p>Everything possible continues to be done for the Belgian refugees who, some months ago, came to Warrington as guests of the towns- people, and have been staying, since their arrival, at St. Austin's House, in Bold-street. They have settled down into a happy family; or, rather, a family that is as happy as can be expected under their sad circumstances. To help to while away the long winter even- ings a series of concerts have been held, the most recent being given on Wednesday evening. It was a thoroughly enjoyable affair • the hearti- ness of the applause which greeted the efforts of the lady and gentlemen entertainers leaving no doubt on that score, the aforesaid ladies and gentlemen being Mrs. Smallshaw, the Misses Longdin and Atkinson, and Messrs. J. Bibby and R. H. Pearson.</p>
        <p>The House CApnmittee, which consists of Mes- dames Vere Davies, Ralph Johnson and Henry Roberts, are appreciative of the kind manner in which sympathetic Warringtonians have come forward and shown in tangible form their ap- preciation of the gallantry of little -Belgium by. giving assistance to a few of those belonging to the nation which has "lost -`all save honour," but they will only be too glad to welcome offers of further help, be it in cash, in kind, or in service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>" POUND DAY.'!</head>
        <p>A " POUND DAY.'!</p>
        <p>January 29th has been set apart by the com- mittee of the local War Refugees Fund as a "pound day," and all gifts of provisions will be most gratefully received at St. Austin's House, presents of tea, cocoa, eggs, sugar, bacon and apples being particularly requested.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>FAMOUS BELGIAN ORGANIST'S VISIT.</head>
        <p>FAMOUS BELGIAN ORGANIST'S VISIT.</p>
        <p>In the Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Thursday next, an organ -recital will be given by Professor Firmin Swinnen, organist of the Church of St. Walburgiss, Antwerp, and deputy organist of Antwerp Cathedral. The soloist will be 14m. J. H. Draper, and a silver collection will be made in aid of the Belgian Refugees Fund. The chair will be taken at 7.45 p.m. by Mrs. Peter Ilylands, of Thelwall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>"GOD BLESS YOU ALL!"</head>
        <p>"GOD BLESS YOU ALL!"</p>
        <p>Refugees Thanks for ‘‘'arring ton</p>
        <p>Kindness.</p>
        <p>The following letter, written, as printed, in good English, has been reeeived by. the War Refugee Committee from a Belgian' who was stranded in Warrington and waFi given shelter at St. Austin's House until he found employ- ment and lodgings:— Warrington, January 13th, 1915. Ladies and Gentlemen,—</p>
        <p>Allow me to say a few wordslo you on the occasion of leaving your home for refugees in St. Austin's. None of you, I think, could imagine my feelings, when homeless, destitute and helpless, I quite unexpectedly heard a kind word and saw a friendly hand tended to me. I am unable to express my thankfulness for the treatment I received at your hands, because the words fail me. Therefore I must be con- tented with repeating to you the last words with which our mutual friend, Mr. Pickwick, closed his memorable speech on that memorable Christmas night's dinner at Wardle's : "Ladies and gentlemen, God bless you all !" I remain, Your faithful and grateful'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>LOUIS CRABIbt.</head>
        <p>LOUIS CRABIbt.</p>
        <p>ROMAN CATHOLICS AND WAR REFUGEES.</p>
        <p>National, Not A Denominational Responsibility.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Father Wilson has forwarded us copy of a letter he has addressed to the Trea- surer of the War Refugees' Committee : St. Mary's Warrington, January .21st, 1915. Sir,—l have pleasure in sending you a cheque for .210 as a contribution to the funds of our War Refugees' Committee. It is the amount realised by the recitation of sacred music given in St. Mary's Church for this purpose. I note that at the last meeting of our com- mittee it was suggested by one of the members that the Catholics should "run" a house of their own for refugees. However well-inten- tioned, this seems to me a suggestion peculiarly inept. The duty and responsibility of relievjng the dire distress of the Belgians who have been invited to take temporary refuge in this coun- try is a national one, not a denominational or sectional one. It has been taken in hand by a National Committee, who have enlisted local ones, such as our Borough Committee, to help in the work. As chairman of a representative committee of Catholics in the town, formed to give what assistance we can in the same good cause, I may say that we have not any inten- tion of taking any such action as that suggested. It would be about as sensible to " run " a Con- servative or a Liberal, or a Socialist house, as a Catholic or a Protestant one.</p>
        <p>_ At the same time the Catholics or the town have very willingly taken their share of the burden, and have done, and will continue to do, what they can for the cause. To my knowledge a sum of £250 has already been raised, apart from valuable contributions in kind, and indi- vidual help, pecuniary and otherwise, given directly to our Borough Committee.</p>
        <p>If more has not been-i contributed to this com- mittee it is because quite obviously its needs have not been so great nor so urgent as those of others whose appeals have reached us, and to which we felt bound to respond according to the means at our disposal. It is my intention to send a copy of this letter to the local Press in the hope that it may pre- vent or remove any misappreheusion.—l am, yours truly, NEVILLE WILSON. To Treasurer, War Refugees' Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>DISTRICT BANK.</head>
        <p>DISTRICT BANK.</p>
        <p>Britain's Strong Financial</p>
        <p>Position.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the shareholders of the Man- chester and Liverpool District Banking Com- pany, Limited, was held on Tuesday at the head office of the Bank in Spring Guldens, Man- chester, Sir Edward Donner, 7-3 art., chairman of the Bank, presiding. The report of tie direc- tors, recommending a dividen,l for tle lialf-year ended 31st December, 1914, of f. 11.3. a iare, less income tax, was adopted.</p>
        <p>Sir Edward Donner said that the usual divi- dend of .21 is. per share, now to be declared, amounted to £152,075. This was the first time that they proposed to pay the dividend leas income tax. With an income tax of is. 3d. in the .2 the payment of it by the bank was equivalent sto a bonus of about 1 per cent. This bonus they must now all forego. They pro- posed to make no appropriation to the reserve fund or the pension fund, but to carry forward the whole of the balance, £65,673, to the next account. Current, deposit, and other accounts amounted to £27,412,987. This was the largest amount they had ever had deposited with them. For eight years in succession he had been able to say that the total constituted a record. The Bank of England and the joint-stock banks of the United Kingdom held a larger amount of gold coin at June 30, 1914, than ever before in their history. It was well known in banking quarters that the &amp;Axial amount, according to the census taken by thf, ~oyal Mint, was £82,794,963, or 13 MillionF tnore than at June, 1913, and 49 millions more than at June, 1907, the first year in which the Census was taken. This census did not include the gold• bullion held by the Bank of Englana. Fortunately, therefore, the banks were in an exceptionally strong position when the war broke out. The banks now held more gold than ever before, and the gold held by the Bank of England had increased from £26,000,000 on August 5, to £69,000,000, in addition to which the Treasury held £20,500,000 for the redemption of Treasury notes.</p>
        <p>Their ba-14nce carried forward more than covered the!, depreciation on to-day's prices. Some criticism was directed in August against bankers for not having given the usual facilities to their customers. So far as the District Bank was concerned, no application was refused when they were satisfied with the position, standing. and character of the trader and the nature of the transaction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>WOMEN LIBERALS. Socials to Provide" Tommy" With</p>
        <p>Comforts.</p>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD'S PRAISE.</p>
        <p>To raise funds to provide the Women's Liberal Association Sewing Class withsmaterials from which to make comforts for our gallant soldiers, a series of ward socials have been ar- ranged, and, judging by the success which at- tended the first, that for the Town Hall and Bewsey Wards, which took place in the John Crosfield Memorial Hall on Thursday evening, they promise to be very popular. Those who attended on Thursday spent a most delightful evening. There were about 150 present, and the first part of the evening was given over to a whist drive. Keen interest was taken in the games, the prize-winners being: Ladies: 1, Miss Atherton; 2, Mrs. J. Jones. Gents: 1, Mr. Amos; 2, Mrs. Holford (playing "gentleman"). In a new feature, "Dominoes," the winners were Mrs. Gibson and Mrs. Bradbury. Light refreshments were served, and afterwards there was an entertainment, at which pleasing lime were rendered by Mrs. Sanders, Miss Wilkinson and Miss Winstanley. Dancing was also in- dulged in, Mr. L. Hales performing the duties of pianist and accompanist. Mr. A. H. Crosfield and the Mayor were amongst those present, and after presenting the prizes'to the whist winners, Mr. Crosfield briefly addressed the gathering. After offering everyone a hearty welcome- Mr. CROSPIELD said party politics were in abey- ance and they were doing all they could to further' the national cause.</p>
        <p>"You all know," he said, "the very large quan- tity of garments of various descriptions that have already been turned out, thanks to the energy and industry of our lady friends. We know very well what a perfect God-send these garments must be to our fellow-countrymen, who are facing the rigours of war in the kind of climate of which we have had a taste our- selves at home during the last few weeks. It does not need any great stretch of imagination to understand what the fellows who have to face it out in the open have to put up with. Just to think of that is sufficient to make us right glad to do what we can, and I think it is a happy idea that our charming and attrac- tive social evening here should be made to serve so good a purpose." (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>MADE THE ARRANGEMENTS.</head>
        <p>MADE THE ARRANGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>The arrangements had been carried out by the ward captains, who worked most assidu- ously. Mrs. Locker (captain for Town Hall Ward) was assisted by the following: Mesdames Pendlebury, Percival, Halliwell, Locker, Web- ster, Collin, Goulden, Clayton, Critchley, Hackney, Sanders and Nurse, and Miss Mary Wilkinson; and Messrs. Sanders and Percival. Councillor Lindley and Mr. Sanders acted as stewards for the whist drive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>URGENT APPEALS.</head>
        <p>URGENT APPEALS.</p>
        <p>Committee Hope for an After-</p>
        <p>Christmas Revival.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Edelsten reports:— The Christmas season has naturally caused a diminution in the number of garments sent to the Town Hall, but the committee trust that this is only a temporary falling .off, and that further supplies of warm clothing and money will be forthcoming from the generous people of Warrington, to enable them to respond to the urgent appeals from hospitals both at home and abroad.</p>
        <p>Parcels have been sent this month to the Motor Transport Department, to the Ist General Hospital at the Front, to Fazackerley, to Mrs. Ireland-Blaekburne, and to the Warrington Infirmary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>AT THE ChOSFIELD HALL.</head>
        <p>AT THE ChOSFIELD HALL.</p>
        <p>The valuable work in connection with the Grand Duke Michael's Gloves and Mitten Scheme and the Women's Liberal Sewing Class has continued steadily this week, and further reports will be given later. •</p>
        <p>Either Would Do.</p>
        <p>Pompous Lady: "Must I stick this stamp on myself?"</p>
        <p>Post Office Clerk: "Well, you can if you like, but it's intended to be stuck on the letter."— " Dietetic and Hygienic Gazette." . •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <p>I</p>
        <p>1 46.1..-0. • ^ 1 e •• ' • \ , . ,-,- -- -- - - cr. Jo, • ) •_./ •</p>
        <p>OU SU</p>
        <p>'.l</p>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>Independent Methodist Choir Social—The annual choir social was held in the Assembly Hall of the Independent Methodist Church on Thursday evening, thanks to the generosity of Councillor R. Heushall and the members of the congregation. During the evening refreshments were served and games were. indulged in. At the close Councillor Henshall was thanked for his generosity and for the trouble he had taken in arranging the social.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Working Men's Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>N.A. CENTRE.</head>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>The contrast between -last week's report of Mr. Wallis's takes on the beautiful Hampshire Avon and our local rods could not be greater. One of our members, who visits one of our finest specimen fisheries—LDangollen—did, not have a stir all thrmigh the day.- From this place, last winter, members who are attached to .the Northern Anglers, could weigh in roach up to 11b. 120 z., and dace u to 13oi. Results up to the present have been nil. One of the 'shining lights" of the N.A., during the month of September last, was ready to wager he would beat roach lib. oz. and, 11b. Boz. re- spectively, during the winter months from this famous spot, but we have .not heard of him accomplishing the task.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>WEAVER.</head>
        <p>THE WEAVER.</p>
        <p>The rules of the Northwich and District Joint Anglers' Association are now in the Secretary's hand, and from what we can see of them there should be very little fault found in them by those who affiliate. There will be very little trougle in getting terms with the association, as they seem to meet with the approval of our locals generally.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>NOTICE.</head>
        <p>NOTICE.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting at headquarters on Thursday next, January 28th, will commence at 7.30 p.m. prompt. Members must put in an appearance as early as possible, as there is much business to be gone through. , A. 'S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>‘VAIIIIINGTON ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>‘VAIIIIINGTON ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The War Has a Bad Influence on tlw Funds.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>The 24th annual report of the Warrington Anglers' Association, with which Mr. Peter Knight, the secretary, has kindly furnished us, shows that although the war has had some regrettable results upon the activities of the association, the future is most hopeful. The number of cards, Says the report, issued was less than in 1913, and 128 senior and junior</p>
        <p>members are in arrear, owing £43 Os. 2d., against £l5 14s. in 1913. Many members have responded to the call to arms and others have suffered from lack of employment, but "there are very many who are in fairly good circum- stances who might have responded to the ap- peal recently issued; it should be remembered that we cannot provide all that is necessary unless our members realise the position their neglect has placed us in."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>DEARTH OF SPORT.</head>
        <p>DEARTH OF SPORT.</p>
        <p>There has been little improvement in sport during the year, but the quality of the fish caught has been well maintained. The River Weaver was practically closed against Warring- ton by prohibitive terms demanded by a new governing authority, and this emphasised the dearth of sport; but at a conference recently held, at which Warrington and Runcorn were repr6sented, such reasonable terms were arranged that no serious obstacle will be pre- sented to members of associations who wish to use the fishery in the future. The Dee Fishery raised complex points for the future.</p>
        <p>The need for an observance of a close season upon all waters becomes more pressing year by year. The class of fish so plentiful in the past are now conspicuous by their absence, owing in great measure to want of proper protection during the fence months and the indiscriminate slaughter of immature fish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>GOWY'S DEPARTED GLORY.</head>
        <p>GOWY'S DEPARTED GLORY.</p>
        <p>It is deplorable to find that the glory of the Gowy has departed. This fishery, famous for many years for its gind specimens and fine catches, has, like many others, sunk to mediocrity. Only two members weighed in four fish during the past season. The Bridgewater Canal, despite the immense stocking during the past ten years, is much on a par with other local waters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>SPECIMEN LIST.</head>
        <p>SPECIMEN LIST.</p>
        <p>Our specimen list reveals the fact that only 88 fish have been recorded from all waters, against 110 last year-48 from association waters and 40 from foreign waters. Their aggregate weight was 1311 b. 15oz. 2dr., and their average weight 11b. 7oz. 15dr. Of the 38 prizes, valued at .29, nineteen have been won with fish from home waters, and nineteen from foreign waters.</p>
        <p>The totals of each species were:- 25 roach, averaging 15oz. 121dr.; 12 dace, lloz. 4dr.; 5 perch, 15oz. 13dr.; bream, nil; 15 trout, 11b. 14oz. 10dr.; 14 chub, 21b. 6oz. 6dr.; 9 gud- geon, loz. 814 r.; 3 pike, 81b. soz. 9dr.; 4 gray- ling, 11b. loz. 15dr.; 1 carp, lloz. 12dr. Contests were curtailed considerably owing to the dislocation of traffic on the outbreak of war, the association, tradesmen's and officers' contests being abandoned. Both the Juvenile and Shield Competitions suffered numerically and financially. The winners of the shield were Mr. J. Newton's team with the weight of 11b. 12oz. lldr., the winners of the cup being Mr. G. Smith's team with 11b. 51oz. There was a total catch of 221 b. 210 z., the total value of the medals and prizes being .t2O 19s. 6d. • The value of the prizes and medals in con- tests and for specimen fish was £32 12s. 6d., against £5B 12s. 6d. in 1913.</p>
        <p>Stocking operations were Practically nil. In the first place there was a disappointment in a source of supply. In the second pltice a misfor- tune Overtook the fine stock of healthy young Fario purchased from the Solway Fishery; the whole of them were destroyed in the rearing pond in a single night.</p>
        <p>Five members of the committee retired by rotation, and as there were no nominations put forward up to November 30th, as per rule, the committee re-elected Messrs. E. Hart, G. Smith and S. Jenks, and elected Messrs. T. Mason and W. Leigh to fill the vacancies caused by the retirement of Messrs. N. Cook and T. Nixon. The new members' and travelling cards are now ready for distribution.</p>
        <p>The committee, with a desire to preserve the sport and the interests of all anglers, confidently rely upon the continued support of members. The balance in hand is ..el6 18s. BN., against .228 Bs. 11d, last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>TICKETLESS TRAVELLERS. _ Railway Company Prosecute Two</head>
        <p>TICKETLESS TRAVELLERS. _ Railway Company Prosecute Two</p>
        <p>VVothen.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Tuesday, Alice Fleet, formerly of Reddish Cot- tages, Lymm, and now of South View Cottages, off Sandy-lane, Oughtrington, and Mary Stand- bank, of Reddish Cottages, Lymm, were sum- moned for having travelled on the L. and N.-W. Railway without having paid their fares, with intent to defraud the company. For the company, Mr. A. Browne stated that on December 29 Detective Hewitt, employed by the company, saw the women get on the train at Heatley. At Arpley Station, Warrington, Mrs. Standbank handed the ticket collector threepence and said, "Two from Latchford." The collector asked if they were sure they had only come from Latchford, and he received a reply in the affirmative. Detective Hewitt saw the women returning to Heatley from Bank Quay Station, and when he questioned them. Fleet said,. "I had not any more money until I got some in town." Standbank said, "I am willing to pay the fare, but I was waiting for Mrs. Fleet to get bliange." Mr. Browne added: " This sort of thing has been going on between eatley; Lymni and 'Warrington for 35 years, to my personal knowledge; and an example. ought to be made of these people. Neither of the defendants appeared, but Mrs. Fleet had written letter, in IPhich she stated that 3he intended to 'pay when she got her money in Warrington. She had two children, one of whom was very-ill, and her husband was on active service. She hoped the Bench would take a lenient view of the ease. Defendants were each sued 108, and cats. ;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>ORFORD.</head>
        <p>ORFORD.</p>
        <p>Capture of a Swan.-4n event, quite unique in the village of Orford, occurred on Sunday morning, when Mr. W. Dumbell effected the capture of a large white swan, in the field at the back of his house in Hall Fields-road. Mr. Dumbell was outside at about eleven o'clock, and saw this largo bird conie flying from the direction of Winwick and settle on the field, which was flooded by the recent heavy rains. By dint of- much persuasion, and with the aid of a quantity of bread, he managed to coax the bird to within reach, and promptly secured it. He reported his capture to the police, and it has now been handed back to its owners—the Corporation of Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>MOORE-.</head>
        <p>MOORE-.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild Entertainment. —The members of the Wesley • • Guild were entertained on Wednesday evening by a party of friends from the Warrington Wesleyan Cycle Club, under the leadership of Mr. C. Wilcockson. The artistes included Mrs. Plinston, Miss L. Kershaw, Miss M. Jones, Miss V. Jones, Mr. Lawton, Mr. G. Whitfield, Mr. C. Wilcockson and Mr. Meter. The ladies acquitted themselves well in songs; Mr. Lawton showed the audience what could be done with a one-stringed violin; Mr. Whitfield recited; Mr. Wilcockson gave readings from Mark Twain, thus providing the humorous ele- ment; and Mr. Turner sang two songs. Mrs. Wilcockson presided over the entertainment. A very hearty vote of thanks to the visitors was proposed by Mr. Meldrum, and seconded by Miss Dutton, Mr. Wilcockson responding for the Cycle Club. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>PADG-ATE.</head>
        <p>PADG-ATE.</p>
        <p>Visit of the Rev. J. E. Rattenbury.—Under the auspices of the Padgate Wesley Guild. the Rev. J. E. Rattenbury, the well-known Wesleyan orator and writer, will pay a visit to the Til- lage on Thursday next, and will lecture, at 7.30 in the evening, on "The War and the Church?' He will also preach at four o'clock in the afternoon, and after the sermon tea will be served in the Guild Room.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild Debate Total Abstinence.—A very interesting discussion took place at Mon- day's meeting of the Wesley Guild, the subject being "Is total abstinence necessary to a Christian?" Councillor J. Shaw, speaking in the affirmative, said that as Christians they had a standard to keep up. They ought te- consider the influence they had upon others,. and set them a good example. He also en- larged upon the physical, moral, social and spiritual sides of the question.—Mr. J. Hamb- lett championed the negative, the bulk of his argument hinging on the fact that doctors in many cases advised the use of alcohol. It was very evident that the feeling of the meeting was strongly in favour of Councillor Shaw. Mrs. E. Andrews and Miss L. Sykes both made! spirited speeches in support of the affirmative,. and Mr. E. England supported the negative„ Mr. IV. E. Plinston presided. Funeral of the Late Mrs. Ward.—The funerak took place on Saturday of Mrs. Harriet Ward,.. of Padgate, who passed away at her reeideneet on the previous Tuesday, in her 69th year. Mrs. Ward enjoyed the esteem of a wide circle of friends and neighbours, and there were many tokens of affection on the occasion of the funeral. The coffin was carried to the Padgett, Wesleyan Chapel by friends who had borne the body of her late husband eight years ago. The service in the chapel was conducted by the • Rev. A. J. Morton, who gave a brief address, and the hymns "Come, let us join our friends above,' "For all Thy saints," and "Give me the wings of faith" were sung. Miss H. Ben- nett, the organist, also played the Dead March in "Saul," and "0 rest in the Lord." Among the mourners were Mr. A. Ward (son), Mrs. Dakin, Mrs. Cutland, Mrs. Bate and Miss Ward (datighters), • Mrs. A. Ward (daughter-in-law), Mr. Dakin, Mr. Cutland and Mr. Bate (sons-in- law), and a number of old friends. The mem- bers of the Wesleyan Sisterhood walked before the hearse as it proceeded to the Cemetery. By the express wish of the deceased lady the only floral tribute provided was that sent by her own children, and it took the form of a large floral anchor, placed :upon. 4 laurel wreath. The Rev. A. J. Morton read the committal service at the graveside, at which a large number of mourners assembled. Messrs. J. Dlonnfield and Sons carried out the funeral arrangements.</p>
